(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e2b.84f08c594a.js"]={"36":[{"id":"sdl36_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Chronic Mountain Sickness (CMS) — Pathogenesis, Mechanisms, and Recovery","batch":1,"stem":"A 54-year-old man born and raised at 4,200 meters in the Andes presents with progressive dyspnea on exertion and a ruddy complexion. Laboratory studies reveal hemoglobin of 23 g/dL, hematocrit of 68%, and arterial PO2 of 45 mmHg. Echocardiography shows right ventricular hypertrophy with elevated pulmonary artery pressure. His physician explains that the initial response to chronic altitude hypoxia — increased red blood cell production — eventually becomes maladaptive. Which of the following BEST describes the mechanism by which the marked erythrocytosis in this patient directly impairs tissue oxygen delivery?","choices":{"A":"Elevated hemoglobin causes a leftward shift of the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve, increasing oxygen-hemoglobin affinity and preventing adequate oxygen unloading at the tissue level despite high O2 content","B":"Increased blood viscosity from elevated hematocrit reduces flow through capillary beds (particularly at low shear rates), decreasing tissue oxygen delivery despite a normal or elevated arterial oxygen content","C":"Polycythemia stimulates platelet aggregation, causing widespread microvascular thrombosis in systemic capillary beds that physically blocks red blood cell transit through tissues","D":"The elevated hematocrit increases cardiac afterload, reducing left ventricular stroke volume and systemic oxygen delivery through a fall in cardiac output","E":"Marked erythrocytosis activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, causing sodium retention and plasma volume contraction that offsets the increased RBC mass and net reduces O2-carrying capacity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chronic mountain sickness (CMS; Monge's disease) develops when erythropoiesis becomes excessive — a maladaptive overshoot of an initially appropriate response to hypoxia. Three interconnected mechanisms impair oxygen delivery: (1) Hyperviscosity — an exceptionally high hematocrit (Guyton Ch 44) substantially increases blood viscosity, which reduces capillary perfusion, decreasing tissue O2 delivery despite elevated O2 content per deciliter; (2) Global hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction (HPV) — because all alveoli are chronically hypoxic, HPV is diffuse and sustained, elevating pulmonary artery pressure and leading to right ventricular failure (cor pulmonale); (3) Pulmonary arteriovenous shunts — alveolar arteriolar spasm creates bypass channels that allow desaturated blood to bypass gas exchange entirely. The question specifically asks about the mechanism by which erythrocytosis DIRECTLY impairs delivery — that is hyperviscosity. A leftward ODC shift would actually improve loading, not decrease delivery. Platelet aggregation is not the described mechanism. LV afterload is not the primary issue in CMS — RV overload is.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CMS = maladaptive polycythemia at altitude. Exceptionally high Hct → hyperviscosity → ↓capillary flow → ↓O2 delivery despite ↑Hb. Also: global HPV → sustained ↑PAP → cor pulmonale (RV failure). Treatment: phlebotomy + descent. First Aid p.688: chronic HPV → pulmonary hypertension → RVH. COMLEX: CP 9.3 SOB — altitude resident with polycythemia + right heart failure = CMS (Monge's disease).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 1 — HIF-driven erythropoietin production behind the polycythemia","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A leftward ODC shift would act","C":"Platelet aggregation is not th","D":"LV afterload is not the primar"}},{"id":"sdl36_q02","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Chronic Mountain Sickness (CMS) — Pathogenesis, Mechanisms, and Recovery","batch":1,"stem":"A 49-year-old man who has lived at 3,800 meters his entire life presents with lower extremity edema, elevated jugular venous pressure, and hepatomegaly. Chest X-ray shows right heart enlargement and prominent central pulmonary arteries. Hemoglobin is 22 g/dL. Echocardiography confirms severe tricuspid regurgitation and right ventricular dilation. He is diagnosed with chronic mountain sickness. Which of the following BEST describes the pulmonary vascular mechanism producing right heart failure in this patient and how it differs from the pathophysiology of high-altitude pulmonary edema (HAPE)?","choices":{"A":"In CMS, high-altitude polycythemia clogs the pulmonary microcirculation through hyperviscosity-mediated thrombosis of small pulmonary arterioles; in HAPE, the mechanism is completely non-vascular, arising from direct hypoxic injury to type II pneumocytes and surfactant depletion","B":"In CMS, left ventricular diastolic dysfunction from elevated blood viscosity backs up pressure into the pulmonary veins, mimicking cardiogenic pulmonary edema; in HAPE, hypoxia directly damages the capillary endothelium via reactive oxygen species, producing a chemically-induced capillaritis","C":"In CMS, hypoxia-induced sympathetic activation constricts pulmonary veins (post-capillary), not arterioles, increasing left atrial filling pressure and producing pulmonary venous hypertension; in HAPE, pre-capillary arteriolar constriction is the primary event","D":"Both CMS and HAPE share identical mechanisms of uniform hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction, but CMS produces left-sided failure while HAPE produces right-sided failure due to differential timing of alveolar versus vascular involvement","E":"In CMS, chronic GLOBAL hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction keeps pulmonary artery pressure high for years and overloads the right ventricle; in HAPE, HPV is UNEQUAL (patchy), causing regional capillary hypertension and acute non-cardiogenic edema"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The global vs. patchy HPV distinction is the conceptual pivot for understanding CMS vs. HAPE. In CMS: all alveoli are chronically hypoxic (the patient breathes low-PO2 air continuously). This produces GLOBAL HPV — diffuse, symmetric arteriolar constriction throughout the entire pulmonary vascular bed. Over months to years, pulmonary artery pressure is sustainedly elevated → RV hypertrophies → eventually fails → cor pulmonale. In HAPE: HPV is spatially non-uniform (some arterioles constrict more than others). Blood is diverted from highly constricted territories into less-constricted zones at very high pressure → focal capillary hydrostatic pressure exceeds filtration safety threshold → non-cardiogenic edema in those zones. HAPE is an ACUTE process; RV is not the primary locus of failure. CMS cor pulmonale is a CHRONIC progressive process driven by years of sustained global HPV.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CMS = chronic GLOBAL HPV → sustained ↑PAP → RV failure (cor pulmonale). HAPE = acute UNEQUAL HPV → focal ↑capillary pressure → non-cardiogenic edema. Board pearl: altitude pulmonary hypertension → RV disease (not LV). COMLEX: HAPE vs. CMS is a high-yield distinction — one is acute (HAPE, hours) and one is chronic (CMS, years).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — pulmonary hypertension due to hypoxia (WHO group 3)","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q03","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Chronic Mountain Sickness (CMS) — Pathogenesis, Mechanisms, and Recovery","batch":1,"stem":"A 57-year-old Peruvian miner with chronic mountain sickness (hemoglobin = 22.5 g/dL, hematocrit = 68%, pulmonary artery systolic pressure = 58 mmHg) is transferred to a sea-level hospital. His physician counsels him about expected recovery. Which of the following BEST characterizes the timeline and mechanism of hematologic and symptomatic improvement after descent to sea level?","choices":{"A":"Improvement occurs over days to weeks: normoxia lowers EPO within days as HIF-1α is again degraded, normal clearance of excess RBCs over weeks lowers hematocrit and viscosity, and pulmonary artery pressure falls as HPV resolves","B":"Recovery takes months to years because the bone marrow undergoes permanent hyperplastic changes in CMS that cannot be reversed by altitude change alone; serial phlebotomy every two weeks is required to normalize hematocrit regardless of descent","C":"Recovery requires pharmacologic suppression of HIF-1α with prolyl hydroxylase activators administered intravenously because sea-level normoxia alone is insufficient to overcome the constitutive HIF-1 activation established over years at altitude","D":"Recovery is immediate and occurs within hours, as supplemental oxygen rapidly reverses erythropoietin secretion and simultaneously stimulates accelerated reticuloendothelial destruction of all excess RBCs through a direct oxygen-sensing pathway in the spleen","E":"Permanent improvement is not possible once right ventricular hypertrophy has developed because cardiomyocyte hypertrophy is irreversible; heart transplantation is the only definitive treatment for cor pulmonale complicating CMS"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Recovery from CMS with descent is gradual: (1) Within days: PO2 normalizes → PHD enzymes reactivate → HIF-1α is hydroxylated → VHL degrades it → EPO secretion falls dramatically within 1–2 days. (2) Over weeks: with no new EPO stimulus, bone marrow erythroid output returns to baseline. Excess RBCs undergo normal reticuloendothelial clearance (average RBC lifespan about 120 days, First Aid 2025, so the hematocrit falls gradually as old cells are cleared and few new ones are added). During this time, viscosity falls, capillary flow improves, and pulmonary artery pressure begins to normalize as HPV resolves in sea-level normoxia. RV remodeling can improve (the RV is more plastic than the LV). The bone marrow does NOT undergo permanent hyperplasia — the erythroid expansion is entirely EPO-driven and fully reversible.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CMS recovery: EPO ↓ in days (as PHD/VHL reactivate → HIF-1α degraded) → Hct normalizes in weeks (RBC clearance). RV pressure overload also reverses as HPV resolves. No permanent marrow changes. COMLEX: Descent = primary treatment across altitude illness spectrum. CMS = weeks for hematologic recovery; HAPE = hours for O2/descent; AMS = days.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 1 — HIF-1α degradation once oxygen is restored","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"RV remodeling can improve (the"}},{"id":"sdl36_q04","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — High-Altitude Cerebral Edema (HACE) — Mechanisms and Clinical Presentation","batch":1,"stem":"A 26-year-old medical student travels from sea level to a ski resort at 2,500 meters (8,200 feet). Approximately 14 hours after arrival, she develops a severe frontal headache, nausea, and fatigue. She has no focal neurologic deficits, no ataxia, and no respiratory symptoms. Pulse oximetry shows SpO2 of 88%. Which of the following BEST characterizes her condition and the altitude at which it characteristically first manifests?","choices":{"A":"High-altitude pulmonary edema (HAPE); this condition first manifests at similar altitudes but is distinguished by bibasilar crackles, pink frothy sputum, and worsening dyspnea as the primary presenting complaints","B":"High-altitude cerebral edema (HACE); this condition requires ascent to altitudes exceeding 15,000 feet and is distinguished from AMS by the additional requirement for meningismus and fever at presentation","C":"Acute mountain sickness (AMS); this condition characteristically develops above 8,000–9,000 feet (approximately 2,400–2,700 m) after rapid ascent, with symptom onset typically within hours to 2 days of arrival at altitude","D":"Chronic mountain sickness (CMS); this condition develops in any individual who ascends rapidly to altitude and is characterized by polycythemia detectable within the first 24–48 hours of altitude exposure","E":"Hypoxic hypocapnic syncope; this altitude syndrome occurs exclusively above 18,000 feet when alveolar PO2 falls below 35 mmHg and is characterized primarily by loss of consciousness rather than headache or nausea"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This patient has classic acute mountain sickness (AMS). Guyton specifically identifies the altitude threshold for AMS onset as greater than 8,000–9,000 feet (~2,400–2,700 m). Pathophysiology: rapid ascent → ↓arterial PO2 → hypoxia → cerebral arteriolar vasodilation → ↑cerebral blood flow → ↑intracranial pressure → stretching of pain-sensitive meningeal and intracranial structures → pounding headache. Associated symptoms (nausea, fatigue, dizziness, sleep disturbance) reflect diffuse CNS hypoxia. Onset: hours to 2 days after arriving at altitude. Guyton Ch 44 gives the same onset window (a few hours up to about 2 days after ascent). HACE is a more severe form requiring frank cerebral edema (ataxia + disorientation + AMS not responding to treatment) — it occurs at higher altitudes or in those who ignore initial AMS. HAPE is a pulmonary process with dyspnea and crackles predominating. CMS requires years of residence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AMS threshold = >8,000–9,000 ft (2,400–2,700 m) after rapid ascent. Onset: hours to 2 days. Triad: headache + fatigue + nausea/vomiting ± sleep disturbance. Mechanism: hypoxia → cerebral vasodilation → ↑ICP → headache. Treatment: no further ascent, acetazolamide, descent if no improvement. COMLEX: CP 9.3 SOB/fatigue at altitude = AMS. Acetazolamide (SDL 38 Obj 2) = treatment by forcing renal HCO3− excretion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 2 — carbonic anhydrase inhibitors in acute mountain sickness","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"HAPE is a pulmonary process wi","D":"CMS requires years of residenc"}},{"id":"sdl36_q05","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — High-Altitude Cerebral Edema (HACE) — Mechanisms and Clinical Presentation","batch":1,"stem":"A 40-year-old male mountaineer at 5,200 meters develops worsening confusion and stumbling gait. On examination, he cannot perform tandem walking and is disoriented to time and place. MRI of the brain shows T2 signal abnormalities in the corpus callosum consistent with vasogenic edema. Which of the following sequences MOST accurately describes the PRIMARY initiating mechanism leading to cerebral edema in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Hypoxia → cerebral arteriolar vasodilation → increased cerebral capillary hydrostatic pressure → transcapillary fluid filtration exceeds reabsorption capacity → vasogenic cerebral edema accumulates in the brain interstitium","B":"Hypercapnia from impaired ventilation at altitude → CO2-driven cerebrovascular reactivity → ↑cerebral blood flow → ↑capillary pressure → edema; the primary driver is elevated CO2, not reduced O2","C":"Acute mountain sickness → systemic complement activation → direct endothelial cytotoxicity → complement-mediated lysis of blood-brain barrier tight junction proteins → protein-rich fluid extravasation","D":"Hypoxia → sympathetic activation → α1-adrenergic constriction of cerebral arterioles → paradoxical downstream microarteriolar dilation → microhemorrhages with perivascular edema in cerebral white matter","E":"Hypoxia → global cerebral vasoconstriction due to sympathetic activation → downstream ischemia → disruption of Na+/K+-ATPase → cytotoxic edema from osmotic water movement into swollen neurons"},"correct":"A","explanation":"HACE pathophysiology per Guyton Chapter 44: hypoxia causes cerebral ARTERIOLAR VASODILATION (the cerebrovascular response to hypoxia is dilation — the opposite of the pulmonary vascular response). This vasodilation increases cerebral capillary hydrostatic pressure, causing fluid to filter into the brain interstitium — VASOGENIC edema. A second amplifying mechanism: hypoxia → HIF-1 activation → VEGF + inflammatory cytokines (IL-6, TNF-α) → increased endothelial permeability of the blood-brain barrier → further amplifies the vasogenic process. The MRI T2 signal in corpus callosum and white matter reflects vasogenic (extracellular) edema — treated by dexamethasone. Importantly: (1) The cerebrovascular response to hypoxia is DILATION (not constriction), which rules out a vasoconstriction-ischemia sequence. (2) Ventilation at altitude is increased (↓PaCO2, not ↑CO2). (3) Cytotoxic edema (from ischemia/Na+/K+ pump failure) is intracellular — not the primary HACE mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HACE = vasogenic cerebral edema from: (1) hypoxia → cerebral VASODILATION → ↑cap pressure; (2) VEGF + cytokines → ↑BBB permeability. T2 MRI changes = white matter vasogenic edema. Treatment: immediate descent + dexamethasone (reduces vasogenic edema). Guyton Ch 44: hypoxia → local cerebral vasodilation → ↑cerebral blood flow. COMLEX: HACE clinical triad: severe headache + ataxia + altered mental status at altitude.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — the acute effects of hypoxia on the brain and other organ systems","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"(2) Ventilation at altitude is","D":"Importantly: (1) The cerebrova","E":"Importantly: (1) The cerebrova"}},{"id":"sdl36_q06","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — High-Altitude Cerebral Edema (HACE) — Mechanisms and Clinical Presentation","batch":1,"stem":"Researchers performing lumbar punctures on mountaineers with high-altitude cerebral edema at base camp (5,400 m) find markedly elevated VEGF, interleukin-6, and tumor necrosis factor-alpha in CSF compared to altitude-exposed controls without HACE. Which of the following BEST describes the mechanism by which these mediators amplify cerebral edema in HACE beyond the initial hemodynamic insult?","choices":{"A":"VEGF stimulates rapid angiogenesis within the brain parenchyma by inducing endothelial proliferation; these immature new capillaries lack tight junctions and serve as the primary source of the vasogenic edema seen in HACE","B":"TNF-alpha directly depolarizes neuronal voltage-gated sodium channels, causing massive cortical spreading depression → ↑metabolic demand → focal tissue hypoxia → secondary osmotic edema from glycolytic byproduct accumulation in astrocytes","C":"Interleukin-6 activates cerebral mast cells to release histamine and bradykinin, which produce post-capillary venule dilation raising cerebral venous pressure, thereby increasing capillary hydrostatic pressure independently of any arteriolar vasodilation","D":"VEGF and inflammatory cytokines disrupt blood-brain barrier tight junctions (claudin-5, occludin, ZO-1), letting protein-rich fluid leak out and amplifying the edema begun by hypoxic arteriolar vasodilation","E":"VEGF upregulates aquaporin-4 water channels on choroid plexus cells, stimulating CSF overproduction that creates a communicating hydrocephalus, raising intracranial pressure through a volume-driven rather than edema-driven mechanism"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In HACE, the edema results from two complementary pathways: (1) PRIMARY hemodynamic — hypoxia → cerebral arteriolar vasodilation → ↑hydrostatic capillary pressure → fluid filtration. (2) AMPLIFIED by vascular permeability — the local hypoxia activates HIF-1, which drives transcription of VEGF and inflammatory cytokines. These mediators directly disrupt blood-brain barrier tight junction proteins (claudin-5, occludin, ZO-1), increasing paracellular permeability and allowing protein-rich fluid (albumin, immunoglobulins) to enter the interstitium. The protein-rich fluid distinguishes this permeability-mediated vasogenic edema from a pure hydrostatic transudate and explains why edema exceeds what the hemodynamic component alone would predict. Note: VEGF's acute role here is INCREASED PERMEABILITY in existing vessels — NOT angiogenesis (a days-to-weeks process requiring endothelial proliferation). Aquaporin-4 is expressed on astrocyte end-feet for fluid reabsorption, not on choroid plexus for production.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HACE = two-hit vasogenic edema: (1) hypoxia → vasodilation → ↑hydrostatic pressure; (2) VEGF + cytokines → ↑BBB permeability → protein-rich exudate. Same VEGF/cytokine mechanism also amplifies HAPE. Dexamethasone targets vasogenic edema by suppressing cytokine-mediated permeability. COMLEX: HACE + HAPE share the VEGF/cytokine permeability mechanism — key integrating concept for altitude physiology questions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 1 — HIF target genes, including VEGF","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Note: VEGF's acute role here i"}},{"id":"sdl36_q07","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — High-Altitude Pulmonary Edema (HAPE) — Pathophysiology and Clinical Findings","batch":1,"stem":"A 32-year-old competitive mountain runner ascends to 3,800 meters over 36 hours. On day 2, he develops progressive dyspnea at rest and produces pink frothy sputum. Temperature is 37.1°C. SpO2 = 58% on room air. Chest X-ray shows bilateral patchy infiltrates without cardiomegaly. Echocardiography shows normal left ventricular ejection fraction (65%), normal left atrial size, and pulmonary artery systolic pressure of 68 mmHg. Which of the following physiologic mechanisms is the PRIMARY driver of the elevated pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure in this patient?","choices":{"A":"High-altitude sympathetic activation causes systemic arterioconstriction that shifts blood volume centrally into the pulmonary circuit, creating a volume overload pattern similar to cardiogenic pulmonary edema with an elevated pulmonary capillary wedge pressure","B":"Left ventricular failure from hypoxia-induced negative inotropy reduces cardiac output and causes backward pressure elevation in pulmonary veins, elevating pulmonary capillary wedge pressure and driving hydrostatic transudation across the alveolar-capillary membrane","C":"Global symmetric hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction raises mean pulmonary artery pressure uniformly across all vascular territories, and the uniformly elevated PAP is transmitted equally to capillaries throughout both lungs simultaneously","D":"Unequal hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction diverts flow into the less-constricted arterioles, raising capillary hydrostatic pressure in those regions far above normal and producing edema selectively in the under-constricted segments","E":"Hypocapnia from altitude hyperventilation produces pulmonary arteriolar vasodilation via a direct CO2-mediated mechanism on vascular smooth muscle, paradoxically increasing capillary transmural pressure through reduced arteriolar resistance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"HAPE is the most common cause of altitude-related death and is a NON-CARDIOGENIC pulmonary edema. The pathophysiology hinges on UNEQUAL HPV: the HPV response is spatially heterogeneous — some arteriolar territories receive a stronger HPV stimulus than others (due to local variations in alveolar PO2, smooth muscle sensitivity, or neural input). Blood is redirected from highly-constricted territories to less-constricted zones. In those less-constricted zones, perfusion pressure rises dramatically, rising far above the normal ~7 mmHg and past the ~28 mmHg plasma colloid osmotic pressure. This capillary hypertension drives fluid into the alveolar space — initially a proteinaceous transudate, later amplified by VEGF/cytokine-mediated permeability changes. The hallmarks are: (1) elevated PAP, (2) NORMAL PCWP (ruling out cardiogenic cause — the echocardiogram and normal EF here confirm no LV failure), (3) bilateral patchy, often asymmetric infiltrates reflecting the regional nature of the process.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HAPE = non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema from UNEQUAL HPV → regional ↑capillary pressure → edema. Key findings: normal EF, normal PCWP, elevated PAP, patchy bilateral infiltrates, rapid ascent history. Most common fatal altitude illness. Treatment: O2 (reverses HPV in hours) + descent; nifedipine ↓HPV when O2/descent unavailable. First Aid p.688: chronic HPV → pulmonary HTN (CMS). Guyton Ch 44: acute unequal HPV → HAPE. COMLEX: CP 9.3 SOB at altitude + crackles + normal heart size = HAPE.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Robbins' categories of pulmonary edema, which place high-altitude edema under undetermined origin","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The hallmarks are: (1) elevate","B":"The hallmarks are: (1) elevate","C":"The pathophysiology hinges on "}},{"id":"sdl36_q08","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — High-Altitude Pulmonary Edema (HAPE) — Pathophysiology and Clinical Findings","batch":1,"stem":"A physician retrieves a 28-year-old climber with HAPE from 4,500 m. Echocardiography confirms a normal pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP = 7 mmHg). Bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) fluid is sent for analysis. Which BAL finding would MOST strongly confirm the contribution of increased endothelial permeability — beyond the hemodynamic component — to the pulmonary edema in HAPE?","choices":{"A":"Elevated B-type natriuretic peptide (BNP) and N-terminal pro-BNP — indicating cardiomyocyte wall stress from left ventricular diastolic dysfunction producing backward pressure elevation despite the normal measured PCWP","B":"Neutrophil-predominant exudate with gram-negative bacilli and high lactate dehydrogenase — indicating superimposed bacterial pneumonia precipitated by hypoxia-induced suppression of alveolar macrophage function during altitude exposure","C":"Elevated protein concentration, VEGF, and inflammatory cytokines (IL-6, IL-8) — indicating protein-rich edema from increased endothelial permeability added to the initial hydrostatic mechanism of HAPE","D":"Elevated amylase levels and squamous epithelial cell clusters — indicating esophageal rupture from forceful coughing, with gastric contents aspirated into the alveoli producing a chemical pneumonitis that mimics HAPE on chest imaging","E":"Predominantly lipid-laden alveolar macrophages with low protein concentration and normal cytokine levels — indicating a pure hydrostatic transudate consistent with mild volume overload from altitude-induced antidiuretic hormone release"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Modern research has clarified that HAPE involves both a HEMODYNAMIC component (unequal HPV → regional ↑capillary pressure → hydrostatic filtration) AND a PERMEABILITY component (local hypoxia → HIF-1 → VEGF → ↑endothelial permeability → protein-rich exudate). BAL studies from HAPE patients consistently show: (a) high total protein, much higher than in cardiogenic transudates; (b) elevated inflammatory cytokines (IL-6, IL-8, TNF-α); (c) elevated VEGF; (d) later stages — alveolar macrophages and mild neutrophilia. The KEY distinguishing point from cardiogenic edema: cardiogenic = low-protein transudate with normal cytokines; HAPE = high-protein exudate with elevated VEGF/cytokines (indicating permeability involvement beyond pure hydrostatic filtration). The normal PCWP in this patient already rules out LV failure. BNP in BAL is not diagnostic; it would be measured in serum/plasma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HAPE BAL = high-protein fluid with VEGF + cytokines (NOT the low-protein transudate of cardiogenic pulmonary edema). The permeability component explains why dexamethasone has some role in HAPE and why protein accumulates in alveoli. Normal PCWP = non-cardiogenic. First Aid p.688: chronic HPV → pulmonary HTN; Guyton Ch 44 attributes HAPE to unequal acute HPV. COMLEX: High-protein exudate with cytokines = permeability edema; low-protein transudate = hydrostatic/cardiogenic.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — the permeability edema of ARDS, the other protein-rich alveolar edema","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"BNP in BAL is not diagnostic; ","E":"The KEY distinguishing point f"}},{"id":"sdl36_q09","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — High-Altitude Pulmonary Edema (HAPE) — Pathophysiology and Clinical Findings","batch":1,"stem":"A 36-year-old trekker in Nepal develops HAPE at 4,200 meters. Helicopter evacuation is impossible due to weather. The camp physician administers 4 L/min supplemental oxygen via nasal cannula. Within 90 minutes, SpO2 improves from 60% to 94% and respiratory distress visibly diminishes. Which of the following BEST explains the physiologic mechanism underlying this rapid clinical improvement?","choices":{"A":"Supplemental oxygen activates mitochondrial cytochrome oxidase in alveolar type II pneumocytes, stimulating surfactant synthesis that reduces alveolar surface tension, expands collapsed alveoli, and eliminates the capillary-alveolar pressure gradient responsible for fluid filtration","B":"High-flow oxygen competes directly with nitrogen for hemoglobin binding sites, displacing nitrogen from erythrocytes into the plasma and raising intravascular colloid osmotic pressure, which draws fluid from the alveolar space back into the pulmonary capillaries","C":"Supplemental oxygen rapidly suppresses VEGF gene transcription by restoring normoxia and triggering PHD enzyme reactivation that degrades HIF-1α; VEGF protein levels fall within 30 minutes of O2 administration, immediately restoring tight junction integrity","D":"Supplemental O2 raises alveolar PO2 and reverses hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction, so flow redistributes evenly across the lung and the regional capillary hypertension subsides, allowing the alveolar edema fluid to be reabsorbed within hours","E":"Supplemental oxygen paradoxically causes pulmonary vasoconstriction by activating ATP-sensitive potassium channels in pulmonary vascular smooth muscle, directly raising vessel wall tension and compressing dilated capillaries to reduce their filtration surface area"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The rapid reversibility of HAPE with oxygen is a clinically critical board fact. The mechanism is strictly hemodynamic: (1) Supplemental O2 raises alveolar PO2. (2) HPV is driven by LOW alveolar PO2 — it is a direct, locally mediated response of pulmonary vascular smooth muscle to hypoxia (not neurally dependent). When PO2 rises, HPV signal is removed. (3) Pulmonary arterioles relax → blood flow equalizes across all vascular territories → the focal capillary hypertension in the under-constricted zones is relieved. (4) With reduced transcapillary pressure, lymphatic drainage and Starling reabsorption can clear the alveolar fluid over hours. This is the same reason nifedipine (a calcium channel blocker that inhibits HPV) can treat HAPE. VEGF suppression via HIF-1α degradation takes hours to days, not minutes — the rapid response (90 minutes) confirms a VASCULAR mechanism, not a transcriptional one.","boardPrep":"Step 1: O2 → reverses HPV → equalizes pulmonary flow → ↓regional capillary pressure → HAPE resolves in HOURS. Same mechanism as nifedipine (CCB → ↓HPV → ↓capillary pressure). HPV is LOCAL and direct — no neural mediation. COMLEX: Treatment priority in HAPE: O2 first (rapid vascular reversal), then descent (definitive). Nifedipine = pharmacologic adjunct via ↓HPV (links to SDL 38 Obj 1 — medications for pulmonary hypertension).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 12 — the value of oxygen therapy in different types of hypoxia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q10","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Physiological Adaptations of High-Altitude Natives","batch":1,"stem":"A pediatrician working at a clinic in the Bolivian Altiplano (3,600–4,200 m) notes that children born and raised at altitude have significantly larger chest circumferences and higher vital capacities than (a) lowland children of the same age, and (b) children of lowland-origin parents who migrated to altitude as adults. The children of lowland parents raised from birth at altitude show GREATER structural development than their adult-migrant parents. Which of the following BEST explains why structural chest and lung adaptations occur in native-born children but NOT in adults who ascend to altitude later in life?","choices":{"A":"Native-born children express altitude-specific genetic alleles (EPAS1, PPARA variants) that program larger lung and chest dimensions; these alleles are absent in lowland-origin children regardless of the altitude at which they are raised or the age at which exposure begins","B":"Native-born children at altitude benefit from increased growth hormone secretion in response to chronic hypoxia, causing diffuse somatic overgrowth that preferentially expands the thoracic cage because respiratory muscles require disproportionate support during hypoxic breathing","C":"Native-born children develop larger chests because high-altitude diets are richer in calcium and phosphorus, supporting superior musculoskeletal development; the chest size difference reflects nutritional rather than physiological adaptation to chronic hypoxia","D":"Hypoxic stimuli acting during infancy and childhood, the critical window for chest wall, lung parenchymal, and cardiac growth, drive structural remodeling; adults have completed skeletal and organ growth and cannot remodel to the same degree","E":"Altitude adaptation in children depends entirely on exposure during the fetal period via placental transfer of maternal HIF-1α target gene products; postnatal altitude exposure in the absence of fetal priming cannot produce structural chest or lung changes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The key insight from Guyton is that high-altitude natives (not merely acclimatized visitors) demonstrate structural differences that arise during a CRITICAL DEVELOPMENTAL WINDOW in infancy and childhood: (1) Barrel-shaped chest with increased anteroposterior diameter; (2) Significantly larger lung volumes (FVC, FRC); (3) Larger hearts (greater cardiac output from the neonatal period onward); (4) Greater capillary density in tissues. These changes cannot be acquired by adults because skeletal maturity prevents the degree of structural chest wall and parenchymal remodeling that occurs when chronic hypoxia acts during active growth phases. The fact that children of lowland parents raised from birth at altitude show greater structural adaptation than their adult-migrant parents (who share the same genetic background) confirms this is a DEVELOPMENTAL/ENVIRONMENTAL response — driven by timing of hypoxic exposure during growth — not solely a genetic predetermination.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Native altitude adaptations = STRUCTURAL changes beginning in INFANCY: ↑chest size, ↑lung volume, ↑cardiac output from birth. CANNOT be acquired by adult acclimatization. Acclimatized adults achieve ~68% of sea-level work capacity; natives achieve ~87%. This structural advantage is why elite altitude athletes outperform even long-acclimatized lowlanders. COMLEX: Acclimatization (reversible, weeks) vs. native structural adaptation (permanent, lifelong, developmentally acquired).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — the timing of lung maturation, including postnatal growth","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The fact that children of lowl"}},{"id":"sdl36_q11","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Physiological Adaptations of High-Altitude Natives","batch":1,"stem":"Researchers measure arterial oxygen parameters in three groups: lifelong Andean natives at 4,200 m, lowlanders fully acclimatized at altitude for 6 months, and sea-level controls. Despite the high-altitude natives having PaO2 = 40 mmHg — far below sea-level controls at 95 mmHg — their arterial oxygen content (CaO2 = 1.34 × Hb × SaO2 + 0.003 × PaO2) matches or exceeds that of sea-level controls. Which single physiologic variable MOST directly accounts for this paradox?","choices":{"A":"Markedly elevated hemoglobin concentration (Hb ≈ 20 g/dL vs. sea-level 15 g/dL in natives): even at reduced PaO2 and SaO2, the elevated Hb increases O2 carrying capacity per deciliter of blood so substantially that total CaO2 equals or exceeds sea-level values","B":"Persistent leftward shift of the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve in high-altitude natives, increasing hemoglobin-oxygen affinity such that SaO2 at PaO2 = 40 mmHg closely matches sea-level SaO2 at PaO2 = 95 mmHg","C":"Greatly increased dissolved oxygen in plasma via altitude-induced changes in plasma protein composition that raise the Bunsen solubility coefficient fourfold, making the (0.003 × PaO2) dissolved term the dominant contributor to CaO2 in natives","D":"Increased RBC mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration (MCHC) from altitude: each individual RBC carries proportionally more hemoglobin per cell, increasing O2 content per unit blood volume without requiring an elevated total hemoglobin concentration","E":"Continuous hyperventilation at altitude maintains alveolar PO2 near 95 mmHg despite reduced barometric pressure; the measured PaO2 of 40 mmHg is therefore artificially low and does not reflect the true oxygen tension available for hemoglobin loading"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This is Figure 44.2 from Guyton — the classic 'native altitude O2 transport paradox.' The CaO2 formula: CaO2 = (1.34 × Hb × SaO2) + (0.003 × PaO2). Native at 4,200 m: Hb ≈ 20 g/dL (EPO/HIF-driven), SaO2 ≈ 73% at PaO2 = 40 mmHg (from ODC sigmoid). CaO2 = (1.34 × 20 × 0.73) + (0.003 × 40) = 19.6 + 0.12 ≈ 19.7 mL/dL. Sea-level control: Hb = 15, SaO2 ≈ 97%, PaO2 = 95 mmHg. CaO2 = (1.34 × 15 × 0.97) + (0.003 × 95) = 19.5 + 0.29 ≈ 19.8 mL/dL. Nearly identical — the native's elevated Hb fully compensates for the low PaO2. There is actually a RIGHTWARD ODC shift at altitude (from elevated 2,3-BPG) — not leftward. Dissolved O2 is always negligible (~1% of total). MCHC is not the altitude adaptation variable.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CaO2 = (1.34 × Hb × SaO2) + dissolved. Hb is the DOMINANT variable. Natives: Hb ≈ 20 g/dL → CaO2 ≈ sea-level despite PaO2 = 40 mmHg. Formula math (showing that ↑Hb compensates for ↓SaO2) is directly tested on Step 1. First Aid p.688: ↑EPO → ↑Hct → compensatory for altitude. COMLEX: O2 delivery = CO × CaO2; Hb concentration = dominant determinant of CaO2.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — hemoglobin's O2-carrying capacity and arterial O2 content","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"There is actually a RIGHTWARD ","C":"Dissolved O2 is always negligi","D":"MCHC is not the altitude adapt"}},{"id":"sdl36_q12","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Physiological Adaptations of High-Altitude Natives","batch":1,"stem":"A research team tests maximum aerobic work capacity in three groups working at 5,200 meters (17,000 feet): Group 1 = unacclimatized sea-level lowlanders; Group 2 = lowlanders acclimatized at altitude for 2 months; Group 3 = Andean natives living at 4,000 m (13,200 ft). Compared to each group's sea-level performance, which of the following CORRECTLY matches each group to its expected maximum work capacity at altitude?","choices":{"A":"Group 1 ≈ 70% of sea-level capacity; Group 2 ≈ 90% of sea-level capacity; Group 3 = 100% of sea-level capacity — demonstrating that 2 months of acclimatization restores near-complete aerobic performance and that natives perform identically to sea-level-tested athletes","B":"Group 1 ≈ 50% of sea-level capacity; Group 2 ≈ 68% of sea-level capacity; Group 3 ≈ 87% of sea-level capacity — demonstrating that even complete physiologic acclimatization cannot fully compensate for the structural and developmental advantages of lifelong native altitude residents","C":"Group 1 ≈ 50% of sea-level capacity; Group 2 ≈ 50% of sea-level capacity; Group 3 ≈ 75% of sea-level capacity — demonstrating that physiologic acclimatization provides no measurable work capacity benefit over unacclimatized exposure","D":"Group 1 ≈ 80% of sea-level capacity; Group 2 ≈ 80% of sea-level capacity; Group 3 ≈ 95% of sea-level capacity — demonstrating that the initial hypoxic response is minimally impaired and only the final 5–20% gap requires native adaptations to overcome","E":"Group 1 ≈ 35% of sea-level capacity; Group 2 ≈ 55% of sea-level capacity; Group 3 = 100% of sea-level capacity — demonstrating that only lifelong native structural adaptation fully normalizes aerobic performance at altitude to sea-level equivalence"},"correct":"B","explanation":"These values come directly from Guyton & Hall Table 44.2 — work capacity at 17,000 ft: unacclimatized = ~50%, 2-month acclimatized = ~68%, lifelong native = ~87%. Key takeaways: (1) Unacclimatized: acute hypoxia severely limits VO2max (50% of baseline). (2) 2-month acclimatized: ventilatory, hematologic (↑Hb), and cardiovascular (↑capillary density) adaptations improve performance to 68% — but cannot match native adaptations. (3) Lifelong native: structural chest/lung expansion, ↑cardiac output from birth, ↑capillary density → 87% of sea-level capacity. Neither the acclimatized lowlander nor the native reaches 100% — altitude always imposes some constraint. These exact percentages (50/68/87) are board-testable. The principle: acclimatization = physiologic, reversible; native adaptation = structural, permanent, developmentally acquired in infancy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Memorize Table 44.2 values: unacclimatized 50%, 2-month acclimatized 68%, native 87% of sea-level max work capacity. Neither group fully reaches 100% at altitude — altitude always impairs maximum aerobic output to some degree. COMLEX: This principle explains why altitude training at moderate elevation then competing at sea level improves athletic performance — acclimatization adaptations (↑Hb, ↑ventilatory capacity) persist for weeks after descent.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 9 — exercise adaptations that enhance O2 transport","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Neither the acclimatized lowla","E":"Neither the acclimatized lowla"}},{"id":"sdl36_q13","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — G-Force Effects, Baroreceptor Reflex, and Microgravity Physiology","batch":1,"stem":"A flight physiology instructor presents G-force concepts to aerospace medicine students. She explains that centripetal force acting on a pilot during aerial maneuvers can be quantified by a standard formula, and that G-force is expressed as a multiple of a reference unit. Which of the following CORRECTLY states both the formula for centripetal force (f) acting on a body moving in a circular path AND the definition of +1G?","choices":{"A":"f = m²v/r, where the squared mass term reflects the amplified inertial effect of acceleration on a rotating body; +1G represents the minimum gravitational force required to maintain arterial perfusion pressure at the level of the cerebral cortex in a standing subject","B":"f = m²v²/2r, derived from kinetic energy principles of circular motion; +1G represents the gravitational acceleration threshold at which loss of consciousness occurs in a resting supine individual without any cardiovascular disease","C":"f = mv/r², where the inverse-square-of-radius reflects the dependence of gravitational force on distance; +1G represents the standard gravitational force at the altitude of commercial aircraft cruise (approximately 10,000 meters)","D":"f = mv²/r, where m = mass, v = velocity, and r = radius of curvature; +1G represents the force of normal Earth gravity on a stationary body at sea level (approximately 9.8 m/s²), which serves as the reference baseline for all gravitational force measurements","E":"f = mv²/r²; +1G represents the force at which baroreceptor activation is first triggered in a standing person experiencing transition from seated to erect posture during routine orthostatic testing"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The formula for centripetal force is f = mv²/r — standard Newtonian mechanics, explicitly cited in Guyton Chapter 44 as the basis for understanding G-force physiology. Variables: m = mass of the pilot, v = velocity of the aircraft, r = radius of curvature of the maneuver. As v increases (faster flight) or r decreases (tighter turn), force increases proportionally with v² and inversely with r. +1G is defined as the force of NORMAL EARTH GRAVITY — the baseline reference (9.81 m/s²). A person standing at rest experiences +1G. Aerial maneuvers produce multiples of this: +2G = twice normal gravity, +4G = four times, etc. +G (positive G, head-to-foot) → blood pools in lower body; −G (negative G, foot-to-head) → blood to head. The threshold for G-induced loss of consciousness (G-LOC) is +4–6G in untrained pilots — not +1G.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Centripetal force: f = mv²/r. +1G = normal gravity reference = 9.81 m/s². Positive G (head-to-foot) → blood pools lower body → ↓venous return → ↓CO → ↓cerebral perfusion → grayout → blackout → G-LOC above +4–6G. Negative G (foot-to-head) → blood to head → redout. COMLEX: G-force physiology = environmental extremes category; baroreceptor reflex response to +G is tested as cardiovascular regulatory mechanism question.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 7 — hydrostatic pressure gradients in the upright lung, the gravity effect that G-force multiplies","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The threshold for G-induced lo"}},{"id":"sdl36_q14","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — G-Force Effects, Baroreceptor Reflex, and Microgravity Physiology","batch":1,"stem":"During a high-performance aircraft maneuver, a pilot experiences sustained +5G acceleration in the positive (head-to-foot) direction. The flight surgeon explains the cardiovascular consequences to medical students. Beginning with the primary redistribution of blood volume, which of the following CORRECTLY traces the cardiovascular sequence of events during sustained positive G-force exposure?","choices":{"A":"Positive G compresses both the thoracic aorta and the inferior vena cava simultaneously, squeezing blood centrally from peripheral veins and paradoxically INCREASING cardiac output; the net effect is transiently elevated cerebral perfusion pressure during the maneuver before eventual cardiovascular exhaustion","B":"Positive G activates pulmonary stretch receptors via increased lung volume, triggering the Hering-Breuer reflex that reduces respiratory rate; the resulting CO2 retention causes cerebral vasodilation that maintains cerebral perfusion despite lower cardiac output and blood pressure","C":"Positive G immediately activates baroreceptors by raising carotid sinus pressure, causing reflexive parasympathetic activation with marked bradycardia and systemic hypotension as the baroreceptor system attempts to prevent cerebrovascular overload","D":"Positive G activates peripheral chemoreceptors in the carotid bodies by mechanical compression of the carotid sinus vessels, triggering reflex hyperventilation that reduces PaCO2 and causes cerebral vasoconstriction as the primary mechanism of grayout","E":"Blood is displaced into the lower extremities and abdomen → venous return and right ventricular preload fall → cardiac output falls → head-level perfusion pressure drops → retinal then cerebral hypoperfusion → grayout → blackout (vision lost, consciousness kept) → G-LOC"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Positive G (head-to-foot direction) exaggerates normal gravitational displacement of blood. The sequence per Guyton Chapter 44: (1) +G force → blood pools in lower body (legs, abdomen) due to gravitational displacement. (2) Less blood returns via IVC to right heart → ↓venous return. (3) ↓right heart preload → ↓stroke volume (Frank-Starling mechanism). (4) ↓cardiac output → ↓perfusion pressure at head level (which is literally higher than the heart in +G — additional hydrostatic resistance against upward blood flow is dramatically increased). (5) Retinal/choroidal perfusion falls → grayout (peripheral scotoma, loss of peripheral vision). (6) Cerebral perfusion falls → blackout (complete visual loss without loss of consciousness). (7) G-LOC if sustained above +4–6G. The baroreceptor reflex (see Q15) does activate and partially compensates — but is overwhelmed at high G forces. Positive G does NOT cause Hering-Breuer activation in this context; it causes LOWER lung volume (abdominal organs pushed down), not stretch.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Positive G → blood pools lower body → ↓venous return → ↓CO → ↓cerebral perfusion. Sequence: grayout → blackout → G-LOC. Anti-G suits compress legs/abdomen → ↑venous return → ↑CO → delay grayout. Reclining position (tilted back, perpendicular to G vector) = spacecraft strategy to tolerate high G at launch. COMLEX: Baroreceptors, venous return, Starling law = cardiovascular regulatory mechanisms tested in G-force context.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 8 — the zone differences in pressure and blood flow, exaggerated under positive G","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl36_q15","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — G-Force Effects, Baroreceptor Reflex, and Microgravity Physiology","batch":1,"stem":"A physiology researcher simulates +3.3G exposure in a centrifuge. Continuous intra-arterial pressure monitoring at head level records: an initial rapid fall in systolic pressure from 80 mmHg to less than 22 mmHg within seconds, followed by partial recovery over the next 10–15 seconds to approximately 55/20 mmHg, where pressure stabilizes. Consciousness is maintained throughout. Which reflex mechanism is responsible for the observed pressure recovery after the initial fall?","choices":{"A":"The initial hypotension stimulates peripheral chemoreceptors in the carotid bodies to perceive tissue hypoxia, triggering the diving reflex — a massive reflex bradycardia with intense peripheral vasoconstriction that paradoxically elevates blood pressure primarily through a dramatic increase in systemic vascular resistance","B":"The recovery reflects activation of the Bainbridge reflex: reduced right atrial pressure detected by atrial mechanoreceptors signals the brainstem to reduce heart rate, paradoxically improving ventricular filling time and restoring stroke volume through improved myocardial relaxation","C":"The fall in pressure reduces stretch of the carotid sinus and aortic arch baroreceptors → decreased afferent firing to the medullary cardiovascular center → increased sympathetic outflow → ↑heart rate, ↑contractility, and ↑vascular resistance → partial recovery of head-level pressure","D":"Adrenal medullary epinephrine release is triggered directly by spinal sympathetic neurons bypassing the brainstem; the adrenal response precedes and dominates over the baroreceptor response, and this catecholamine surge alone accounts for the pressure recovery","E":"The recovery represents passive redistribution of blood from the lower extremities back toward the heart via venous elastic recoil alone; the pressure restoration occurs without any active neural reflex involvement and plateaus at 55/20 mmHg due to venous capacitance limits"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Figure 44.3 in Guyton demonstrates the baroreceptor-mediated recovery during +3.3G exposure — with the specific values: initial head-level systolic pressure <22 mmHg → recovery to ~55/20 mmHg after baroreceptor activation. The mechanism: (1) At +3.3G, blood pools inferiorly → ↓venous return → ↓cardiac output → arterial pressure at head level falls rapidly to <22 mmHg. (2) Carotid sinus and aortic arch baroreceptors detect the ↓stretch → reduce afferent firing to medullary cardiovascular center. (3) Medullary response: ↑sympathetic outflow + ↓vagal tone. (4) Sympathetic effectors: ↑HR (positive chronotropy), ↑contractility (positive inotropy), vasoconstriction of arterioles and capacitance veins, splenic contraction (autotransfusion). (5) Net effect: partial pressure recovery to 55/20 mmHg — incomplete because +3.3G continues to pool blood inferiorly, limiting venous return despite maximal sympathetic compensation. Above +4–6G: this reflex is overwhelmed → G-LOC. At ~20G: vertebral compressive fracture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: At +3.3G: initial BP <22 mmHg → baroreceptor reflex → ↑sympathetic → recovery to 55/20 mmHg. Above +4–6G: baroreceptors overwhelmed → G-LOC. Anti-G suit delays G-LOC by enhancing venous return. The baroreceptor reflex plateaus at 55/20 mmHg (not full recovery) because gravitation continues to oppose venous return. COMLEX: Baroreceptor reflex = cardiovascular regulatory mechanism — response to acute hypotension: ↑HR, ↑contractility, ↑SVR.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 6 — the CN IX and CN X afferents to the medulla that carotid and aortic chemoreceptors share","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl36_q16","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Chronic Mountain Sickness (CMS) — Pathogenesis, Mechanisms, and Recovery","batch":2,"stem":"A high-altitude researcher compares hematologic parameters in three groups at 4,200 m: (A) permanent residents with chronic mountain sickness, (B) acclimatized lowlanders (6 months at altitude), and (C) sea-level controls. Among the CMS residents, which finding is MOST characteristic and distinguishes them pathologically from the acclimatized lowlanders — who also show elevated hemoglobin as an appropriate response?","choices":{"A":"Hemoglobin and hematocrit rising beyond the adaptive range of acclimatized lowlanders and producing symptomatic hyperviscosity (headache, dyspnea, plethora), so the polycythemia impairs rather than improves O2 delivery","B":"Elevated 2,3-BPG causing a rightward shift of the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve to P50 > 35 mmHg — a finding common to acclimatized lowlanders but absent in CMS patients, whose O2-hemoglobin affinity paradoxically increases as part of the maladaptive process","C":"Blunted hypoxic ventilatory response to inhaled CO2 — a respiratory reflex abnormality that selectively affects CMS patients and distinguishes them from acclimatized lowlanders who maintain normal ventilatory responses throughout altitude residence","D":"Elevated pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP > 18 mmHg) indicating left ventricular diastolic failure from hyperviscosity — the hemodynamic finding that distinguishes CMS cardiac failure from the cor pulmonale of the acclimatized lowlander","E":"Polycythemia vera confirmed by JAK2 V617F mutation analysis — an acquired clonal hematopoietic stem cell mutation that selectively develops in CMS patients due to chronic HIF-1 activation driving somatic mutagenesis in erythroid progenitors"},"correct":"A","explanation":"CMS (Monge's disease) represents the MALADAPTIVE extreme of altitude-driven erythropoiesis. Acclimatized lowlanders show appropriate polycythemia (Guyton Ch 44: hematocrit about 60%, Hb about 20 g/dL) — this improves O2 content and delivery. In CMS, this process overshoots and the red cell mass and hematocrit become exceptionally high (Guyton Ch 44). At this extreme, the three CMS mechanisms converge to impair delivery: (1) Hyperviscosity reduces capillary flow; (2) Global HPV → sustained ↑PAP → cor pulmonale; (3) AV shunts bypass gas exchange. The diagnosis of CMS vs. acclimatization is based on the DEGREE of polycythemia AND the presence of symptoms (headache, extreme dyspnea, plethora, reduced exercise tolerance) — not on the presence of polycythemia per se. Note: CMS polycythemia is SECONDARY (appropriate EPO response, but excessive) — NOT polycythemia vera (which requires a JAK2 mutation and is EPO-independent).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CMS = MALADAPTIVE polycythemia beyond the adaptive range (exceptionally high red cell mass and Hct). Distinguish from normal altitude adaptation by: degree of polycythemia + symptoms of hyperviscosity + cor pulmonale. NOT polycythemia vera (no JAK2 mutation; EPO is elevated, not low). Treatment: phlebotomy, descent, acetazolamide. COMLEX: CMS = altitude resident with excessive polycythemia + cor pulmonale + hyperviscosity symptoms.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors: pharmacology and therapeutic role in acute mountain sickness","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Note: CMS polycythemia is SECO"}},{"id":"sdl36_q17","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Chronic Mountain Sickness (CMS) — Pathogenesis, Mechanisms, and Recovery","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old high-altitude resident is diagnosed with cor pulmonale secondary to chronic mountain sickness. Echocardiography shows right ventricular dilation, right ventricular hypertrophy, tricuspid regurgitation, and estimated pulmonary artery systolic pressure of 72 mmHg. His left ventricular function is normal. A medical student asks the attending to trace the complete pathophysiologic sequence from altitude hypoxia to right heart failure in CMS. Which of the following MOST accurately describes this sequence?","choices":{"A":"Chronic hypoxia → reduced cardiac inotropy → decreased left ventricular output → elevated pulmonary venous pressure (backward failure) → elevated pulmonary capillary wedge pressure → pulmonary vein hypertension → reactive arteriolar vasoconstriction → secondary right ventricular overload","B":"Chronic hyperviscosity → microvascular thrombosis in pulmonary arterioles → progressive obliterative pulmonary arteriopathy → anatomic obstruction of the pulmonary vascular bed → elevated pulmonary vascular resistance → right ventricular overload","C":"Chronic hypoxia → sympathetic activation → systemic hypertension → left ventricular hypertrophy → septal shift into the right ventricle → mechanical compression of right ventricular outflow tract → elevated right-sided pressures","D":"Chronic hypoxia → HIF-1 → ↑VEGF → pulmonary angiogenesis with abnormal vessel formation → arteriovenous fistula development in the pulmonary circulation → high-output right ventricular failure from increased pulmonary blood flow","E":"Chronic alveolar hypoxia → global hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction → sustained pulmonary hypertension → RV pressure overload → RV hypertrophy, then dilation and failure → tricuspid regurgitation → raised systemic venous pressure → edema, hepatomegaly, ascites"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The cor pulmonale of CMS follows a strict mechanical sequence driven by global HPV. Unlike HAPE (where the primary issue is capillary hydrostatic edema) or left heart failure (where PCWP is elevated), CMS cor pulmonale is a PRE-CAPILLARY pulmonary arterial hypertension from arteriolar vasoconstriction: (1) All alveoli are chronically hypoxic → GLOBAL HPV → all pulmonary arterioles constrict. (2) PAP rises and remains elevated (not transient as in acute altitude exposure). (3) RV works against sustained elevated afterload → hypertrophies (adaptive). (4) Eventually: RV dilation → contractile failure → tricuspid annular dilation → tricuspid regurgitation. (5) Elevated right heart filling pressures → raised systemic venous pressure → hepatomegaly, peripheral edema, ascites. Left ventricle is uninvolved (PCWP normal) — distinguishes CMS cor pulmonale from LV failure-induced pulmonary HTN. The cause is ARTERIOLAR constriction (not thrombosis, not angiogenesis, not LV failure).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CMS cor pulmonale pathway: Chronic alveolar hypoxia → global HPV → ↑PAP → RV pressure overload → RVH → RV failure → right heart failure signs (JVD, edema, hepatomegaly). PCWP is NORMAL (pre-capillary pulmonary HTN). Contrast with LV failure: PCWP elevated + pulmonary edema. First Aid p.688: chronic hypoxic vasoconstriction → pulmonary HTN → RVH. COMLEX: Cor pulmonale = right heart failure from pulmonary cause (not LV failure).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors: pharmacology and therapeutic role in acute mountain sickness","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The cause is ARTERIOLAR constr","B":"The cause is ARTERIOLAR constr","D":"The cause is ARTERIOLAR constr"}},{"id":"sdl36_q18","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Chronic Mountain Sickness (CMS) — Pathogenesis, Mechanisms, and Recovery","batch":2,"stem":"A pathophysiology professor describes the three mechanisms by which chronic mountain sickness impairs oxygen delivery to tissues. After explaining hyperviscosity (Mechanism 1) and global HPV leading to cor pulmonale (Mechanism 2), she turns to the third mechanism. She states: 'In CMS, alveolar arteriolar spasm creates pathologic bypass channels through which desaturated blood circumvents ventilated alveoli entirely.' Through which specific vascular pathway does this desaturated blood re-enter the left heart without undergoing gas exchange?","choices":{"A":"Thebesian veins connecting pulmonary arterioles directly to left ventricular chambers: venous blood draining from hypoxic airway walls re-enters the left ventricle through these minimally-oxygenated cardiac venous channels, mimicking a right-to-left shunt","B":"Pulmonary arteriovenous shunts (anatomic or functional): alveolar arteriolar spasm in hypoxic regions diverts blood flow into pre-capillary bypass channels that connect pulmonary arterioles to pulmonary venules, allowing mixed venous blood (low PO2) to reach the left heart without crossing ventilated alveolar surfaces","C":"Bronchopulmonary anastomoses: bronchial arteries carrying systemic deoxygenated blood connect directly to pulmonary veins in the peribronchial plexus, draining desaturated blood from the airway walls into pulmonary venous return to the left atrium","D":"Intracardiac right-to-left shunting through a patent foramen ovale or ventricular septal defect: elevated pulmonary artery pressure in CMS forces blood across a previously asymptomatic congenital communication into the left heart, bypassing pulmonary gas exchange","E":"Hypoxic zones create true anatomic dead space (type III V/Q mismatch) rather than shunt: arterioles are vasoconstricted to zero flow in the most hypoxic regions, while blood is perfectly gas-exchanged in all other regions — the result is that overall mixed venous PO2 falls, not that unsaturated blood enters the left heart"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The third CMS oxygen delivery impairment mechanism per Guyton: alveolar arteriolar spasm in the most severely hypoxic alveolar territories creates pathologic pulmonary arteriovenous shunt pathways. These pre-capillary bypass channels allow blood to flow from pulmonary arterioles to pulmonary venules without traversing the alveolar capillary bed — effectively a V/Q ratio = 0 (shunt) rather than a dead space (V/Q = infinity). The blood exiting these shunt pathways has the same O2 content as mixed venous blood (PO2 ~40 mmHg, SaO2 ~73% in altitude residents). This desaturated blood mixes with fully oxygenated blood from other lung regions, reducing the overall arterial SaO2 in the left heart below what even the reduced alveolar PO2 would predict. This is distinct from intracardiac shunts (which require anatomic defects) and from dead space (which produces CO2 retention but not arterial desaturation from shunting of deoxygenated blood).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Three CMS oxygen delivery failure mechanisms: (1) Hyperviscosity → ↓capillary flow; (2) Global HPV → ↑PAP → cor pulmonale; (3) AV shunts → desaturated blood bypasses alveoli → worsens arterial hypoxemia. Pulmonary AV shunts = true shunt (V/Q = 0), not dead space (V/Q = infinity). True shunt: O2 supplementation does NOT fully correct SaO2. COMLEX: Distinguishing shunt vs. dead space vs. V/Q mismatch — a classic physiology question tested in environmental medicine context.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors: pharmacology and therapeutic role in acute mountain sickness","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"This is distinct from intracar","E":"This is distinct from intracar"}},{"id":"sdl36_q19","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — High-Altitude Cerebral Edema (HACE) — Mechanisms and Clinical Presentation","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old experienced mountaineer is found stumbling in his tent at 5,000 meters by teammates. He cannot identify his current location, is unable to name the current month, and falls when asked to perform heel-to-toe walking. His teammates report he had a severe headache and nausea the previous day but did not report them. Oxygen saturation is 71%. This presentation is consistent with high-altitude cerebral edema. Which of the following BEST describes the expected clinical progression of HACE if this patient is NOT immediately evacuated?","choices":{"A":"Worsening disorientation and ataxia → stupor and obtundation → coma → death from cerebral herniation or cardiorespiratory failure; deterioration is rapid once cerebral edema raises intracranial pressure","B":"Progression to focal motor deficits → aphasia → hemisensory loss → eventual recovery over weeks, following the pattern of a cerebellar or frontal lobe infarction rather than diffuse cerebral edema","C":"Spontaneous resolution within 24–48 hours without descent because the cerebrovascular autoregulation eventually restores normal intracranial pressure through compensatory CSF reabsorption, even in the absence of treatment","D":"Conversion to high-altitude pulmonary edema as the increased intracranial pressure activates a neurogenic pulmonary edema response, clearing the cerebral symptoms while producing new respiratory failure","E":"Plateau in neurological status after 48 hours of continued altitude exposure, as maximal cerebral vasodilation establishes a new steady-state equilibrium between edema formation and lymphatic reabsorption in the brain parenchyma"},"correct":"A","explanation":"HACE is a MEDICAL EMERGENCY. The clinical course without treatment: (1) AMS (headache, nausea, fatigue) at the earliest stage — failure to descend. (2) Disorientation and ataxia develop (early HACE — the findings in this vignette). (3) Progressive stupor and obtundation. (4) Coma. (5) Death from cerebral herniation (as cerebellar tonsils compress the brainstem) or cardiorespiratory failure from brainstem compression. This deterioration can occur over hours once established cerebral edema is present. The ataxia in HACE is typically truncal/gait ataxia from diffuse white matter edema and cerebellar involvement — distinct from focal cerebellar infarction. There is NO spontaneous resolution at altitude; the continued hypoxic stimulus continues to drive vasodilation and VEGF-mediated permeability. Immediate treatment: descent (definitive), dexamethasone (pharmacologic), portable hyperbaric bag if available.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HACE clinical progression: AMS → disorientation + ataxia → stupor → coma → death. No spontaneous resolution at altitude. Treatment: IMMEDIATE descent + dexamethasone (addresses vasogenic edema by ↓cytokine-mediated permeability). COMLEX: HACE = neurological altitude emergency; ataxia at altitude = must descend immediately — delay is fatal. Dexamethasone buys time for evacuation (SDL 38 Obj 2 link).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors: pharmacology and therapeutic role in acute mountain sickness","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The ataxia in HACE is typicall","C":"There is NO spontaneous resolu"}},{"id":"sdl36_q20","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — High-Altitude Cerebral Edema (HACE) — Mechanisms and Clinical Presentation","batch":2,"stem":"A wilderness medicine physician is educating a mountaineering expedition team before they ascend from base camp (3,200 m) to a high camp (5,100 m) over 2 days. She counsels them about the timing of acute mountain sickness development. Which of the following statements MOST accurately reflects the typical onset characteristics of AMS?","choices":{"A":"AMS symptoms begin immediately upon arrival at any new altitude above sea level, within the first 15–30 minutes, as cerebral vasodilation is near-instantaneous upon even modest decreases in arterial PO2","B":"AMS symptoms develop after a minimum of 5–7 days at any given altitude, as the adaptation mechanisms (EPO, 2,3-BPG, ventilatory acclimatization) must first be fully exhausted before hypoxic symptoms emerge","C":"AMS does not follow a predictable onset pattern; symptoms can appear at any point from 30 minutes to 6 months after altitude gain depending entirely on individual genetic variation in HIF-1α promoter polymorphisms","D":"Symptoms typically begin within hours to 2 days of arrival; headache usually comes first, followed by fatigue, nausea, dizziness, and poor sleep, because the cerebrovascular and intracranial pressure changes develop progressively","E":"AMS develops only during sleep, because nocturnal hypoxemia from periodic breathing (Cheyne-Stokes at altitude) is the primary mechanism of AMS headache; daytime symptoms represent misidentification of dehydration headache as altitude illness"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AMS onset characteristics per Guyton: symptoms begin within HOURS TO 2 DAYS of arriving at altitude above 8,000–9,000 feet (2,400–2,700 m). The delay (not immediate) occurs because the intracranial pressure changes require time to develop: hypoxia → cerebral vasodilation → ↑cerebral blood flow and blood volume → ↑ICP → stretching of pain-sensitive meningeal structures → headache. The same vasodilation responsible for AMS headache is the initiating step in HACE if progression continues. Guyton Ch 44 gives the same timing (a few hours up to about 2 days after ascent). Individual variation exists (some develop symptoms at lower altitudes, some are more resistant) but the general timing window is hours-to-2-days. Onset is NOT immediate (cerebral vascular changes take hours), NOT exclusively nocturnal (though periodic breathing worsens symptoms during sleep), and NOT weeks-to-months delayed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AMS onset = hours to 2 days after rapid ascent to >8,000–9,000 ft. NOT immediate, NOT weeks delayed. Headache = first symptom (from ↑ICP via cerebral vasodilation). AMS is the clinical spectrum that precedes HACE — failing to descend or treat AMS allows progression to HACE. COMLEX: Acetazolamide prophylaxis for AMS = take before ascent or at first symptoms. Mechanism: ↑renal HCO3− excretion → speeds acclimatization ventilatory response (SDL 38 Obj 2).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 2 — Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors: pharmacology and therapeutic role in acute mountain sickness","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Onset is NOT immediate (cerebr","B":"Onset is NOT immediate (cerebr","C":"Onset is NOT immediate (cerebr"}},{"id":"sdl36_q21","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — High-Altitude Cerebral Edema (HACE) — Mechanisms and Clinical Presentation","batch":2,"stem":"Two climbers arrive simultaneously at a base camp medical station at 4,600 meters. Climber A (age 31) has progressive dyspnea at rest, SpO2 = 54%, bibasilar crackles on auscultation, and pink frothy sputum. Climber B (age 38) presents with truncal ataxia, inability to perform tandem gait, disorientation, and SpO2 = 66%. Both started at sea level 3 days ago. Which of the following MOST accurately distinguishes the PRIMARY pathophysiologic mechanism of Climber A's condition from Climber B's?","choices":{"A":"Climber A has HACE with cerebral edema causing pulmonary vascular reflexes that produce bilateral crackles; Climber B has HAPE with pulmonary hypertension reflexively causing intracranial hypertension via the pulmonary-brain reflex arc","B":"Both climbers have the identical mechanism (uniform HPV raising capillary pressure), but Climber A's weaker chest wall musculature preferentially directed edema into the pulmonary space while Climber B's greater cerebrovascular sensitivity preferentially directed edema to the brain","C":"Climber A has cytotoxic pulmonary edema from direct alveolar hypoxic injury to type II pneumocytes; Climber B has cytotoxic cerebral edema from direct neuronal hypoxic injury — both are cytotoxic, not vasogenic","D":"Both conditions share identical VEGF-mediated permeability as the SOLE mechanism; the difference is entirely anatomic (pulmonary vs. cerebral endothelium), not mechanistic — the same VEGF signal produces clinically distinct syndromes based on organ location","E":"Climber A has unequal HPV forcing blood through under-constricted vessels at high pressure (HAPE); Climber B has hypoxic cerebral arteriolar dilation raising cerebral capillary pressure (HACE) — opposite vascular responses in two organ beds"},"correct":"E","explanation":"HAPE and HACE represent OPPOSITE vascular responses to altitude hypoxia in different organ beds — this is the key integrating concept: PULMONARY vascular response to hypoxia = VASOCONSTRICTION (HPV). This is the unique, opposite-to-systemic response of pulmonary vasculature. In HAPE, this vasoconstriction is SPATIALLY UNEQUAL → blood forced through under-constricted vessels → focal ↑capillary pressure → edema. CEREBRAL vascular response to hypoxia = VASODILATION (normal for systemic vasculature — a homeostatic response to increase oxygen delivery to the brain). In HACE, this vasodilation → ↑cerebral capillary pressure → vasogenic cerebral edema. Both are then amplified by the same VEGF/cytokine permeability mechanism. But the PRIMARY initiating mechanisms are opposite: PULMONARY = vasoconstriction (unequal HPV); CEREBRAL = vasodilation. This opposition is a classic board pearl. Both HAPE and HACE can coexist in severe altitude illness.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Key contrast — HAPE: unequal HPV (pulmonary vasoconstriction) → ↑pulmonary capillary pressure → non-cardiogenic edema. HACE: cerebral vasodilation → ↑cerebral capillary pressure → vasogenic edema. Opposite vascular responses! COMLEX: Both amplified by VEGF + cytokines. HAPE treatment: O2/nifedipine (reverse HPV). HACE treatment: descent + dexamethasone (suppress cytokine-mediated permeability). SDL 38 Obj 1 link: nifedipine = CCB used for HAPE via ↓HPV.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — Nitric oxide pharmacology, physiologic role; medications for pulmonary hypertension","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"But the PRIMARY initiating mec","D":"But the PRIMARY initiating mec"}},{"id":"sdl36_q22","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — High-Altitude Pulmonary Edema (HAPE) — Pathophysiology and Clinical Findings","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old skier (no cardiac history) develops HAPE after ascending to 3,800 m. A 62-year-old woman with known heart failure develops cardiogenic pulmonary edema after missing her diuretics. Both have bilateral infiltrates on chest X-ray and SpO2 of 78%. Which combination of findings would MOST reliably differentiate HAPE in the 34-year-old from cardiogenic pulmonary edema in the 62-year-old?","choices":{"A":"In HAPE: bilateral diffuse symmetric infiltrates with cardiomegaly; in cardiogenic edema: patchy unilateral infiltrates without cardiomegaly — because HAPE affects both lungs uniformly while cardiogenic edema reflects regional V/Q mismatch","B":"In HAPE: frothy pink sputum is pathognomonic and diagnostic of altitude-related pulmonary edema; in cardiogenic edema: sputum is white and non-frothy because the lower protein content of the cardiogenic transudate prevents foam formation at the alveolar surface","C":"In HAPE: serum BNP is normal; in cardiogenic edema: serum BNP is massively elevated — BNP measurement alone is sufficient to distinguish HAPE from cardiogenic pulmonary edema without the need for hemodynamic monitoring","D":"In HAPE: the edema responds to supplemental oxygen within 4–6 hours; in cardiogenic edema: the edema does NOT respond to supplemental oxygen because the fundamental mechanism (left heart failure) is unaffected by changes in alveolar PO2","E":"HAPE: normal PCWP with elevated pulmonary artery pressure, a pre-capillary pattern without left heart failure; cardiogenic edema: elevated PCWP, a post-capillary pattern from left ventricular dysfunction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The gold-standard distinction between HAPE and cardiogenic pulmonary edema is HEMODYNAMIC: HAPE: (1) Elevated PAP (from unequal HPV-driven arteriolar vasoconstriction); (2) NORMAL PCWP (First Aid 2025: normal < 12 mmHg) — the left heart is uninvolved; (3) Normal left ventricular function on echo; (4) Pre-capillary pulmonary hypertension pattern (elevated PVR = [mPAP − PCWP]/CO). Cardiogenic edema: (1) Elevated PAP; (2) ELEVATED PCWP — reflecting elevated left atrial pressure backing up into pulmonary veins; (3) Reduced EF or elevated filling pressures. The PCWP differential is the most reliable distinguisher. Note about the supplemental-oxygen option: supplemental O2 DOES improve oxygenation in BOTH conditions — it reverses HPV in HAPE (more dramatically) and reduces hypoxic vasoconstriction in cardiogenic edema too. BNP can be mildly elevated in HAPE from RV stretch, so it is not a reliable absolute discriminator.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HAPE vs. cardiogenic pulmonary edema key distinction: HAPE = normal PCWP + elevated PAP (pre-capillary pulmonary HTN). Cardiogenic = elevated PCWP + elevated PAP (post-capillary). CXR: HAPE = no cardiomegaly + patchy asymmetric infiltrates; cardiogenic = cardiomegaly + symmetric perihilar 'butterfly' infiltrates. COMLEX: Use PCWP as the hemodynamic discriminator; then treat HAPE with O2/nifedipine (SDL 38 Obj 1 — PH medications).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — Nitric oxide pharmacology, physiologic role; medications for pulmonary hypertension","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"BNP can be mildly elevated in "}},{"id":"sdl36_q23","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — High-Altitude Pulmonary Edema (HAPE) — Pathophysiology and Clinical Findings","batch":2,"stem":"Bronchoalveolar lavage studies from the 1990s initially described HAPE as purely hydrostatic (from elevated pulmonary capillary pressure). More recent research has demonstrated that VEGF concentrations and inflammatory cytokines are also elevated in BAL fluid even in the early stages of HAPE before significant hypoxemia develops. A physiology professor asks: 'What is the contribution of VEGF and inflammatory cytokines to the permeability component of HAPE, and does this change the classification from hydrostatic to inflammatory edema?'","choices":{"A":"VEGF and cytokines are epiphenomena secondary to alveolar hypoxia and do not contribute to the permeability of pulmonary endothelium; HAPE is exclusively a hydrostatic process and the elevated cytokines in BAL represent reactive surfactant protein release by type II pneumocytes, not endothelial permeability changes","B":"VEGF and cytokines completely reclassify HAPE as an inflammatory exudate (ARDS equivalent); the hydrostatic component is negligible and the primary mechanism is diffuse alveolar damage with neutrophil-mediated endothelial destruction, identical to sepsis-induced ARDS","C":"VEGF and cytokines disrupt pulmonary endothelial tight junctions and add a protein-rich permeability component to the primary hydrostatic edema of unequal HPV, so HAPE is best classified as a MIXED edema — hydrostatic and permeability-mediated","D":"VEGF causes apoptosis of pulmonary capillary endothelial cells, producing gaps in the capillary wall through which RBCs (not just fluid) extravasate; the frothy pink sputum in HAPE is therefore entirely from frank pulmonary hemorrhage rather than edema fluid","E":"VEGF increases pulmonary arterial smooth muscle contractility, amplifying HPV and raising PAP further; the cytokines function exclusively as vasoconstrictors rather than permeability-increasing agents in the pulmonary vasculature"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Modern understanding positions HAPE as a MIXED mechanism edema, not purely hydrostatic: (1) PRIMARY/INITIATING: Unequal HPV → regional ↑capillary hydrostatic pressure → Starling filtration exceeds reabsorption → edema begins. (2) AMPLIFIED by permeability component: Local alveolar and capillary hypoxia → HIF-1 → VEGF + IL-6, IL-8, TNF-α → disruption of pulmonary endothelial tight junctions → ↑permeability → protein-rich fluid added to the initial transudate. Evidence: BAL from early HAPE shows BOTH elevated protein AND elevated VEGF/cytokines. The permeability component explains: (a) Why the edema fluid has high protein content (not just a low-protein hydrostatic transudate); (b) Why dexamethasone has some role in HAPE treatment; (c) Why nifedipine (reducing HPV = addressing the hydrostatic component) does not provide 100% resolution. HAPE is NOT ARDS (no neutrophil-mediated diffuse alveolar damage, no sepsis, rapid reversal with O2 rather than weeks-long ARDS course).","boardPrep":"Step 1: HAPE = mixed edema — hydrostatic (unequal HPV → ↑cap pressure) + permeability (VEGF + cytokines → ↑endothelial permeability). NOT pure ARDS (no diffuse alveolar damage, no neutrophilic destruction, reverses rapidly with O2). BAL evidence: high protein + elevated VEGF + cytokines. Treatment addresses both: O2/nifedipine (hydrostatic component) + dexamethasone (permeability component). COMLEX: HAPE ≠ ARDS — distinguish by context (altitude vs. sepsis), timeline (hours vs. days), and reversibility.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — Nitric oxide pharmacology, physiologic role; medications for pulmonary hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q24","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — High-Altitude Pulmonary Edema (HAPE) — Pathophysiology and Clinical Findings","batch":2,"stem":"A medical student studying altitude physiology is asked to compare the mechanisms of HAPE and HACE for her physiology shelf exam. She writes: 'Both HAPE and HACE involve increased capillary hydrostatic pressure, and both are amplified by VEGF-mediated endothelial permeability.' Her professor then asks: 'Correct so far — but what is the KEY mechanistic difference in HOW capillary pressure becomes elevated in HAPE compared to HACE?'","choices":{"A":"Both HAPE and HACE use identical mechanisms — the only difference is the anatomic bed affected; the capillary pressure elevation in both is driven by the same hypoxia-induced increase in cardiac output that raises perfusion pressure globally across all organs","B":"In HAPE, left ventricular end-diastolic pressure elevation transmits backward through pulmonary veins to raise capillary pressure uniformly; in HACE, cerebrovascular autoregulation fails in the setting of hypocapnia, causing loss of arteriolar tone and passive pressure transmission from the systemic circulation","C":"In HAPE, the mechanism involves CO2 retention causing pulmonary arteriolar constriction; in HACE, the mechanism involves CO2 retention causing cerebral arteriolar constriction — both are therefore driven by CO2, but in different vascular beds","D":"In HAPE, direct hypoxic injury to pulmonary capillary endothelium disrupts the membrane barrier without any hydrostatic elevation; in HACE, baroreceptor reflex activation after hypotension diverts blood flow to the brain via sympathetic cerebrovascular dilation","E":"In HAPE, unequal HPV pushes blood into under-constricted pulmonary vessels, raising capillary pressure focally (the lung constricts to hypoxia); in HACE, hypoxia dilates cerebral arterioles, raising capillary pressure throughout the brain (the brain dilates)"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This is the CORE integrating concept for altitude physiology: HAPE and HACE both increase capillary hydrostatic pressure, but via OPPOSITE primary vascular mechanisms. PULMONARY vasculature has a UNIQUE response to hypoxia — it CONSTRICTS (hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction). In HAPE, this constriction is spatially non-uniform, forcing blood into under-constricted segments at high pressure. CEREBRAL vasculature has the NORMAL systemic response to hypoxia — it DILATES (to increase O2 delivery to the brain). In HACE, this dilation directly raises cerebral capillary pressure. So: HAPE capillary pressure ↑ via ARTERIAL CONSTRICTION elsewhere forcing flow through unconstricted zones; HACE capillary pressure ↑ via DIRECT ARTERIOLAR DILATION increasing flow into the capillary bed. Both processes are then amplified by VEGF-mediated endothelial permeability. This opposite-mechanism insight explains why nifedipine (CCB blocking HPV) helps HAPE but NOT HACE, while dexamethasone helps HACE more than HAPE.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HAPE vs. HACE primary capillary pressure mechanism: HAPE = unequal VASOCONSTRICTION (HPV) forces blood into under-constricted zones; HACE = cerebral VASODILATION directly raises capillary pressure. Opposite vascular responses! Treatment difference: nifedipine (CCB) reverses HPV → helps HAPE; dexamethasone reduces cytokine-mediated edema → helps HACE. COMLEX: Integration question — understanding that pulmonary and cerebral vasculatures respond OPPOSITELY to hypoxia explains both syndromes and their treatments.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — Nitric oxide pharmacology, physiologic role; medications for pulmonary hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q25","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Physiological Adaptations of High-Altitude Natives","batch":2,"stem":"Anthropometric studies consistently show that lifelong high-altitude Andean natives have significantly greater chest circumferences and total lung capacities than both (a) sea-level controls and (b) lowlanders who have resided at altitude for 10–20 years. Adults who migrate to altitude — regardless of duration of residence — never achieve the same chest size as native-born residents. Which of the following BEST accounts for why only those raised from infancy at altitude develop these structural differences?","choices":{"A":"Thoracic cage and lung parenchymal growth occur during a critical window from infancy through adolescence; sustained hypoxia acting during these years remodels the growing chest wall, lungs, and heart, whereas adults have completed skeletal maturation and cannot remodel equivalently","B":"Altitude-specific genetic variants (EPAS1 on chromosome 2p21) are exclusively found in Andean populations and directly code for increased chest wall skeletal muscle fiber diameter regardless of age of exposure; lowland migrants with different EPAS1 alleles cannot develop these structural changes","C":"Native-born altitude residents benefit from maternal respiratory alkalosis during fetal development that causes in-utero lung volume expansion through the Hering-Breuer inflation reflex; this fetal lung expansion is irreversible and explains all post-natal chest structural differences","D":"Adult residents at altitude do develop the same structural chest changes as native-born residents, but these changes take more than 20 years to become anthropometrically measurable; studies showing no difference at 10–20 years simply lack sufficient duration of follow-up","E":"High-altitude diet in native populations is rich in micronutrients (particularly selenium and zinc) that specifically stimulate chondrocyte proliferation in costal cartilage; the chest expansion is therefore primarily nutritional and occurs regardless of altitude exposure timing"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guyton explicitly states that the enlarged chest and lung volumes in high-altitude natives begin developing in INFANCY — during the critical period when hypoxic stimuli act on actively growing skeletal and pulmonary tissue. The mechanism: sustained hypoxia → HIF-1 targets → increased ventilatory drive → ↑tidal volume and ventilatory frequency during a period of active chest wall growth → mechanical stretching drives rib cage, costal cartilage, and lung parenchymal expansion. Once growth plates close and lung parenchymal growth is complete, the same hypoxic stimulus cannot drive equivalent structural remodeling in adults. Adults achieve physiologic acclimatization (hematologic, ventilatory) but not the structural chest expansion. The evidence from the question that lowland-parentage children raised from birth at altitude show greater chest development than their adult-migrant parents — with the SAME genetic background — conclusively demonstrates the developmental/environmental nature of this adaptation, not purely genetic determinism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Native altitude structural adaptations require childhood exposure during active bone/lung growth. Adult migrants can achieve physiologic acclimatization (↑Hb, ↑ventilation, ↑2,3-BPG) but NOT the structural adaptations (barrel chest, ↑FVC). The structural adaptations explain why natives achieve 87% vs. acclimatized lowlanders' 68% of sea-level work capacity. COMLEX: HIF-1 during development → structural target gene expression → organomegaly (chest, heart); same HIF pathway (SDL 35 Obj 1) operating during a critical window.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 1 — HIF pathway: PHD/VHL mechanism and transcriptional targets of hypoxia","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The evidence from the question"}},{"id":"sdl36_q26","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Physiological Adaptations of High-Altitude Natives","batch":2,"stem":"A research team measures mixed venous PO2 (PvO2) at rest in high-altitude Andean natives (PaO2 ~40 mmHg) and in sea-level subjects (PaO2 ~95 mmHg). Despite the dramatically lower arterial PO2 in natives, the arterial-venous PO2 difference is remarkably preserved. Specifically, how does the resting venous PO2 of high-altitude natives compare to that of sea-level subjects, and what does this reveal about tissue oxygen extraction in natives?","choices":{"A":"The venous PO2 of high-altitude natives is equal to that of sea-level subjects; native adaptations completely normalize tissue oxygen delivery so that the same amount of oxygen is extracted per unit blood flow, producing identical venous PO2 despite dramatically different arterial PO2 levels","B":"The venous PO2 of high-altitude natives is 50 mmHg lower than that of sea-level subjects, indicating severe chronic tissue hypoxia with near-complete oxygen extraction; the fact that natives survive confirms that cells adapted to function at near-zero oxygen tension over generations","C":"The venous PO2 of natives is only about 15 mmHg lower than that of sea-level subjects; despite a PaO2 of 40 mmHg (vs. 95 mmHg), tissue O2 extraction is well maintained, largely because the elevated hemoglobin concentration preserves total O2 delivery","D":"The venous PO2 of high-altitude natives is 30–35 mmHg lower than that of sea-level subjects, confirming substantially impaired oxygen delivery; the high work capacity of natives is explained by anaerobic glycolysis, not preserved aerobic oxygen delivery","E":"The venous PO2 of high-altitude natives is higher than that of sea-level subjects because elevated 2,3-BPG causes complete rightward ODC shift that releases oxygen so efficiently that very little is retained in hemoglobin after passage through systemic capillaries"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Figure 44.2 in Guyton provides this specific comparison. Sea-level subjects: PaO2 ≈ 95 mmHg, PvO2 ≈ 40 mmHg (A-V difference ≈ 55 mmHg). High-altitude natives at 4,200 m: PaO2 ≈ 40 mmHg (dramatically reduced), PvO2 ≈ 25 mmHg. So the venous PO2 of natives is only ~15 mmHg less than sea-level subjects' venous PO2 (25 vs. 40 mmHg). This means: despite starting with a PaO2 of only 40 mmHg (the sea-level venous PO2!), the natives' tissues are adequately supplied and extract a similar absolute amount of oxygen as sea-level tissues. How? Elevated Hb (20 g/dL) provides sufficient TOTAL oxygen content despite low PaO2, maintaining DO2 (delivery = CO × CaO2). The 15-mmHg difference in PvO2 (not 50-60 mmHg) demonstrates that tissues are not in severe chronic oxygen debt.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Native altitude PvO2 ≈ 25 mmHg vs. sea-level PvO2 ≈ 40 mmHg — only 15 mmHg difference. Despite PaO2 = 40 mmHg (the sea-level VENOUS value!), natives maintain adequate O2 delivery via elevated Hb. This shows: DO2 = CO × CaO2 is preserved by ↑Hb compensating for ↓PaO2. COMLEX: O2 delivery equation — both CO and CaO2 matter. Hb = dominant variable; PaO2 alone does not determine O2 delivery.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 1 — HIF pathway: PHD/VHL mechanism and transcriptional targets of hypoxia","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"So the venous PO2 of natives i","B":"The 15-mmHg difference in PvO2"}},{"id":"sdl36_q27","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Physiological Adaptations of High-Altitude Natives","batch":2,"stem":"A sports medicine researcher explains why high-altitude Andean natives can perform maximum aerobic work at 87% of sea-level capacity while unacclimatized lowlanders can only achieve 50% at the same altitude. The researcher identifies two primary physiologic adaptations in natives — one cardiovascular and one hematologic — as the core mechanisms underlying their superior aerobic performance. Which combination BEST describes these two adaptations?","choices":{"A":"Blunted hypoxic ventilatory response (HVR) reducing wasted respiratory muscle work, combined with a persistent fetal hemoglobin (HbF) component providing leftward ODC shift at high altitude PO2 values — together maximizing hemoglobin loading efficiency at low PaO2","B":"Increased resting and maximum cardiac output from birth (enlarged heart, greater stroke volume capacity) combined with elevated hemoglobin concentration (~20 g/dL) — together maintaining oxygen delivery (DO2 = CO × CaO2) well above what low-altitude visitors can achieve despite the reduced arterial PO2","C":"Preferential reliance on anaerobic glycolysis for ATP production (bypassing oxidative phosphorylation), combined with greatly enhanced lactic acid buffering capacity from ↑carbonic anhydrase activity in muscle — together allowing sustained high-intensity work without aerobic constraint","D":"Increased adrenal cortical output producing elevated aldosterone and cortisol levels that maintain plasma volume during exercise, combined with reduced red blood cell 2,3-BPG (leftward ODC shift) that maximizes arterial O2 loading at PaO2 = 40 mmHg","E":"Enhanced cerebral autoregulation protecting the CNS from hypoxic impairment during exercise, combined with increased sympathetic activation of skeletal muscle mitochondrial biogenesis — providing more mitochondria per muscle fiber to extract oxygen from the lower tissue PO2 gradient"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The two dominant mechanisms underlying native altitude aerobic superiority per Guyton: (1) ELEVATED HEMOGLOBIN (~20 g/dL vs. 15 g/dL at sea level) — EPO-driven, HIF-1-mediated. Even at PaO2 = 40 mmHg, CaO2 ≈ 19.7 mL/dL (matching sea-level controls at 19.8 mL/dL). This maintains the CaO2 term in DO2. (2) GREATER CARDIAC OUTPUT FROM BIRTH — larger hearts, higher stroke volumes, greater maximum CO during exercise. This maintains the CO term in DO2. Together: DO2 = CO × CaO2 is preserved despite reduced PaO2. Why acclimatized lowlanders cannot match this: they can increase Hb to 18–20 g/dL (hematologic), but they cannot achieve the same cardiac output (no enlarged heart from birth) or the same ↑chest wall mechanical advantage. The native's larger lungs also mean less work of breathing. Blunted HVR and HbF do not explain this aerobic advantage.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Native altitude aerobic superiority = ↑CO (from birth) + ↑Hb (EPO/HIF) → preserved DO2 = CO × CaO2 despite low PaO2. This explains 87% vs. 68% (acclimatized) vs. 50% (unacclimatized) work capacity. COMLEX: Both cardiac and hematologic components contribute — cardiac output AND O2 content must both be considered in oxygen delivery physiology questions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 1 — HIF pathway: PHD/VHL mechanism and transcriptional targets of hypoxia","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Blunted HVR and HbF do not exp"}},{"id":"sdl36_q28","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — G-Force Effects, Baroreceptor Reflex, and Microgravity Physiology","batch":2,"stem":"During centrifuge testing at an aviation medicine training facility, an untrained volunteer experiences a progressive sequence: peripheral vision loss (grayout), then complete visual loss while remaining conscious (blackout), then loss of consciousness (G-LOC). At which G-force level does frank loss of consciousness (G-LOC) typically occur in untrained individuals without anti-G suits or trained straining maneuvers?","choices":{"A":"Greater than +1.5G; any G-force above normal resting gravity immediately exceeds the baroreceptor reflex compensation capacity of untrained subjects, causing loss of consciousness proportional to the degree of G-force excess above baseline","B":"Greater than +3G; this is the threshold at which baroreceptor reflexes are overwhelmed and ventricular fibrillation supervenes from coronary hypoperfusion, causing cardiac arrest rather than neurogenic loss of consciousness","C":"Greater than +4–6G in untrained individuals; below this the baroreceptor reflex partially compensates (as at +3.3G), but above it blood pooling drops cerebral perfusion below the level needed for consciousness despite maximal sympathetic activation","D":"Exactly +8G for all individuals regardless of training status; G-LOC threshold is fixed at +8G because this is the point where coronary perfusion pressure falls to zero and myocardial ischemia acutely impairs cardiac output, not baroreceptor failure","E":"Greater than +10G; the human cardiovascular system is capable of tolerating up to +10G for brief periods without loss of consciousness due to the large reserve capacity of the baroreceptor reflex and the protective effect of the spine compressing abdominal veins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The G-LOC threshold per Guyton: untrained individuals without protective equipment: +4–6G. At +3.3G (Figure 44.3), baroreceptors activate and partially restore pressure to 55/20 mmHg — consciousness is maintained. Above +4–6G, gravitational blood pooling overwhelms baroreceptor compensation: (1) Blood pools so massively in lower extremities that venous return to the heart approaches zero. (2) Cardiac output falls to near zero. (3) Cerebral perfusion pressure (ABP at head level − ICP) falls too low to sustain consciousness. (4) Guyton: acceleration greater than 4 to 6 G causes blackout of vision within a few seconds and unconsciousness shortly thereafter. Physical limits: at ~20G, vertebral compressive fracture occurs from axial loading. Anti-G suits delay G-LOC by compressing lower extremity veins → improving venous return. Trained G-straining maneuvers (Valsalva-like) increase intrathoracic pressure → maintain venous return → extend tolerance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: G-LOC threshold: untrained pilots = +4–6G (baroreceptors overwhelmed). Compare with +3.3G (partial compensation to 55/20 mmHg, consciousness maintained). Anti-G suit extends tolerance via ↑venous return. ~20G = vertebral fracture threshold. COMLEX: G-force physiology = cardiovascular regulatory limits test — baroreceptor reflex can compensate partially (as seen at +3.3G) but is overwhelmed at higher levels.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 1 — Pulmonary barotrauma, decompression sickness, and nitrogen narcosis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl36_q29","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — G-Force Effects, Baroreceptor Reflex, and Microgravity Physiology","batch":2,"stem":"An aerobatic pilot performing an outside loop maneuver pushes into −3G (negative G-force, foot-to-head direction). She reports severe facial pressure, facial petechiae, and a red haze filling her entire visual field (redout). She does not lose consciousness during the brief maneuver. Which of the following BEST explains both the redout phenomenon AND why she does not immediately develop life-threatening cerebral hemorrhage at this level of negative G?","choices":{"A":"Negative G displaces blood toward the head, raising intracranial pressure and engorging retinal veins (redout); cerebrospinal fluid in the subarachnoid space supplies a counterpressure that partly buffers transmural pressure across intracranial vessel walls during brief exposure","B":"Negative G activates carotid baroreceptors by raising carotid sinus pressure, triggering an immediate reflex parasympathetic response that constricts cerebral vessels to protect against cerebral hyperemia; the redout occurs because retinal veins are outside the blood-brain barrier and cannot be protected by this reflex","C":"Negative G produces cerebral edema from acute blood-brain barrier disruption caused by the sudden rise in intracranial capillary pressure; the redout represents choroidal effusion visualized through the retina, and the edema is limited by aquaporin-4 water channel activation on astrocytes","D":"Negative G causes the eyelids to be mechanically forced upward, exposing the inferior conjunctiva to air turbulence; the subsequent conjunctival hyperemia produces the red visual field as reflected light from the engorged conjunctiva reaches the macula, with no actual intracranial vascular changes","E":"Negative G completely reverses the normal baroreceptor reflex, causing paradoxical sympathetic activation and extreme hypertension; the cerebral vessels are protected from rupture by a Cushing response (bradycardia + hypertension) that maintains normal cerebral perfusion pressure despite the external negative G force"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Negative G (foot-to-head direction) produces the OPPOSITE of positive G: blood is displaced superiorly into the head, thorax, and face. The effects: (1) Facial hyperemia and petechiae from superficial venous engorgement. (2) REDOUT — blood floods into the retinal and choroidal vasculature, distending thin-walled retinal veins; the red color visualized by the pilot is the retinal/choroidal vascular congestion. (3) Elevated intracranial pressure from venous engorgement in the cranial vault. WHY NOT IMMEDIATE HEMORRHAGE: Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) bathes the brain in the subarachnoid space. As intracranial venous pressure rises, CSF can be displaced (via foramen of Luschka/Magendie into the spinal canal) and provides a counterbalancing hydraulic pressure on the outside of cerebral vessels, reducing transmural vessel wall stress. This protective buffering allows brief negative G exposure without rupture. Guyton notes outside loops up to −4 to −5 G usually cause no permanent harm; at more extreme or sustained negative G this protection is overwhelmed and serious injury (cerebral hemorrhage, retinal hemorrhage) can occur.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Negative G → blood displaced to head → ↑ICP → retinal vein engorgement → REDOUT. CSF provides partial protective counterpressure against vessel rupture. Positive G → blood to lower body → ↓cerebral perfusion → GRAYOUT/BLACKOUT. Redout = retinal venous congestion (not arterial spasm). COMLEX: G-force directions: positive G (head-to-foot) = pilots pulling out of dive; negative G (foot-to-head) = pilots pushing over top of loop.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 1 — Pulmonary barotrauma, decompression sickness, and nitrogen narcosis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl36_q30","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — G-Force Effects, Baroreceptor Reflex, and Microgravity Physiology","batch":2,"stem":"Astronauts returning to Earth after 6 months aboard the International Space Station consistently experience dizziness, presyncope, and syncope upon standing — symptoms that resolve over 1–4 weeks after return. Which constellation of physiologic changes acquired during prolonged microgravity exposure BEST explains the orthostatic intolerance seen upon return to Earth's gravitational environment?","choices":{"A":"Increased total blood volume from fluid retention due to chronic ADH elevation in microgravity, combined with hyperactive baroreceptor reflexes causing excessive vasodilation on return — producing a hyperdynamic circulation that paradoxically reduces organ perfusion through a steal phenomenon","B":"Decreased total blood volume (cephalad fluid shift → renin-angiotensin suppression → renal salt and water loss), decreased red cell mass, blunted baroreceptor sensitivity, and skeletal muscle deconditioning — together preventing compensation when gravitational pooling resumes upright","C":"Hypertrophy of leg musculature from increased muscle activity required to move in microgravity, combined with ↑RBC mass from chronic hypoxia in the International Space Station atmosphere — producing hyperviscosity that impairs venous return upon return to gravity","D":"Permanent vestibular dysfunction from fluid shifts within the semicircular canals during microgravity causing chronic spatial disorientation, combined with cortisol deficiency from adrenal atrophy secondary to weightlessness — producing orthostatic hypotension from both neurologic and endocrine causes","E":"Pulmonary fibrosis from elevated O2 concentration in the spacecraft atmosphere, combined with diaphragmatic hypertrophy from altered breathing mechanics in microgravity — producing restrictive lung disease that reduces cardiac output and causes exercise intolerance on return to Earth"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Prolonged microgravity produces a predictable constellation of maladaptive changes: (1) BLOOD VOLUME REDUCTION: In microgravity, lack of gravitational pooling shifts blood/fluid TOWARD the head and thorax. The brain interprets this as volume excess → suppresses ADH → suppresses renin-angiotensin → ↑renal diuresis → total blood volume decreases. (2) RBC MASS DECREASE: Reduced EPO (hypoxia-unrelated, from altered erythropoietic regulation in microgravity), phlebotomy for medical monitoring, and 'space anemia' → ↓RBC mass. (3) BARORECEPTOR IMPAIRMENT: Chronic absence of gravitational orthostatic challenges → baroreceptors become less sensitive; on return, they cannot rapidly respond to sudden blood pooling. (4) MUSCLE DECONDITIONING: ↓skeletal muscle mass reduces the muscle venous pump that normally assists venous return on standing. On return to Earth: gravity → immediate blood pooling in lower extremities → ↓venous return → ↓CO → ↓cerebral perfusion → presyncope/syncope. Treatment: counterpressure garments, fluid loading, gradual reconditioning.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Microgravity effects: ↓blood volume (fluid diuresis from cephalad shift), ↓RBC, impaired baroreceptors, ↓skeletal muscle → orthostatic intolerance on return. Countermeasures: fluid loading, pressurized garments, exercise during spaceflight. COMLEX: Environmental extremes physiology — same baroreceptor reflex dysfunction that causes orthostatic hypotension in bed-bound patients also occurs in astronauts (deconditioning → impaired cardiovascular reflexes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 1 — Pulmonary barotrauma, decompression sickness, and nitrogen narcosis","isHighYield":false}],"37":[{"id":"sdl37_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Pulmonary Barotrauma, Decompression Sickness, and Nitrogen Narcosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 32-year-old recreational scuba diver breathing compressed air at 150 feet for 90 minutes develops lightheadedness, euphoria, and impaired judgment. He ignores his dive partner's warning signals and continues descending. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of his symptoms?","choices":{"A":"CO2 accumulation in the alveoli causing cerebral vasodilation and elevated intracranial pressure","B":"Hemoglobin saturation with dissolved nitrogen reducing its oxygen-carrying capacity","C":"Nitrogen dissolving into neuronal lipid membranes and reducing neuronal excitability","D":"Increased ambient pressure causing direct mechanical compression of cerebral cortical tissue","E":"Alveolar oxygen depletion from nitrogen displacement at high partial pressures"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Nitrogen narcosis results from nitrogen dissolving into the lipid bilayers of neuronal membranes, altering ionic conductance in a manner analogous to other gas anesthetics (e.g., halothane). This physical effect reduces neuronal excitability and produces a clinical syndrome resembling alcohol intoxication ('raptures of the deep'). Symptoms begin at ~120 feet (light-headedness, euphoria, impaired judgment) and progress to drowsiness at 150–200 feet and severe incapacitation beyond 250 feet. CO2 narcosis is a separate entity caused by rebreathing apparatus failure, not depth alone. Nitrogen does not bind hemoglobin. The narcotic effect is entirely reversible upon ascent to shallower depth. Ambient pressure does not mechanically squeeze the brain; Guyton attributes the narcosis to nitrogen dissolving in neuronal membranes like a gas anesthetic.","boardPrep":"First Aid p.691 — decompression sickness (caisson disease). Guyton Ch. 45 — nitrogen narcosis: onset ~120 ft, mechanism = lipid membrane dissolution, 'raptures of the deep'; reverses within minutes of ascent. Helium used in deep dives has ~1/5 the narcotic potency of nitrogen.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 3 — Henry's law: dissolved gas rises with partial pressure","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CO2 narcosis is a separate ent","B":"Nitrogen does not bind hemoglo","D":"Ambient pressure does not mech"}},{"id":"sdl37_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Pulmonary Barotrauma, Decompression Sickness, and Nitrogen Narcosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old professional diver surfaces rapidly after a 200-foot, 90-minute compressed-air dive without following the prescribed decompression schedule. Approximately 30 minutes after surfacing, he develops severe bilateral knee and hip pain. What percentage of patients with decompression sickness experience this musculoskeletal presentation?","choices":{"A":"5–10%","B":"20–30%","C":"50–60%","D":"85–90%","E":"95–99%"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Joint and muscle pain — the classic 'bends' — is the most common manifestation of decompression sickness, occurring in 85–90% of affected divers. The term 'bends' refers to the characteristic flexed posture adopted to relieve the pain. Neurological symptoms (dizziness, weakness, paralysis, or collapse) occur in 5–10%, and 'the chokes' — pulmonary capillary microbubble obstruction causing severe dyspnea, pulmonary edema, and occasionally death — accounts for approximately 2% of cases. Nitrogen bubbles form when dissolved N2 comes out of solution faster than it can be eliminated via the lungs on rapid ascent.","boardPrep":"First Aid p.691 — decompression sickness: N2 bubble formation on rapid ascent; treat with hyperbaric O2 (recompression). Guyton Ch. 45 — bends = 85–90%; neurological DCS = 5–10%; 'chokes' (pulmonary DCS) = ~2% → severe dyspnea, pulmonary edema, potential death.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — the partial pressure gradients for O2, CO2, and N2","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Pulmonary Barotrauma, Decompression Sickness, and Nitrogen Narcosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old navy trainee escaping from a submerged submarine at 150 feet ascends rapidly to the surface without exhaling. Immediately upon surfacing, he develops sudden chest pain, hemoptysis, and right-sided focal neurological deficits. Which pathophysiological mechanism is most directly responsible?","choices":{"A":"Rapid decompression triggers massive nitrogen bubble formation throughout joint spaces","B":"Expanding lung gas ruptures pulmonary vessels during ascent, causing arterial air embolism","C":"Nitrogen narcosis causes cerebrovascular spasm and ischemic stroke","D":"Boyle's law-mediated lung collapse due to failure of pressure equalization valves in diving gear","E":"CO2 toxicity from rebreathing apparatus dead-space accumulation during the ascent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"During rapid ascent, gases in the lungs expand in accordance with Boyle's law (V ∝ 1/P). If the diver does not exhale continuously, expanding air can rupture pulmonary capillaries and enter the circulation, causing arterial gas embolism. Gas emboli traveling to the CNS produce focal neurological deficits. This is pulmonary barotrauma — distinct from decompression sickness, which results from dissolved N2 bubble formation. The key distinction: barotrauma occurs during ascent from breath-holding or failure to exhale; DCS results from dissolved nitrogen supersaturation in tissues after prolonged deep exposure.","boardPrep":"First Aid p.691 — air emboli: 'can be iatrogenic 2° to invasive procedures (e.g., central line placement)' — same pathophysiology as diving ascent. Guyton Ch. 45 — submarine escape: must exhale continuously during ascent to prevent pulmonary gas expansion → vessel rupture → arterial air embolism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 → Obj 2 — pneumothorax, another consequence of air escaping ruptured alveoli","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Hyperbaric Oxygen (HBO) Therapy — Rationale and Mechanism of Action","batch":1,"stem":"A 67-year-old diabetic farmer sustains a deep laceration to his right thigh and presents 3 days later with a crepitant, foul-smelling wound extending proximally. Gram stain of wound exudate shows large gram-positive rods. Hyperbaric oxygen therapy is initiated emergently alongside surgical debridement. Which of the following best explains HBO's mechanism of action in this condition?","choices":{"A":"HBO displaces CO from hemoglobin, restoring oxyhemoglobin to ischemic wound tissue","B":"HBO raises tissue PO2 above 70 mmHg, where oxidizing free radicals stop Clostridial growth","C":"High-pressure O2 reverses osmotic tissue edema by creating a hydrostatic counter-gradient","D":"HBO stimulates CD8+ T-lymphocyte cytotoxic activity against Clostridial surface antigens","E":"HBO suppresses Clostridial exotoxin binding by competitive antagonism at host cell receptors"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Clostridial organisms (C. perfringens, C. septicum) are obligate anaerobes that grow optimally in the absence of oxygen. HBO raises tissue PO2 to levels at which Clostridial growth is inhibited — specifically, PO2 >70 mmHg stops the infectious process by overwhelming bacterial antioxidant defenses via reactive oxygen species (superoxide, H2O2). This converts a condition formerly almost 100% fatal into one curable with early HBO combined with surgical debridement. CO poisoning is a separate indication for HBO. The mechanism of HBO in gas gangrene is ROS-mediated bactericidal effect, not immunological or osmotic.","boardPrep":"Guyton Ch. 45 — HBO indications: gas gangrene (Clostridium — obligate anaerobe inhibited at PO2 >70 mmHg), decompression sickness, CO poisoning, osteomyelitis, myocardial infarction, arterial gas embolism. Administered at 2–3 atm via mask/ETT. First Aid p.691 — decompression sickness: treat with hyperbaric O2.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 12 — the value of oxygen therapy in different types of hypoxia","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CO poisoning is a separate ind","C":"The mechanism of HBO in gas ga","D":"The mechanism of HBO in gas ga"}},{"id":"sdl37_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Hyperbaric Oxygen (HBO) Therapy — Rationale and Mechanism of Action","batch":1,"stem":"A 44-year-old man is brought to the ED after being found unconscious in a garage with the car running. His carboxyhemoglobin (COHb) is 38%. After intubation with normobaric 100% O2 via mechanical ventilator, his COHb falls to 28% over 90 minutes. He is transferred to the hyperbaric chamber. Which of the following correctly explains why HBO accelerates CO elimination beyond what normobaric O2 achieves?","choices":{"A":"HBO reduces cerebral blood flow, limiting CO-mediated vasogenic edema in the brain","B":"Superoxide dismutase activated by HBO directly metabolizes CO to CO2 in neurons","C":"At 2–3 atm, high dissolved plasma O2 displaces CO from hemoglobin far faster by mass action","D":"HBO increases the respiratory rate by stimulating peripheral chemoreceptors, accelerating pulmonary CO excretion","E":"HBO volatilizes CO at high ambient pressure, allowing it to exit through the skin rather than the lungs"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Carbon monoxide binds hemoglobin with about 250 times greater affinity than oxygen (Guyton Ch 41; First Aid 2025: > 200 times). At normobaric PO2, displacement is slow. HBO at 2–3 atm elevates alveolar PO2 to 1,500–2,280 mmHg, creating an enormous dissolved O2 pool in plasma (Henry's law: dissolved O2 ∝ PO2). This elevated dissolved O2 provides an overwhelming competitive mass-action effect at the heme binding site, accelerating CO displacement. HBO also helps prevent CO binding to cytochrome c oxidase in mitochondria. HBO is used for severe CO poisoning; Katzung (Ch 56) recommends considering it when COHb exceeds 50% and particularly in pregnancy, and First Aid 2025 lists it \"if severe.\" The benefit is not reduced brain blood flow, superoxide dismutase acts on superoxide radicals rather than on CO, and CO does not leave through the skin; high O2 pressure works by displacing CO from hemoglobin, which it otherwise holds about 250 times as tightly as O2.","boardPrep":"Guyton Ch. 45 — HBO at 2–3 atm PO2 via mask or ETT; surrounding chamber pressurized with compressed air. First Aid p.691 — HBO for decompression sickness. Key board indication: CO poisoning — HBO displaces CO from hemoglobin; also: gas gangrene, DCS, osteomyelitis, arterial gas embolism, myocardial infarction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 11 — carbon monoxide's effects on hemoglobin and O2-carrying capacity","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"The benefit is not reduced bra"}},{"id":"sdl37_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Hyperbaric Oxygen (HBO) Therapy — Rationale and Mechanism of Action","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the standard technique of administering hyperbaric oxygen therapy in clinical practice?","choices":{"A":"Patients breathe 100% O2 at 1 atm in a sealed chamber filled with pure oxygen","B":"O2 is administered at 1 atm after placing the patient in a negative-pressure (iron lung) chamber","C":"O2 is delivered via nasal cannula at 6 L/min inside a chamber pressurized to 4 atm with pure oxygen","D":"Patients breathe room air at 3 atm; pure O2 is infused directly into the bloodstream via IV","E":"O2 at 2–3 atm via mask or endotracheal tube inside a chamber of air compressed to the same pressure"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In clinical HBO, the patient breathes O2 at a PO2 of 2–3 atm via mask or endotracheal tube, while the surrounding chamber atmosphere is compressed air at the same pressure level. This configuration prevents the fire hazard of a pure-oxygen chamber while still delivering the therapeutic high PO2. The critical distinction: the patient's airway receives O2 at 2–3 atm, but the chamber itself contains air — separating the inspired gas from the ambient environment. Pure-oxygen chambers (monoplace) exist but introduce significant explosion risk. The elevated PO2 markedly increases dissolved O2 in plasma, reaching tissues that standard Hb-O2 transport cannot adequately perfuse.","boardPrep":"Guyton Ch. 45 — HBO: O2 at 2–3 atm PO2 via mask or intratracheal tube; surrounding gas = normal air compressed to same pressure level. Indications: gas gangrene, DCS, CO poisoning, osteomyelitis, arterial gas embolism, myocardial infarction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — oxygen transport principles, including dissolved versus hemoglobin-bound O2","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Oxygen Toxicity — Excessive Intracellular Oxidation and CNS Susceptibility","batch":1,"stem":"A diver breathing 100% oxygen at 4 atm pressure develops nausea, muscle twitching, and visual disturbances. Within 25 minutes, he develops a generalized tonic-clonic seizure. His instructor notes that he was exercising vigorously at the time. Which of the following best explains why exercise increases susceptibility to CNS oxygen toxicity?","choices":{"A":"Higher O2 consumption during exercise generates free radicals faster than SOD and catalase can clear them","B":"Exercise-induced hypercapnia potentiates CNS oxygen toxicity through respiratory acidosis and CO2-mediated vasoconstriction","C":"Higher cardiac output during exercise delivers proportionally more dissolved O2 to the CNS per minute","D":"Lactic acidosis from anaerobic exercise independently lowers the neuronal seizure threshold","E":"Exercise-induced hyperthermia directly sensitizes neuronal membrane phospholipids to free radical peroxidation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"At extreme PO2 (>2 atm), the hemoglobin-O2 buffering mechanism fails and tissue PO2 rises to hundreds or thousands of mmHg. This drives the continuous formation of oxidizing free radicals (O2−, H2O2). During exercise, mitochondrial O2 consumption increases markedly, and the rate of free radical generation through partial O2 reduction rises proportionally. This faster generation rate overwhelms antioxidant enzymes (SOD, catalase, peroxidase) more quickly than at rest, precipitating acute CNS toxicity with shorter latency. Symptoms before seizure: nausea, muscle twitching, dizziness, visual disturbances, irritability, disorientation.","boardPrep":"Guyton Ch. 45 — acute O2 poisoning: 4 atm PO2 → seizures ± coma in 30–60 min; exercise greatly accelerates onset. Prodrome: nausea, muscle twitching, dizziness, visual disturbances, irritability, disorientation. Free radical mechanism: O2− and H2O2 overwhelm SOD/catalase/peroxidase.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — the acute CNS effects of hypoxia, the low-PO2 counterpart of oxygen toxicity","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl37_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Oxygen Toxicity — Excessive Intracellular Oxidation and CNS Susceptibility","batch":1,"stem":"At sea level, breathing ambient air, the hemoglobin-O2 buffering mechanism maintains tissue PO2 at approximately 40 mmHg. A diver is now breathing 100% O2 at 3 atm (alveolar PO2 ≈ 2,280 mmHg). What is the expected fate of tissue PO2 under these conditions?","choices":{"A":"Tissue PO2 remains at ~40 mmHg because hemoglobin remains fully saturated and continues to buffer O2 delivery","B":"Tissue PO2 falls to near zero because extreme PO2 inhibits mitochondrial cytochrome c oxidase","C":"Tissue PO2 rises modestly to ~100 mmHg because the Bohr effect right-shifts the O2-Hb dissociation curve","D":"Tissue PO2 remains normal because alveolar type II pneumocytes downregulate O2 absorption at supraphysiologic PO2","E":"Tissue PO2 rises to hundreds or thousands of mmHg because dissolved O2 overwhelms the hemoglobin buffering capacity"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Under normal conditions (alveolar PO2 <120 mmHg), hemoglobin's sigmoid dissociation curve allows it to unload O2 to tissues and maintain tissue PO2 between 20–60 mmHg regardless of minor alveolar PO2 changes — the 'hemoglobin-O2 buffer' mechanism. However, above a critical alveolar PO2 (~2 atm, ≈1,520 mmHg), hemoglobin is fully saturated AND dissolved O2 in plasma rises markedly (Henry's law). At alveolar PO2 of 3,040 mmHg (4 atm), blood contains ~20 vol% O2 bound to Hb PLUS ~9 vol% dissolved — a total of ~29 vol%. As only ~5 vol% is consumed in tissues, venous blood leaving capillaries still has PO2 ~1,200 mmHg. The Hb buffer is overwhelmed, and tissue PO2 climbs dangerously.","boardPrep":"Guyton Ch. 45 — Hb-O2 buffer fails above ~2 atm PO2; at 4 atm: blood = ~20 vol% Hb-bound + ~9 vol% dissolved = ~29 vol%; venous tissue PO2 ~1,200 mmHg → toxic free radical accumulation. This is the mechanistic basis for CNS O2 toxicity in divers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — the partial pressure gradients that move O2 from alveoli to tissues","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl37_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Oxygen Toxicity — Excessive Intracellular Oxidation and CNS Susceptibility","batch":1,"stem":"Under normal tissue PO2 conditions, small amounts of oxygen free radicals form continuously from mitochondrial O2 reduction but are rapidly scavenged. Which of the following correctly describes the sequential enzymatic pathway that neutralizes superoxide (O2−)?","choices":{"A":"Peroxidases directly reduce O2− to water in a single enzymatic step without H2O2 intermediate","B":"Cytochrome P450 hydroxylates O2− for conjugation and urinary excretion","C":"Superoxide dismutase converts O2− → H2O2; catalase and peroxidases then degrade H2O2 → H2O + O2","D":"Glutathione reductase directly quenches O2− without generating any H2O2 intermediate","E":"Monoamine oxidase reduces O2− by coupling with NADH oxidation in neuronal mitochondria"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The primary antioxidant defense against superoxide proceeds in two enzymatic steps: (1) Superoxide dismutase (SOD) dismutates O2− to H2O2 (2 O2− + 2H+ → H2O2 + O2); (2) Catalase and/or glutathione peroxidase then break H2O2 into water and O2 (or H2O and GSSG). At normal tissue PO2 (~40 mmHg), this system rapidly clears free radicals with no net harm. At extreme PO2 (>2 atm), massive O2 delivery overwhelms these enzyme systems — the amounts of O2− and H2O2 generated far exceed the enzymatic capacity to neutralize them, leading to lipid peroxidation of polyunsaturated FAs in neuronal membranes and oxidation of cellular enzymes. Cytochrome P450 enzymes metabolize drugs and other compounds and are not among the free-radical scavengers Guyton lists (peroxidases, catalases, and superoxide dismutases).","boardPrep":"Guyton Ch. 45 — antioxidant enzymes: SOD (O2− → H2O2), catalase, peroxidases, superoxide dismutase. At extreme PO2, these enzymes are overwhelmed → unchecked free radical damage. Nervous tissue is especially susceptible due to high lipid (polyunsaturated FA) content.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — ARDS, where reactive oxygen species from neutrophils injure the alveolus","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Cytochrome P450 enzymes metabo"}},{"id":"sdl37_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Pulmonary Barotrauma, Decompression Sickness, and Nitrogen Narcosis","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old recreational scuba diver surfaces rapidly after a 150-foot, 2-hour compressed-air dive. He then drives 4 hours to the nearest hospital, arriving with severe bilateral knee pain, right arm weakness, and mild dyspnea. Vital signs: BP 128/82, HR 102, RR 18, SpO2 94% on room air. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate management?","choices":{"A":"Administer 100% O2 via nonrebreather mask and arrange emergent orthopedic joint aspiration","B":"Transfer immediately to a hyperbaric oxygen chamber for recompression followed by staged decompression","C":"Obtain urgent MRI of the spine and administer high-dose IV methylprednisolone for cord edema","D":"Administer IV acetazolamide to acidify blood and increase nitrogen solubility in tissues","E":"Initiate IV heparin anticoagulation to prevent thrombosis around intravascular nitrogen microbubbles"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The diagnosis is decompression sickness (bends + neurological + pulmonary features), and the definitive treatment is hyperbaric recompression. Recompression physically redissolves nitrogen bubbles back into tissue fluids by raising ambient pressure; subsequent staged decompression following US Navy tables allows slow, safe nitrogen elimination via the lungs. Even after a 4-hour delay, recompression is still indicated and effective. Normobaric 100% O2 via NRB can be used as a temporizing measure during transport but is not definitive. Heparin, methylprednisolone, and acetazolamide have no established role in acute DCS. Joint aspiration would not address the intravascular or tissue-level bubble burden.","boardPrep":"First Aid p.691 — DCS: treat with hyperbaric O2. Guyton Ch. 45 — DCS treatment: immediate hyperbaric recompression → staged decompression per US Navy tables. 2/3 of dissolved N2 eliminated in 1st hour; 90% in 6 hours under proper schedule. Normobaric 100% O2 is temporizing only.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 2 — Pneumothorax: spontaneous, traumatic, and tension mechanisms; pulmonary barotrauma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Heparin, methylprednisolone, a","D":"Heparin, methylprednisolone, a","E":"Heparin, methylprednisolone, a"}},{"id":"sdl37_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Pulmonary Barotrauma, Decompression Sickness, and Nitrogen Narcosis","batch":2,"stem":"A team of commercial divers planning a saturation dive to 800 feet is briefed on using a helium-oxygen mixture instead of compressed air. Which of the following correctly identifies one advantage of helium over nitrogen AND provides an accurate physiological explanation for that advantage?","choices":{"A":"Greater decompression safety — helium is more soluble in fat than nitrogen, creating a larger tissue reservoir that buffers pressure changes","B":"Higher airway resistance tolerance — helium's greater molecular density reduces turbulent flow during forced inhalation at extreme depth","C":"Reduced narcosis — helium has about one-fifth the narcotic potency of nitrogen because it is less lipid-soluble","D":"Better oxygen delivery — helium binds directly to hemoglobin at high pressure, increasing total O2-carrying capacity","E":"Improved CO2 clearance — helium inhibits carbonic anhydrase, augmenting bicarbonate-to-CO2 conversion in the lungs"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Helium offers three key advantages over nitrogen in deep diving: (1) ~1/5 the narcotic potency due to lower lipid solubility (less membrane dissolution); (2) only about half as much volume dissolves in body tissues AND diffuses out several times more rapidly, greatly reducing DCS risk; (3) helium's density is only 1/7 that of nitrogen, dramatically reducing airway resistance at extreme pressures (where compressed nitrogen becomes so dense that breathing work may exceed physiological tolerance). The answer choices about greater fat solubility and higher density are reversed — helium is LESS fat-soluble and LESS dense than nitrogen.","boardPrep":"Guyton Ch. 45 — helium advantages in deep saturation diving: (1) 1/5 narcotic potency of N2; (2) 1/2 the tissue dissolution volume + faster diffusion out → less DCS; (3) 1/7 the density of N2 → reduced airway resistance at depth. O2 concentration must also be reduced (1% O2 at 700 ft = adequate FiO2).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 2 — Pneumothorax: spontaneous, traumatic, and tension mechanisms; pulmonary barotrauma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The answer choices about great","B":"The answer choices about great"}},{"id":"sdl37_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Pulmonary Barotrauma, Decompression Sickness, and Nitrogen Narcosis","batch":2,"stem":"A diver breathing compressed air at 200 feet for 45 minutes becomes markedly drowsy and loses significant muscle strength. A dive partner notes extreme clumsiness. The diver is slowly ascended to 50 feet over 5 minutes. What is the expected clinical course and underlying basis for this change?","choices":{"A":"Symptoms reverse within minutes of ascent because nitrogen's lipid-membrane dissolution is purely physical","B":"Symptoms resolve over 24–48 hours as nitrogen slowly exits myelin sheaths via retrograde axonal transport","C":"Symptoms are permanent because nitrogen has covalently modified NMDA receptor subunits at depth","D":"Symptoms worsen transiently during ascent as decompression releases toxic nitrogen metabolites from adipose","E":"Symptoms persist for 4–6 hours as hepatic cytochrome P450 enzymes metabolize dissolved CNS nitrogen"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Nitrogen narcosis is entirely and rapidly reversible. Nitrogen dissolves into neuronal lipid membranes through purely physical intermolecular forces — there is no covalent modification of receptors, no toxic metabolite formation, and no structural neuronal damage. As ambient pressure decreases during ascent, the partial pressure of nitrogen in the alveoli falls, establishing a gradient for nitrogen to leave tissues and be expired. Within minutes at a shallower depth, neuronal membrane nitrogen concentration drops and function normalizes. This distinguishes nitrogen narcosis from decompression sickness, which requires staged decompression, and from O2 toxicity, which can cause permanent neuronal damage from lipid peroxidation.","boardPrep":"Guyton Ch. 45 — nitrogen narcosis: mechanism = physical dissolution of N2 in lipid membranes (same as general anesthetic gases — halogenated agents, nitrous oxide); no covalent bonding → fully and rapidly reversible on ascent. No known long-term effects if ascent is not too rapid.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 2 — Pneumothorax: spontaneous, traumatic, and tension mechanisms; pulmonary barotrauma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Nitrogen dissolves into neuron","D":"Nitrogen dissolves into neuron"}},{"id":"sdl37_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Hyperbaric Oxygen (HBO) Therapy — Rationale and Mechanism of Action","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old commercial diver develops severe bilateral joint pain, right leg paresthesias, and acute dyspnea with frothy sputum within 15 minutes of surfacing from a 300-foot saturation dive. He is emergently placed in a hyperbaric chamber. Which of the following best explains the dual mechanism by which HBO treats his decompression sickness?","choices":{"A":"HBO produces superoxide that directly oxidizes N2 gas into nontoxic nitrous oxide metabolites","B":"HBO eliminates N2 through pulmonary capillaries via a new pressure gradient while simultaneously generating bactericidal free radicals against organisms introduced during the dive","C":"HBO reduces surface tension of N2 microbubbles by oxidizing their lipid-protein coat, enabling macrophage phagocytosis of the bubbles","D":"HBO improves O2 delivery to bubble-obstructed tissues via dissolved plasma O2 while acting as a pulmonary vasoconstrictor to prevent paradoxical embolism","E":"Recompression redissolves N2 bubbles, and staged decompression while breathing high-PO2 gas lets N2 diffuse out through the lungs"},"correct":"E","explanation":"HBO treats DCS through a dual physical-chemical mechanism: First, the increased ambient pressure (recompression) physically compresses N2 bubbles back into solution — Boyle's law in reverse (higher pressure → smaller volume → redissolution). This immediately relieves bubble-mediated obstruction and tissue ischemia. Second, because the diver is now breathing 100% O2 at high pressure, tissue nitrogen partial pressure falls rapidly (O2 replaces N2 in tissues), creating a large gradient for N2 to diffuse into capillaries and be expired via the lungs. The staged decompression schedule then slowly reduces pressure, allowing N2 to leave tissues faster than new bubbles can form. The elevated dissolved O2 simultaneously treats any ischemic injury from bubble-induced vascular occlusion. High-pressure O2 does not convert nitrogen into nitrous oxide; recompression simply forces the nitrogen bubbles back into solution so the gas can be breathed off during slow decompression.","boardPrep":"First Aid p.691 — DCS: treat with hyperbaric O2. Guyton Ch. 45 — HBO for DCS: recompression → redissolves bubbles; staged decompression → slow N2 elimination via lungs. 2/3 of N2 eliminated in 1 hour; 90% in 6 hours under proper schedule.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — Nitric oxide pharmacology and inhaled gas therapy for pulmonary hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Hyperbaric Oxygen (HBO) Therapy — Rationale and Mechanism of Action","batch":2,"stem":"A hospital reviews its hyperbaric oxygen unit utilization. A hospitalist asks the wound care team to identify which of the following conditions has the strongest mechanistic rationale for HBO based specifically on the organism's susceptibility to oxidizing free radicals at elevated PO2.","choices":{"A":"Influenza pneumonia — HBO kills respiratory viruses by oxidizing viral neuraminidase surface proteins","B":"Pulmonary embolism — HBO reduces thrombus burden by generating plasminogen-activating superoxide radicals","C":"Autoimmune pulmonary fibrosis — HBO neutralizes TGF-β by oxidizing its cysteine disulfide bonds","D":"Clostridial gas gangrene — an obligate anaerobe whose growth stops at tissue PO2 above 70 mmHg","E":"Acute respiratory distress syndrome — HBO directly oxidizes alveolar hyaline membrane components"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The strongest mechanistic match for HBO's free radical mechanism is gas gangrene (Clostridial myonecrosis). Clostridial organisms are obligate anaerobes that lack the antioxidant enzyme systems (catalase, SOD, peroxidase) that aerobic organisms possess. When tissue PO2 rises above ~70 mmHg via HBO, the oxidizing free radicals (O2−, H2O2, •OH) generated from dissolved O2 cannot be neutralized by the bacteria, halting their growth and toxin production. Viruses, thromboembolism, autoimmune fibrosis, and ARDS involve fundamentally different pathologies where HBO's free radical mechanism provides no specific therapeutic benefit in the same direct way.","boardPrep":"Guyton Ch. 45 — HBO for gas gangrene: Clostridium is an obligate anaerobe; stops growing at PO2 >70 mmHg → oxidizing free radicals are bactericidal. Formerly almost 100% fatal; early HBO + surgical debridement now curative in most cases. Other HBO indications: DCS, CO poisoning, osteomyelitis, arterial gas embolism, MI.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — Nitric oxide pharmacology and inhaled gas therapy for pulmonary hypertension","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Viruses, thromboembolism, auto","B":"Viruses, thromboembolism, auto","C":"Viruses, thromboembolism, auto","E":"Viruses, thromboembolism, auto"}},{"id":"sdl37_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Hyperbaric Oxygen (HBO) Therapy — Rationale and Mechanism of Action","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man is pulled from a house fire with a carboxyhemoglobin level of 35% and confusion. The team weighs normobaric 100% O2 by nonrebreather mask against transfer to a hyperbaric chamber. First Aid gives a CO-Hb half-life of about 300 minutes on room air and about 80 minutes on 100% O2. Approximately what half-life does it give in a hyperbaric O2 chamber?","choices":{"A":"About 150 minutes","B":"About 80 minutes","C":"About 60 minutes","D":"About 20 minutes","E":"About 2 minutes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"First Aid 2025 gives the carboxyhemoglobin half-life as about 300 minutes on room air, falling to about 80 minutes on 100% O2 and to about 20 minutes in a hyperbaric O2 chamber, and recommends hyperbaric oxygen if CO poisoning is severe. Guyton explains the basis: O2 at 2 to 3 atm raises alveolar and dissolved O2 so greatly that it displaces CO from hemoglobin rapidly, even though CO binds about 250 times as tenaciously as O2. About 150 minutes lies between the room-air and normobaric values. About 80 minutes is the normobaric 100% O2 value, which HBO improves on. About 60 minutes is only a modest gain over normobaric O2. About 2 minutes is far faster than any source gives.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CO-Hb half-life ≈ 300 min (room air) → ≈ 80 min (100% O2) → ≈ 20 min (hyperbaric O2) (First Aid). CO binds Hb > 200× (FA) or ~250× (Guyton) more avidly than O2, shifts the curve left, and leaves PaO2 normal. COMLEX: Give 100% O2 at once; consider HBO in severe poisoning; suspect concurrent cyanide in fire victims.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 11 — carbon monoxide's effects on hemoglobin and O2-carrying capacity","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"About 80 minutes is the normob"}},{"id":"sdl37_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Oxygen Toxicity — Excessive Intracellular Oxidation and CNS Susceptibility","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher studying hyperbaric oxygen toxicity measures reactive oxygen species in blood samples from a diver breathing 100% O2 at 4 atm. She identifies superoxide (O2−) and hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) as the primary culprits. Which of the following best describes the sequential generation of these species and the reason they escape scavenging at extreme PO2?","choices":{"A":"O2 is reduced by one electron in mitochondria to O2−, which SOD converts to H2O2; at extreme PO2, generation outpaces both enzymes","B":"O2− forms from oxyhemoglobin spontaneous dissociation; H2O2 arises from the mitochondrial proton pump acting on excess dissolved O2","C":"O2 directly forms H2O2 via spontaneous hemoglobin autoxidation; H2O2 then undergoes Fenton reaction reversal to regenerate O2−","D":"Both O2− and H2O2 are generated by hepatic cytochrome P450 mixed-function oxidases acting on excess plasma dissolved O2","E":"O2− forms from high-pressure N2 oxidation; H2O2 forms from CO2 hydration at extreme alveolar PO2"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Mitochondrial electron transport is the primary site of free radical generation under normal conditions: a small fraction of the O2 consumed undergoes incomplete single-electron reduction to form superoxide (O2−) rather than full 4-electron reduction to water. SOD rapidly converts O2− to H2O2 (2 O2− + 2H+ → H2O2 + O2), and catalase/peroxidase then convert H2O2 to water. At extreme PO2 (>2 atm), the rate of O2− generation increases proportionally with tissue PO2 — the absolute concentration of O2 driving single-electron reductions rises dramatically. The enzymatic clearance capacity has a fixed maximum rate (Km/Vmax), which is saturated at extreme free radical generation rates, allowing both O2− and H2O2 to accumulate to toxic levels.","boardPrep":"Guyton Ch. 45 — O2 free radical cascade: O2 → O2− (superoxide, via one-electron reduction) → H2O2 (via SOD) → H2O (via catalase/peroxidase). At extreme PO2: rate of generation exceeds enzyme capacity → O2− and H2O2 accumulate → lipid peroxidation of polyunsaturated FAs in neuronal membranes → CNS toxicity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — ARDS: oxidative injury, diffuse alveolar damage, and pathogenesis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl37_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Oxygen Toxicity — Excessive Intracellular Oxidation and CNS Susceptibility","batch":2,"stem":"An animal study examines tissue vulnerability to acute hyperbaric O2 toxicity at 4 atm. Nervous tissue demonstrates the greatest degree of lipid peroxidation and membrane injury, while skeletal muscle and hepatic tissue show far less damage at the same exposure duration. Which of the following best explains the disproportionate neuronal vulnerability?","choices":{"A":"Neurons express fewer Na+/K+-ATPase copies, making them more dependent on intact membrane lipid environment for electrochemical gradients","B":"Myelin is primarily composed of saturated fatty acids that react preferentially with superoxide radicals compared with unsaturated lipids","C":"CNS microglia and astrocytes completely lack superoxide dismutase and catalase, leaving neurons without any antioxidant defense","D":"Neuronal membranes are rich in polyunsaturated fatty acids, and nervous tissue has a very high lipid content, favoring peroxidation","E":"Neurons have the highest ATP turnover rate of any cell type, generating proportionally more mitochondrial superoxide per unit time"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lipid peroxidation — the chain reaction by which free radicals attack carbon-carbon double bonds in polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFAs) — is the primary mechanism of O2 toxicity in the nervous system. Neurons are uniquely vulnerable because: (1) neuronal membranes contain exceptionally high concentrations of PUFAs (arachidonic acid, docosahexaenoic acid, etc.) — essential for membrane fluidity and receptor function; (2) brain tissue has the highest total lipid content of any organ. Each PUFA carbon-carbon double bond is a target for free radical attack; once peroxidation begins, it self-propagates as a chain reaction until quenched by antioxidants. Saturated FAs lack these double bonds and are far less susceptible. Myelin, in fact, contains a mix of lipids but its total PUFA load contributes to myelin vulnerability.","boardPrep":"Guyton Ch. 45 — O2 toxicity mechanism: oxidizing free radicals peroxidize polyunsaturated FAs in cell membranes AND oxidize essential cellular enzymes. Nervous tissue most susceptible: highest lipid content + high PUFA concentration → membrane structural damage → seizures, coma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — ARDS: oxidative injury, diffuse alveolar damage, and pathogenesis","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Saturated FAs lack these doubl"}},{"id":"sdl37_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Oxygen Toxicity — Excessive Intracellular Oxidation and CNS Susceptibility","batch":2,"stem":"A diver breathing 100% O2 at 4 atm has an alveolar PO2 of approximately 3,040 mmHg. Based on the O2-hemoglobin dissociation curve at extreme PO2 values, which of the following best describes the composition of blood O2 content leaving the lungs?","choices":{"A":"Hemoglobin carries ~20 vol% O2 (fully saturated) and dissolved plasma O2 adds ~9 vol% — ~29 vol% per 100 mL","B":"Hemoglobin contributes ~15 vol% O2 and dissolved O2 ~0.5 vol%; total ~15.5 vol% — O2 toxicity reduces Hb capacity","C":"All hemoglobin converts to methemoglobin at extreme PO2, making dissolved O2 the only functional O2 carrier","D":"Dissolved O2 in plasma remains negligible (<0.3 vol%); all O2 is hemoglobin-bound at 100% saturation as usual","E":"Hemoglobin contributes ~20 vol% and dissolved O2 ~30 vol% — total ~50 vol%; extreme PO2 linearly scales dissolved O2"},"correct":"A","explanation":"At normal alveolar PO2 (~100 mmHg), dissolved O2 contributes only ~0.3 vol% (negligible compared to Hb-bound ~20 vol%). However, dissolved O2 increases linearly with PO2 per Henry's law (0.003 mL O2/dL/mmHg). At PO2 3,040 mmHg: dissolved O2 = 3,040 × 0.003 = ~9.1 vol%. Hemoglobin remains fully saturated at ~20 vol%. Total blood O2 content = ~29 vol% per 100 mL. Tissues consume only ~5 vol%, leaving venous blood at ~24 vol% — corresponding to a venous PO2 of ~1,200 mmHg. This extreme tissue PO2 explains the overwhelming free radical production: the Hb buffering mechanism, which normally maintains tissue PO2 at ~40 mmHg, is completely bypassed.","boardPrep":"Guyton Ch. 45 — at 4 atm (PO2 ~3,040 mmHg): Hb-bound O2 ~20 vol% (saturated) + dissolved O2 ~9 vol% = ~29 vol% total. After tissue extraction (~5 vol%), venous PO2 ~1,200 mmHg → Hb buffer overwhelmed → toxic tissue PO2. At normal PO2 <120 mmHg, dissolved O2 is negligible.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 4 — Oxygen transport principles: dissolved versus hemoglobin-bound O2","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Hemoglobin remains fully satur","E":"At PO2 3,040 mmHg: dissolved O"}}],"38":[{"id":"sdl38_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Nitric Oxide — Pharmacology, Physiologic Role, and Medications for Pulmonary Hypertension","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with long-standing hypertension undergoes a graded treadmill exercise test. As cardiac output increases during exertion, the endothelial lining of his coronary arteries begins producing a short-lived vasodilatory molecule. Which of the following best describes the primary stimulus and transducer responsible for initiating this response?","choices":{"A":"Sympathetic adrenergic terminals release norepinephrine, which binds α1 receptors on endothelium to stimulate NOS activity","B":"Elevated arterial PO2 during exercise directly oxidizes L-arginine in endothelial mitochondria, releasing NO as a byproduct","C":"Flow-induced shear stress on the endothelium opens PIEZO1 mechanosensitive channels, which activate eNOS to generate nitric oxide","D":"Bradykinin released from perivascular mast cells activates Gq-coupled B2 receptors, which stimulate eNOS via inositol triphosphate","E":"Myocardial hypoxia triggers nNOS isoform upregulation in cardiomyocytes, releasing NO into the coronary lumen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Endothelial NOS (eNOS) is the primary source of physiologic NO in the vasculature. During increased blood flow, mechanical shear stress is detected by the PIEZO1 mechanosensitive cation channel embedded in the endothelial glycocalyx. PIEZO1 activation triggers calcium influx and calmodulin activation, which binds and activates eNOS, catalyzing the oxidation of L-arginine to L-citrulline + NO. This flow-mediated dilation ensures coronary autoregulation during exercise. While bradykinin and other agonists can also activate eNOS via Gq/IP3/calcium pathways, the physiologic stimulus during increased flow is primarily mechanical (PIEZO1-mediated), not agonist-mediated. nNOS is expressed in neurons, not cardiomyocytes, and iNOS is the cytokine-inducible isoform involved in septic shock.","boardPrep":"Katzung Ch. 19 — Three NOS isoforms: eNOS (endothelial, constitutive, Ca2+/calmodulin-dependent, PIEZO1 shear-force activation), nNOS (neuronal, constitutive), iNOS (inducible, high-output NO in macrophages/sepsis). L-arginine → [NOS] → L-citrulline + NO. Flow-mediated vasodilation = classic boards concept linking eNOS to cardiovascular physiology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 1 — HIF, which upregulates NOS genes in hypoxia","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"While bradykinin and other ago"}},{"id":"sdl38_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Nitric Oxide — Pharmacology, Physiologic Role, and Medications for Pulmonary Hypertension","batch":1,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with stable angina uses sublingual nitroglycerin for chest pain relief. A medical student asks the attending to explain how nitroglycerin ultimately causes vasodilation at the molecular level. Which of the following correctly traces the complete downstream signaling cascade from nitroglycerin to smooth muscle relaxation?","choices":{"A":"Nitroglycerin → mitochondrial ALDH2 bioactivation → NO release → sGC activation → ↑cGMP → PKG → MLCK inhibition + BKCa opening → relaxation","B":"Nitroglycerin → spontaneous NO release at physiologic pH → direct guanylyl cyclase activation → cAMP ↑ → PKA → myosin light chain dephosphorylation","C":"Nitroglycerin → hepatic CYP3A4 bioactivation → NO2– release → vascular smooth muscle adenylyl cyclase → cAMP → MLCK inhibition","D":"Nitroglycerin → calcium channel blockade in vascular smooth muscle → reduced intracellular Ca2+ → calmodulin dissociation from MLCK → relaxation","E":"Nitroglycerin → endothelial PIEZO1 activation → eNOS upregulation → endogenous NO synthesis → sGC/cGMP cascade"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Nitroglycerin (GTN) requires enzymatic bioactivation by mitochondrial aldehyde dehydrogenase-2 (ALDH2) to generate NO. This is clinically significant because ALDH2 polymorphisms (particularly common in East Asian populations) reduce GTN efficacy. The released NO activates soluble guanylyl cyclase (sGC), which converts GTP → cGMP. Elevated cGMP activates PKG (protein kinase G), which phosphorylates and inhibits myosin light chain kinase (MLCK), directly reduces intracellular Ca2+, and opens large-conductance Ca2+-activated K+ channels (BKCa), collectively causing smooth muscle relaxation. The mechanism uses cGMP, not cAMP — a critical distinction from β-agonists and PDE3 inhibitors.","boardPrep":"Katzung Ch. 19 — Nitrate bioactivation: GTN/ISDN/ISMN → ALDH2 (mitochondrial) → NO. Nitrate tolerance = ALDH2 enzyme oxidation/inactivation. Signal cascade: NO → sGC → cGMP → PKG → MLCK inhibition + BKCa → SMC relaxation. PDE5 inhibitors (sildenafil) prolong cGMP by blocking its hydrolysis — hence dangerous combination with nitrates (profound hypotension).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 5 — the thin-walled, low-resistance pulmonary vessels whose tone NO also sets","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The mechanism uses cGMP, not c"}},{"id":"sdl38_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Nitric Oxide — Pharmacology, Physiologic Role, and Medications for Pulmonary Hypertension","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with WHO Group 1 pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) on home oxygen develops progressive dyspnea on minimal exertion. Right heart catheterization confirms mean pulmonary artery pressure of 42 mmHg. She is started on oral sildenafil. Which of the following mechanisms best describes the drug's action?","choices":{"A":"Direct stimulation of soluble guanylyl cyclase (sGC) independent of endogenous NO, increasing cGMP in pulmonary smooth muscle","B":"Blockade of endothelin A and B receptors, preventing the vasoconstrictive and proliferative effects of endothelin-1 in pulmonary arterioles","C":"Inhibition of PDE5, the phosphodiesterase that degrades cGMP in pulmonary vascular smooth muscle, thereby prolonging NO-mediated vasodilation","D":"Inhibition of prostacyclin synthase to shunt arachidonic acid toward PGI2 production, enhancing pulmonary vasodilation","E":"Competitive antagonism of thromboxane A2 receptors on pulmonary arterial smooth muscle, reducing vasoconstriction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sildenafil (and tadalafil) are selective PDE5 inhibitors. PDE5 is the phosphodiesterase responsible for degrading cGMP in pulmonary vascular smooth muscle. By inhibiting PDE5, sildenafil allows cGMP (generated via the NO/sGC pathway) to accumulate, prolonging PKG activation and sustained smooth muscle relaxation/vasodilation selectively in the pulmonary vasculature. PDE5 is highly expressed in pulmonary vessels and relatively less so in systemic vasculature, providing some selectivity. Direct, NO-independent sGC stimulation describes riociguat, and endothelin A/B receptor blockade describes bosentan/macitentan. Thromboxane antagonism and prostacyclin synthase inhibition are not current PAH pharmacotherapy targets.","boardPrep":"Katzung Ch. 19 — PH drug classes for boards: (1) PDE5i = sildenafil, tadalafil; (2) sGC stimulators = riociguat, vericiguat; (3) ERA = bosentan, macitentan (dual ET-A/B); (4) Prostacyclin analogs = epoprostenol, treprostinil, iloprost. PDE5i contraindicated with nitrates (both ↑ cGMP → additive hypotension). Riociguat also contraindicated with PDE5i (same reason) AND with nitrates.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — WHO group 1 pulmonary arterial hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Carbonic Anhydrase Inhibitors — Pharmacology and Therapeutic Role in Acute Mountain Sickness","batch":1,"stem":"A 26-year-old mountain climber travels from sea level to a base camp at 14,000 feet over 18 hours. The following morning she develops headache, nausea, and fatigue consistent with acute mountain sickness (AMS). She was prescribed acetazolamide prophylaxis but forgot to take it. Which of the following best explains the mechanism by which acetazolamide prevents AMS?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition of cerebral carbonic anhydrase → reduced CSF production → decreased intracranial pressure → relief of high-altitude cerebral edema","B":"Stimulation of erythropoietin release from the kidney → accelerated red blood cell production → improved oxygen-carrying capacity within 24 hours","C":"Proximal tubule CA inhibition → bicarbonaturia → mild hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis → chemoreceptor stimulation → ↑ventilation → ↑PaO2","D":"Blockade of aquaporin-4 channels in astrocyte end-feet → reduced cerebral edema formation secondary to hypoxic blood-brain barrier disruption","E":"Inhibition of hypoxia-inducible factor-1α (HIF-1α) → prevention of VEGF-driven pulmonary vascular leak → reduced high-altitude pulmonary edema"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Acetazolamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase (CA) in the proximal tubule, blocking the conversion of CO2 + H2O → H2CO3 → H+ + HCO3–. Without CA, bicarbonate cannot be efficiently reabsorbed, leading to bicarbonaturia. The loss of HCO3– base causes a mild hyperchloremic (non-anion gap) metabolic acidosis. This acidosis stimulates peripheral chemoreceptors (carotid bodies), increasing the respiratory drive and producing compensatory hyperventilation. At altitude, this forced increase in ventilation raises alveolar PO2 and arterial PaO2, partially correcting the hypoxic stimulus behind AMS. The diuretic effect itself is self-limited (as HCO3– stores deplete), but the ventilatory benefit persists throughout altitude exposure — explaining why the AMS benefit outlasts the diuresis.","boardPrep":"Katzung Ch. 15 — CA inhibitors: acetazolamide (systemic), methazolamide, dichlorphenamide. Proximal tubule mechanism: ↓ HCO3– reabsorption → bicarbonaturia → hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis → ↑ ventilation → ↑ PaO2. Used for AMS prophylaxis. Also used for: open-angle glaucoma (↓ aqueous humor), absence epilepsy (rare), idiopathic intracranial hypertension.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — the renal compensation of high-altitude acclimatization","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Carbonic Anhydrase Inhibitors — Pharmacology and Therapeutic Role in Acute Mountain Sickness","batch":1,"stem":"A 34-year-old man is prescribed acetazolamide for open-angle glaucoma. After two weeks of therapy, routine labs reveal: Na+ 138 mEq/L, K+ 3.1 mEq/L, Cl– 112 mEq/L, HCO3– 16 mEq/L, BUN 14 mg/dL, creatinine 0.9 mg/dL. Which of the following acid-base disturbances is most consistent with these findings?","choices":{"A":"Hyperchloremic (non-anion gap) metabolic acidosis with hypokalemia, secondary to carbonic anhydrase inhibition and bicarbonaturia","B":"Respiratory alkalosis from central chemoreceptor stimulation by acetazolamide's direct brainstem effect","C":"Metabolic alkalosis with hypokalemia from enhanced K+/H+ exchange in the collecting duct secondary to increased distal Na+ delivery","D":"High anion gap metabolic acidosis from accumulation of organic acids due to impaired renal tubular secretion","E":"Mixed respiratory alkalosis and metabolic acidosis from acetazolamide-induced Fanconi syndrome"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Anion gap = Na+ – (Cl– + HCO3–) = 138 – (112 + 16) = 10 mEq/L — this is a normal anion gap (hyperchloremic) metabolic acidosis. Acetazolamide blocks proximal tubule CA, preventing H+ secretion and bicarbonate reabsorption. The lost HCO3– is replaced by Cl– (chloride retention by the kidney), producing hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis. Increased distal tubule Na+ delivery (from the proximally retained Na+ that was not reabsorbed with HCO3–) drives Na+/K+ exchange in the collecting duct, causing potassium wasting and hypokalemia. Unlike loop diuretics, acetazolamide does NOT cause metabolic alkalosis — it causes the opposite. The diuretic effect self-limits as HCO3– stores are depleted and Na+ delivery to the proximal tubule normalizes.","boardPrep":"Katzung Ch. 15 — Acetazolamide ADRs: (1) Hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis — normal anion gap; (2) Hypokalemia — ↑ distal Na+ delivery → K+ wasting; (3) Renal stones — calcium phosphate (alkaline urine + phosphaturia and hypercalciuria); (4) Paresthesias (tingling of extremities — common early side effect); (5) Allergic reactions (sulfonamide derivative). Self-limiting diuresis distinguishes it from loop/thiazide diuretics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 10 — carbonic anhydrase in CO2 and bicarbonate transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Carbonic Anhydrase Inhibitors — Pharmacology and Therapeutic Role in Acute Mountain Sickness","batch":1,"stem":"A 51-year-old man with Child-Pugh class B cirrhosis due to alcoholic liver disease presents with increased intraocular pressure and early open-angle glaucoma. His ophthalmologist considers acetazolamide but is concerned about a potentially serious adverse effect specific to this patient's condition. Which of the following best explains this contraindication?","choices":{"A":"Acetazolamide is hepatically metabolized by CYP3A4, which is impaired in cirrhosis, leading to drug accumulation and systemic carbonic anhydrase toxicity","B":"Alkaline urine traps less ammonia as NH4+, so more NH3 is reabsorbed, raising blood ammonia and precipitating hepatic encephalopathy","C":"Cirrhosis reduces proximal tubule CA expression, rendering acetazolamide ineffective and increasing the risk of paradoxical fluid retention","D":"Increased portal venous pressure prevents adequate drug delivery to the kidney, causing preferential CA inhibition in red blood cells and impaired CO2 transport","E":"Acetazolamide is concentrated in hepatocytes and directly inhibits glutamine synthetase, blocking hepatic ammonia detoxification"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In cirrhosis, the liver cannot adequately convert ammonia to urea. Acetazolamide-induced alkalinization of the tubular fluid converts NH4+ to NH3, which is rapidly reabsorbed instead of being trapped and excreted in the urine (Katzung Ch 15: carbonic anhydrase inhibitor-induced urine alkalinization decreases urinary NH4+ excretion). In a patient whose liver already cannot clear ammonia, the extra ammonia load can precipitate hepatic encephalopathy. Acetazolamide is excreted unchanged by the kidney rather than cleared by hepatic CYP3A4, cirrhosis does not abolish its proximal tubule effect, portal pressure does not redirect its action to red cells, and it does not act on glutamine synthetase. Topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (e.g., dorzolamide, brinzolamide eye drops) are preferred in glaucoma patients with hepatic disease, as they achieve negligible systemic absorption.","boardPrep":"Katzung Ch. 15 — Acetazolamide absolute contraindications: (1) Cirrhosis — urine alkalinization ↓ urinary NH4+ excretion → hyperammonemia/hepatic encephalopathy; (2) Sulfonamide allergy (structural sulfonamide → cross-reactivity); (3) Severe renal failure; (4) Adrenocortical insufficiency. Preferred glaucoma alternative in hepatic disease: topical CA inhibitors (dorzolamide, brinzolamide) — avoid systemic CA inhibition.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — renal bicarbonate handling during acclimatization, the same lever acetazolamide pulls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Opioids in Cough Suppression — Role and Differentiation of Medication Derivatives","batch":1,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy with a viral upper respiratory infection develops a dry, non-productive nighttime cough severe enough to disrupt his sleep and his parents'. His pediatrician considers an antitussive agent. Which of the following best describes the neural mechanism by which opioid-class antitussives suppress the cough reflex?","choices":{"A":"Activation of GABA-B receptors in the vagal nucleus ambiguus, hyperpolarizing efferent motor neurons projecting to the respiratory musculature","B":"Peripheral blockade of C-fiber and Aδ-fiber sensory endings in the laryngeal and tracheobronchial mucosa, preventing the trigger signal from reaching the spinal cord","C":"Antagonism of TRPV1 (capsaicin receptor) channels in airway epithelium, desensitizing the chemical cough trigger zone to acid and irritant stimuli","D":"Inhibition of substance P release from vagal afferents in the pulmonary parenchyma, reducing neuropeptide-driven central sensitization of the cough reflex","E":"Activation of μ-opioid receptors in brainstem cough centers (nucleus tractus solitarius and adjacent medulla), suppressing the central cough reflex arc"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Opioids suppress cough centrally by activating μ-opioid receptors in brainstem nuclei that integrate the cough reflex — principally the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS) and adjacent medullary reticular formation. This central mechanism reduces both the afferent sensory processing of cough triggers (e.g., from vagal C-fibers and cough receptors in the larynx/trachea) and the efferent coordinated motor output that produces the expiratory blast. Importantly, this cough suppression occurs at doses substantially lower than those required for analgesia, which is why codeine can be used as an antitussive at sub-analgesic doses (Katzung Ch 31: 15 mg is usually sufficient to relieve cough; codeine combination analgesics contain 15, 30 or 60 mg). Peripheral mechanisms play secondary roles; the primary therapeutic target is the central brainstem cough center.","boardPrep":"Katzung Ch. 31 — Opioid antitussives: central μ-receptor mechanism in brainstem cough centers (NTS/medulla). Sub-analgesic doses effective. Codeine = prototype opioid antitussive; dextromethorphan = non-opioid antitussive (no μ-receptor activity at therapeutic doses). Opioids contraindicated for productive (wet) cough — suppressing expectoration impairs pathogen clearance and risks pneumonia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 15 — the cough reflex arc that antitussives suppress","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Peripheral mechanisms play sec"}},{"id":"sdl38_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Opioids in Cough Suppression — Role and Differentiation of Medication Derivatives","batch":1,"stem":"A pharmacology student is comparing codeine and dextromethorphan as antitussive agents for OTC cold preparations. Which of the following statements most accurately distinguishes dextromethorphan from codeine with respect to both mechanism and abuse potential?","choices":{"A":"Dextromethorphan is the active metabolite of codeine produced by CYP2D6 demethylation, and shares codeine's full μ-opioid agonist activity but with a longer half-life","B":"Dextromethorphan acts on κ-opioid receptors in the periphery, producing cough suppression equivalent to codeine with reduced CNS sedation and less respiratory depression","C":"Unlike codeine, dextromethorphan does not require hepatic bioactivation and exerts its antitussive effect through direct blockade of peripheral C-fiber afferents in the larynx","D":"Dextromethorphan acts as an NMDA antagonist and serotonin reuptake inhibitor rather than a μ-agonist, with little analgesia or opioid-type dependence potential at therapeutic doses","E":"Codeine and dextromethorphan are diastereomers with identical receptor binding profiles; dextromethorphan's lower abuse potential is due to rapid first-pass hepatic metabolism"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Dextromethorphan (DXM) is the d-isomer of levorphanol (an opioid), but at therapeutic doses it does NOT bind μ-opioid receptors significantly. Its antitussive mechanism involves: (1) NMDA receptor antagonism (similar to ketamine/PCP at sub-anesthetic doses) in brainstem cough centers, and (2) serotonin reuptake inhibition. Because it lacks μ-opioid activity at normal doses, it has minimal analgesic effect and low opioid-type dependence potential, enabling OTC availability. However, at high doses (DXM abuse/'robo-tripping'), dissociative/psychotomimetic effects emerge from NMDA antagonism, and deaths have been reported. Dextromethorphan is NOT a metabolite of codeine — codeine is demethylated to morphine by CYP2D6. The two drugs are structurally related but pharmacologically distinct.","boardPrep":"Katzung Ch. 31 — Dextromethorphan: mechanism = NMDA antagonist + serotonin reuptake inhibitor (NOT μ-opioid agonist at therapeutic doses). OTC available (non-scheduled). Key interaction: DXM + MAOI → serotonin syndrome (↑↑ synaptic serotonin). High-dose DXM = NMDA-mediated dissociation ('robo-tripping'). Codeine: μ-opioid, Schedule II/V, CYP2D6 → morphine.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 13 — opioid depression of breathing among the other factors that affect respiration","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Dextromethorphan is NOT a meta","E":"The two drugs are structurally"}},{"id":"sdl38_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Opioids in Cough Suppression — Role and Differentiation of Medication Derivatives","batch":1,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman with major depressive disorder on phenelzine (an irreversible MAOI) develops a dry cough from an upper respiratory infection. Her roommate offers her an OTC cough syrup containing dextromethorphan. Within 30 minutes of taking it, she develops agitation, diaphoresis, tremor, clonus, and hyperthermia (38.9°C). Which mechanism best explains her presentation?","choices":{"A":"Phenelzine inhibits CYP2D6, causing dextromethorphan accumulation to toxic levels that activate μ-opioid receptors and trigger opioid-induced hyperthermia","B":"MAOIs increase synaptic dopamine levels; dextromethorphan's dopamine reuptake inhibition at high doses additively elevates striatal dopamine, causing hyperthermia via the nigrostriatal pathway","C":"Dextromethorphan's NMDA antagonism synergizes with phenelzine's GABAergic enhancement, producing a hyperadrenergic state via disinhibition of the locus coeruleus","D":"Dextromethorphan inhibits serotonin reuptake; combined with MAOI-mediated blockade of serotonin degradation, synaptic serotonin levels surge dramatically, triggering serotonin syndrome","E":"Phenelzine's irreversible MAO-A inhibition blocks conversion of serotonin to melatonin; dextromethorphan's 5-HT3 agonism further increases pineal serotonin, impairing thermostatic regulation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Serotonin syndrome results from excess serotonergic activity in the CNS and PNS. The classic triad is: (1) altered mental status/agitation, (2) autonomic instability (diaphoresis, hyperthermia, tachycardia, hypertension), and (3) neuromuscular abnormalities (clonus, hyperreflexia, tremor). Phenelzine (irreversible MAO-A/B inhibitor) blocks serotonin degradation, raising synaptic serotonin baseline. Dextromethorphan (DXM) inhibits the serotonin reuptake transporter (SERT), preventing serotonin clearance from the synapse. The combination creates a catastrophic excess of synaptic serotonin. This interaction is a hard contraindication — DXM is explicitly listed as contraindicated with MAOIs. Management includes cyproheptadine (5-HT2A antagonist) and supportive care; discontinue both agents immediately.","boardPrep":"Katzung Ch. 31 — DXM + MAOI = serotonin syndrome (hard contraindication). Mechanism: DXM blocks SERT + MAOI blocks MAO → massive synaptic serotonin ↑↑. Triad: altered mentation, autonomic instability, neuromuscular abnormalities. Treatment: discontinue serotonergic agents, benzodiazepines for agitation, cyproheptadine (5-HT2A antagonist), supportive care (cooling, fluids). Distinguish from NMS (dopamine-related, slower onset, 'lead-pipe' rigidity vs. clonus).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 15 — the cough reflex that antitussives target","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl38_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Nitric Oxide — Pharmacology, Physiologic Role, and Medications for Pulmonary Hypertension","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old previously healthy woman develops severe ARDS following influenza pneumonia (P/F ratio 68). Despite lung-protective ventilation, prone positioning, and neuromuscular blockade, her hypoxemia persists. The ICU team initiates inhaled nitric oxide (iNO) at 5 ppm. Which of the following best explains why iNO improves oxygenation without causing systemic hypotension?","choices":{"A":"Inhaled NO diffuses through the alveolar epithelium and directly inhibits hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction systemically by activating sGC in all vascular smooth muscle, but its short half-life prevents sustained systemic effects","B":"Inhaled NO reaches only ventilated alveoli and dilates their capillaries, shifting flow away from shunt regions and improving V/Q matching; NO entering the blood is inactivated at once by oxyhemoglobin","C":"Inhaled NO stimulates bronchodilation via cGMP-mediated relaxation of airway smooth muscle, reducing dead-space ventilation and improving effective alveolar ventilation globally","D":"Inhaled NO activates surfactant synthesis in type II pneumocytes, reducing alveolar surface tension and reopening collapsed alveoli, thereby increasing the effective surface area for gas exchange without altering blood flow distribution","E":"Inhaled NO reversibly binds to cytochrome c oxidase in mitochondria of pulmonary endothelial cells, reducing their O2 consumption and allowing more alveolar O2 to reach the pulmonary capillary blood"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The therapeutic elegance of inhaled NO lies in its exquisite selectivity. When delivered by inhalation, NO reaches only alveoli that are being ventilated — bypassing collapsed or flooded alveolar units. In the microcirculation adjacent to ventilated alveoli, NO diffuses into vascular smooth muscle and activates sGC/cGMP/PKG, producing localized vasodilation. This increases perfusion to ventilated alveoli and 'steals' blood flow away from non-ventilated shunted regions, thereby improving the V/Q ratio. The critical inactivation step: any NO molecule that diffuses into the pulmonary capillary lumen encounters oxyhemoglobin (HbO2), which avidly scavenges NO to form methemoglobin and nitrate (NO + HbO2 → MetHb + NO3–). This reaction is nearly instantaneous, preventing any NO from surviving in the circulation long enough to reach systemic vessels. This is why iNO improves pulmonary hemodynamics without causing systemic hypotension — a key pharmacological distinction from intravenous vasodilators.","boardPrep":"Katzung Ch. 19 — Inhaled NO: indication = PPHN in neonates (FDA-approved), ARDS (off-label rescue). Mechanism of selectivity: reaches only ventilated alveoli → improves V/Q → inactivated by HbO2 → no systemic effect. Toxicity: methemoglobinemia (especially in neonates, G6PD deficiency); rebound pulmonary HTN on abrupt discontinuation; NO2 formation (monitor inspired concentration). Clinical note: iNO improves oxygenation but has not shown mortality benefit in ARDS RCTs.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 9 — Viral ARDS/DAD: inhaled NO as rescue therapy in refractory hypoxic respiratory failure; selective pulmonary vasodilation preserves V/Q matching","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Nitric Oxide — Pharmacology, Physiologic Role, and Medications for Pulmonary Hypertension","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman with severe WHO Group 1 PAH fails maximal doses of sildenafil. Her pulmonologist considers switching to riociguat. A medical student asks how riociguat differs mechanistically from sildenafil at the molecular level. Which of the following correctly distinguishes their mechanisms of action?","choices":{"A":"Sildenafil activates sGC by binding to its heme-containing catalytic domain, while riociguat inhibits PDE5 by competitive displacement of cGMP; both drugs ultimately increase cGMP but at opposite points in the same pathway","B":"Sildenafil prevents cGMP synthesis by blocking endothelial NO release, while riociguat compensates by stimulating sGC directly; their combination produces additive cGMP elevation with an improved therapeutic index","C":"Sildenafil inhibits PDE5 and slows cGMP breakdown; riociguat stimulates sGC directly — sensitizing it to endogenous NO and also activating it without NO — so it increases cGMP synthesis","D":"Sildenafil and riociguat both inhibit PDE5 but in different subcellular compartments; sildenafil is cytosolic and riociguat is membrane-bound, producing differential downstream effects on MLCK and BKCa channels","E":"Riociguat inhibits both PDE5 and PDE3, accounting for its broader efficacy, while sildenafil selectively inhibits PDE5; both require endogenous NO for activation of sGC as a cofactor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This is a clinically important mechanism distinction: sildenafil inhibits PDE5 (which degrades cGMP), while riociguat directly stimulates sGC (which synthesizes cGMP). Riociguat has a dual mechanism: (1) it sensitizes sGC to low levels of endogenous NO, allowing vasodilation even when NO bioavailability is impaired (as in endothelial dysfunction of PAH), and (2) it activates sGC directly in an NO-independent manner by stabilizing the enzyme in an active conformation. This means riociguat works even when endogenous NO production is severely compromised — a theoretical advantage over PDE5i in advanced PAH. Critically, the combination of riociguat + PDE5i (or riociguat + nitrates) is CONTRAINDICATED because both increase cGMP via different mechanisms, risking profound hypotension. Riociguat is also approved for chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension (CTEPH) — unlike sildenafil.","boardPrep":"Katzung Ch. 19 — sGC stimulators: riociguat, vericiguat. Two mechanisms: (1) NO-sensitizing — enhances sGC response to endogenous NO; (2) NO-independent — directly stimulates sGC even without NO. Contraindications: nitrates, PDE5i, other sGC stimulators (all ↑ cGMP). FDA indications: riociguat = PAH (Group 1) + CTEPH (Group 4); vericiguat = HFrEF. Compare: sildenafil = PDE5i (↓ cGMP degradation); riociguat = sGC stimulator (↑ cGMP synthesis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 3 — Group 1 PAH, the pulmonary hypertension class treated with PDE5 inhibitors and sGC stimulators","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Nitric Oxide — Pharmacology, Physiologic Role, and Medications for Pulmonary Hypertension","batch":2,"stem":"A neonate with persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn (PPHN) is treated with inhaled nitric oxide at 20 ppm. On day 3, the nursing staff notes that the infant's blood is a noticeably darker shade of brown during suctioning. Pulse oximetry reads 94%, but co-oximetry reveals 18% methemoglobin. Which of the following correctly explains the biochemical mechanism of this complication?","choices":{"A":"Inhaled NO diffuses into pulmonary capillaries and reacts with oxyhemoglobin (Fe2+–O2) to form methemoglobin (Fe3+), which cannot bind oxygen, plus nitrate (NO3–); high inspired NO concentrations saturate this scavenging reaction, allowing significant MetHb accumulation","B":"Inhaled NO oxidizes the iron center of cytochrome c oxidase from Fe3+ to Fe4+, impairing mitochondrial electron transport; the resulting hypoxic stress drives conformational changes in erythrocyte hemoglobin from T-state to a non-oxygen-binding R-state","C":"NO reacts with molecular oxygen in the alveolar space to form nitrogen dioxide (NO2), which is inhaled and directly oxidizes hemoglobin iron from Fe2+ to Fe3+ through a Fenton-like reaction independent of NO itself","D":"Inhaled NO is converted to nitrite (NO2–) by pulmonary endothelial cells and exported to erythrocytes, where nitrite oxidizes the heme iron; methemoglobin formation is thus a delayed, endothelium-dependent process","E":"High-dose inhaled NO competitively displaces oxygen from all four heme subunits of hemoglobin simultaneously, converting the oxyhemoglobin to a stable NO–hemoglobin adduct that spectrophotometrically resembles methemoglobin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"When inhaled NO diffuses from the alveolus into the pulmonary capillary, it encounters oxyhemoglobin (HbO2, where Fe2+ is bound to O2). The rapid reaction: NO + HbO2 → methemoglobin (MetHb, Fe3+) + NO3– (nitrate). This is the primary inactivation mechanism that prevents systemic NO delivery, but it simultaneously oxidizes hemoglobin iron from the ferrous (Fe2+) to the ferric (Fe3+) state. MetHb cannot carry oxygen (Fe3+ has no oxygen-binding capacity), and also does not release properly in the tissues (shifts oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve left). Some MetHb formation is inevitable during iNO therapy, and at higher concentrations or with prolonged exposure clinically significant methemoglobinemia may occur. Neonates are especially vulnerable because: (1) fetal hemoglobin is more susceptible to oxidation, and (2) methemoglobin reductase (NADH-cytochrome b5 reductase) activity is lower in newborns. The chocolate-brown color of MetHb-containing blood is a classic clinical finding.","boardPrep":"Katzung Ch. 19 — iNO toxicity: (1) Methemoglobinemia — NO + HbO2 → MetHb (Fe3+, cannot bind O2) + NO3–; monitor co-oximetry; treat severe MetHb with methylene blue. (2) Rebound pulmonary HTN — abrupt discontinuation → acute RV failure; taper slowly. (3) NO2 formation — NO + O2 → NO2 (lung irritant); minimize inspired O2 during iNO therapy. (4) Peroxynitrite — NO + O2•– → ONOO– (DNA/lipid damage in sepsis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 11 — Methemoglobin from inhaled NO: a hemoglobin-level (transport) cause of hypoxia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Carbonic Anhydrase Inhibitors — Pharmacology and Therapeutic Role in Acute Mountain Sickness","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old high-altitude porter has taken acetazolamide daily for several months to prevent acute mountain sickness. He presents with colicky right flank pain and hematuria, and imaging shows a renal stone. According to Katzung, which composition of stone is favored by chronic carbonic anhydrase inhibition, and why?","choices":{"A":"Uric acid, because the drug acidifies the urine and raises urate excretion","B":"Cystine, because the drug blocks proximal reabsorption of dibasic amino acids","C":"Struvite, because the drug promotes urease-producing urinary infection","D":"Calcium oxalate, because the drug blocks distal calcium reabsorption entirely","E":"Calcium phosphate, as phosphaturia and hypercalciuria meet alkaline urine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Katzung lists renal stones among the toxicities of carbonic anhydrase inhibitors: phosphaturia and hypercalciuria occur during the bicarbonaturic response, renal excretion of solubilizing factors such as citrate may decline with chronic use, and calcium phosphate salts are relatively insoluble at alkaline pH, so stone formation from these salts is enhanced. The drug alkalinizes rather than acidifies the urine; indeed, raising urine pH is used to keep uric acid and cystine soluble, so neither uric acid nor cystine stones are favored. Acetazolamide does not block amino acid reabsorption or promote urease-producing infection. Calcium oxalate is not the salt Katzung identifies. Other toxicities include hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis, renal K+ wasting, drowsiness and paresthesias, and hypersensitivity reactions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acetazolamide toxicities — hyperchloremic (normal anion gap) metabolic acidosis, renal K+ wasting, calcium phosphate stones (alkaline urine + phosphaturia + hypercalciuria), paresthesias, sulfonamide hypersensitivity; contraindicated in cirrhosis (less NH4+ trapping → hyperammonemia). COMLEX: For AMS prophylaxis, Katzung notes 125 mg twice daily is as effective as higher doses.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — renal compensation for the respiratory alkalosis of altitude, which acetazolamide hastens","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Carbonic Anhydrase Inhibitors — Pharmacology and Therapeutic Role in Acute Mountain Sickness","batch":2,"stem":"A pharmacology professor explains to students that acetazolamide's diuretic effect is 'self-limiting,' yet its benefit in acute mountain sickness is sustained throughout altitude exposure. Which of the following best explains this apparent paradox?","choices":{"A":"The diuretic effect self-limits because renal tubular cells upregulate alternative HCO3– reabsorption pathways (e.g., pendrin) within 48–72 hours, while the respiratory effects persist because brainstem central chemoreceptors do not develop the same compensatory adaptation","B":"The diuresis wanes because bicarbonaturia depletes body HCO3– stores, leaving less filtered HCO3– to lose; the ventilatory benefit needs only a lower blood and CSF pH, which persists as long as the drug is taken and HCO3– stays low","C":"Acetazolamide is rapidly eliminated renally, causing intermittent peak-and-trough cycling; diuretic effects occur at peak concentrations while the pH shift driving ventilation is maintained by the metabolite dehydroacetazolamide during the trough","D":"The kidney compensates for bicarbonaturia by activating the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, restoring NaCl and water reabsorption distally; the respiratory center, lacking aldosterone receptors, cannot make the same adaptive correction","E":"Acetazolamide's diuretic effect self-limits only in patients with normal renal function; in the volume-depleted state at altitude, persistent diuresis causes ongoing bicarbonaturia that coincidentally maintains the ventilatory stimulus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This question tests understanding of the mechanism behind self-limitation versus sustained benefit. CA inhibition prevents HCO3– reabsorption in the proximal tubule. Initially, large amounts of HCO3– spill into the tubular fluid, drawing Na+ and water with it (osmotic effect) → diuresis. As total body HCO3– stores deplete (since HCO3– can only be regenerated slowly from CO2 via the now-inhibited CA), there is simply less HCO3– to excrete — the diuretic substrate is exhausted. Diuresis is therefore self-limited (First Aid 2025: \"self-limited NaHCO3 diuresis\"). However, the acidic pH state persists because the body's HCO3– stores remain low. This low HCO3– state is precisely what stimulates peripheral and central chemoreceptors to increase ventilation — it doesn't require ongoing bicarbonate loss, only maintained low pH. Thus the ventilatory benefit outlasts the diuresis, making acetazolamide an effective sustained AMS prophylactic even after urinary output normalizes.","boardPrep":"Katzung Ch. 15 — Self-limiting diuresis: substrate depletion as HCO3– stores exhaust. Sustained AMS benefit: persistent metabolic acidosis (↓ HCO3–) → ongoing ↑ ventilation. Clinical implication: acetazolamide is started before ascent so the acidosis is present on arrival at altitude. Diuretic effect ≠ antiedema effect; AMS benefit ≠ diuretic benefit — separate mechanisms.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Renal HCO3− excretion and hyperventilatory compensation during altitude acclimatization","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Carbonic Anhydrase Inhibitors — Pharmacology and Therapeutic Role in Acute Mountain Sickness","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old adventure traveler with a documented sulfonamide allergy (rash with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole) plans an expedition to Mount Everest base camp (17,600 ft). He asks his travel medicine physician about AMS prophylaxis. Which of the following represents the most clinically sound approach to this patient?","choices":{"A":"Acetazolamide's sulfonamide group makes cross-reactivity certain, so no drug prophylaxis is possible and he must rely on a slow ascent schedule alone, without any medication","B":"Acetazolamide is a sulfonamide derivative with possible cross-reactivity; dexamethasone is an alternative AMS prophylactic that works through anti-inflammatory/anti-edema effects but does not aid acclimatization","C":"Methazolamide, a related CA inhibitor, does not contain a sulfonamide group and represents the preferred CA inhibitor for patients with sulfa allergy who require AMS prophylaxis","D":"Topical dorzolamide eye drops can be administered orally for AMS prophylaxis in sulfa-allergic patients, achieving equivalent plasma CA inhibition without the systemic sulfonamide exposure","E":"The sulfonamide cross-reactivity concern applies only to patients with IgE-mediated anaphylactic sulfa reactions; a patient with only a cutaneous rash to TMP-SMX can receive acetazolamide safely without precaution"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Acetazolamide is a sulfonamide derivative (contains the –SO2NH2 group), so a documented sulfa allergy raises a cross-reactivity question. The Wilderness Medical Society guideline (Luks 2019; AAFP 2020 summary) says an allergic reaction is extremely unlikely, suggests a supervised trial before travel, and makes acetazolamide a contraindication only after sulfonamide anaphylaxis or Stevens-Johnson syndrome. Dexamethasone is an established alternative that prevents AMS and HACE in adults at moderate to high risk, but it does NOT aid acclimatization, which only acetazolamide does (same guideline). Cross-reactivity is not certain and drug prophylaxis remains possible, so relying on slow ascent alone ignores the dexamethasone option. Methazolamide IS a sulfonamide derivative, so it is not a sulfa-free alternative. Dorzolamide is also a sulfonamide and is not formulated or dosed for systemic use orally. Limiting the contraindication to IgE-mediated anaphylaxis misses Stevens-Johnson syndrome, and the guideline still advises a supervised trial rather than use without precaution.","boardPrep":"Katzung Ch. 15 — Acetazolamide contraindications: (1) Sulfonamide allergy (structural sulfonamide — cross-reactivity); (2) Cirrhosis (hepatic encephalopathy from NH3 ↑); (3) Severe renal failure; (4) Hypokalemia. AMS alternatives if acetazolamide contraindicated: dexamethasone (anti-inflammatory, does NOT improve acclimatization, rebound risk). Ibuprofen also shown in studies to reduce AMS headache but not prophylaxis-level evidence.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 6 — HACE, the severe end of altitude illness: hypoxic cerebral vasodilation causes the edema (Guyton), and dexamethasone is the guideline drug for it (Wilderness Medical Society)","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cross-reactivity is not certain","C":"Methazolamide IS a sulfonamide","D":"Dorzolamide is also a sulfonam","E":"Limiting the contraindication to IgE"}},{"id":"sdl38_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Opioids in Cough Suppression — Role and Differentiation of Medication Derivatives","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy weighing 16 kg undergoes tonsillectomy and adenoidectomy. Post-operatively, he is prescribed codeine-acetaminophen for pain control. On post-operative day 2, he develops progressive somnolence, miosis, bradypnea (8 breaths/min), and cyanosis requiring ICU transfer for respiratory depression. Genetic testing reveals CYP2D6 ultrarapid metabolizer (UM) phenotype. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of his toxicity?","choices":{"A":"The acetaminophen component of the combination product undergoes CYP2D6-mediated conversion to a hepatotoxic metabolite (NAPQI) at an accelerated rate in ultrarapid metabolizers, triggering acute liver failure and secondary metabolic encephalopathy","B":"CYP2D6 poor metabolizers cannot convert codeine to morphine, leading to codeine accumulation; at toxic concentrations, codeine itself binds μ-opioid receptors with higher affinity than morphine, causing respiratory depression","C":"CYP2D6 ultrarapid metabolizers rapidly eliminate codeine via glucuronidation, but the glucuronide metabolite retains full μ-opioid activity and accumulates to toxic levels in pediatric patients with immature renal clearance","D":"CYP2D6 ultrarapid metabolizers convert codeine to morphine at an accelerated rate; standard pediatric doses of codeine produce morphine plasma concentrations equivalent to overdose levels, causing profound μ-opioid–mediated respiratory depression","E":"Post-operative inflammatory mediators upregulate μ-opioid receptor expression in the brainstem respiratory center; standard codeine doses produce greater-than-expected respiratory depression due to receptor sensitization rather than pharmacokinetic variation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Codeine is an inactive prodrug that requires CYP2D6-mediated O-demethylation to convert it to morphine — the active μ-opioid agonist responsible for both analgesia and antitussive effects. In ultrarapid metabolizers (UM), carrying duplicate or multiplied CYP2D6 gene copies, codeine is converted to morphine much faster and more completely than in normal metabolizers. A standard pediatric dose of codeine generates morphine plasma concentrations equivalent to a morphine overdose. Children are particularly vulnerable due to: (1) immature respiratory center μ-opioid sensitivity, (2) smaller body mass amplifying effective dose per kg of morphine generated, and (3) post-surgical CNS vulnerability. Katzung (Ch 31) notes case reports of respiratory depression and death in ultrarapid metabolizers, so routine use of codeine, especially in pediatric age groups, is being eliminated. Poor metabolizers (PM), by contrast, have minimal CYP2D6 activity and derive little analgesic effect from codeine (it remains largely unconverted to morphine).","boardPrep":"Katzung Ch. 31 — Codeine pharmacogenomics: prodrug → CYP2D6 → morphine. Ultrarapid metabolizers (UM): duplication of CYP2D6 → 'overdose' morphine generation from standard dose. FDA black box warning: codeine contraindicated post-tonsillectomy/adenoidectomy in children. UM genotype is most common in Ethiopians and Saudi Arabians, up to one-third of individuals (Katzung Ch 4). Poor metabolizers (PM): ~5–10% of Europeans, ~1% of Asians (Katzung Ch 5) — little analgesic/antitussive effect from codeine. Alternative: a non-opioid regimen (acetaminophen/ibuprofen) after pediatric tonsillectomy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 43 Obj 1 — Host pulmonary defenses: cough reflex, mucociliary clearance; opioid-mediated cough suppression increases lower respiratory tract vulnerability to bacterial superinfection","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Poor metabolizers (PM), by con"}},{"id":"sdl38_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Opioids in Cough Suppression — Role and Differentiation of Medication Derivatives","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old male is brought to the emergency department by his friends after ingesting large amounts of an OTC cough syrup at a party. He is agitated, confused, and appears dissociated from his environment. Vitals: HR 128 bpm, BP 148/92 mmHg, T 38.2°C. Examination reveals horizontal nystagmus, hyperreflexia, and slurred speech. Urine drug screen is negative for opioids and benzodiazepines. Which agent is most likely responsible, and what is its primary mechanism at high doses?","choices":{"A":"Diphenhydramine; anticholinergic toxidrome from OTC cold preparations causes confusion and agitation via muscarinic receptor blockade, with tachycardia and hyperthermia from peripheral sympathomimetic rebound","B":"Pseudoephedrine; adrenergic stimulation from decongestant ingredient causes hypertension, tachycardia, and hyperthermia; CNS effects from blood-brain barrier penetration include agitation and confusion mimicking sympathomimetic toxidrome","C":"Hydrocodone; opioid intoxication from high-dose cough syrup produces CNS and respiratory depression, miosis, and decreased bowel sounds consistent with μ-opioid receptor overactivation","D":"Guaifenesin; osmotic irritation of the gastric mucosa at high doses triggers a vagal reflex causing alternating agitation and sedation with autonomic instability via the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus","E":"Dextromethorphan; at supratherapeutic doses it acts as an NMDA receptor antagonist producing dissociative symptoms analogous to phencyclidine (PCP) and ketamine, including agitation, nystagmus, and psychomotor dissociation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This case describes 'robo-tripping' or 'DXM abuse' — intentional ingestion of large amounts of dextromethorphan-containing OTC cough syrups (e.g., Robitussin DM, NyQuil). At therapeutic doses, DXM acts as an NMDA antagonist at sub-threshold concentrations and a mild serotonin reuptake inhibitor. At supratherapeutic doses, its NMDA antagonism becomes clinically manifest, producing a dissociative syndrome indistinguishable from PCP or ketamine intoxication: agitation, confusion, nystagmus, hyperreflexia, tachycardia, hypertension, and hyperthermia. The urine drug screen is negative because standard opiate immunoassays do not detect dextromethorphan (it is not an opioid — different chemical class from morphine/oxycodone). Diphenhydramine causes anticholinergic syndrome (dry, hot, blind, mad); pseudoephedrine causes a sympathomimetic syndrome but without nystagmus; hydrocodone would cause miosis, CNS depression, and respiratory depression — the opposite presentation.","boardPrep":"Katzung Ch. 31 — DXM abuse ('robo-tripping'): NMDA antagonism at high doses → dissociative syndrome (agitation, nystagmus, hallucinations, psychosis). NOT detected on standard opioid UDS. CYP2D6 converts DXM → dextrorphan (active NMDA antagonist; PM patients have more DXM — more toxic at same dose). Usual antitussive DXM dose 15–30 mg three or four times daily (Katzung Ch 31); toxicity appears at many times that dose. Treatment: supportive; benzodiazepines for agitation; no specific reversal agent.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 43 Obj 1 — Host pulmonary defenses: cough reflex, mucociliary clearance; opioid-mediated cough suppression increases lower respiratory tract vulnerability to bacterial superinfection","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Diphenhydramine causes anticho"}},{"id":"sdl38_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Opioids in Cough Suppression — Role and Differentiation of Medication Derivatives","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with COPD is admitted with community-acquired pneumonia (CAP) and a severe, productive cough with purulent sputum. Her daughter brings in a bottle of codeine cough syrup 'to help Mom rest' and insists the cough is exhausting her. Her pulmonologist declines to prescribe codeine. Which of the following best explains why an opioid antitussive is inappropriate specifically because her cough is productive?","choices":{"A":"Codeine undergoes CYP2D6 metabolism to morphine, and patients with COPD have elevated CYP2D6 activity from chronic smoking; excessive morphine production in this population causes disproportionate respiratory depression even at standard doses","B":"In a productive infection, cough is a key host defense that clears bacteria-laden secretions; suppressing it with an opioid promotes secretion retention and mucus plugging, allowing bacterial proliferation and worsening the pneumonia","C":"Codeine is metabolized to norcodeine by CYP3A4, which accumulates in patients with COPD-related hypoxia; norcodeine acts as a μ-opioid partial agonist that competitively blocks endogenous endorphin-mediated bronchodilation","D":"Respiratory depression from codeine would lower minute ventilation, reducing CO2 elimination; in COPD patients who rely on hypoxic drive rather than hypercapnic drive for ventilation, CO2 retention accelerates respiratory acidosis and triggers respiratory failure","E":"Opioids stimulate mast cell degranulation in the bronchial mucosa, triggering histamine release that causes bronchoconstriction; in COPD patients with already limited expiratory flow reserve, this bronchoconstriction further reduces FEV1 and worsens air trapping"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The cough reflex, while uncomfortable, serves a vital host defense function. In productive lower respiratory tract infections, cough expels bacteria-laden mucus from the bronchial tree that cannot be cleared by mucociliary transport alone. Suppressing this reflex with opioids in a patient with purulent pneumonia creates a biologic trap: (1) secretions accumulate → bacterial load increases in airways → worsened local infection; (2) mucus plugging can cause lobar atelectasis → consolidation → reduced gas exchange; (3) secretion retention promotes spread to previously unaffected lobes. The physiologic rule: suppress the cough reflex ONLY for dry, non-productive cough (where secretion clearance is not at issue), NEVER for productive cough from lower respiratory infection. The hypoxic-drive option (COPD + CO2 retention) is a real concern with opioids in COPD but is a pharmacokinetic/pharmacodynamic concern — not the primary rationale for avoiding antitussives in productive cough specifically.","boardPrep":"Katzung Ch. 31 — Opioid antitussives contraindicated in: productive cough (impairs secretion clearance), COPD/respiratory compromise (CO2 retention risk), acute asthma, concurrent MAOI use (DXM), post-tonsillectomy in children (codeine). Appropriate use: dry, non-productive cough disrupting sleep or causing pain (e.g., post-viral cough, post-surgical). Remember: cough = host defense in infections; its suppression = pathogen retention = worsened pneumonia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 43 Obj 1 — Host pulmonary defenses: cough reflex, mucociliary clearance; opioid-mediated cough suppression increases lower respiratory tract vulnerability to bacterial superinfection","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"The hypoxic-drive option (COPD"}}],"39":[{"id":"sdl39_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Lung Transplantation — Indications, Complications, Pathological Features, and Morphology","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman with cystic fibrosis and Burkholderia cepacia complex pulmonary colonization is evaluated for lung transplantation as her FEV1 has declined to 22% predicted. Her transplant team explains that she requires bilateral lung transplantation rather than a single-lung procedure. Which of the following best explains the mandatory bilateral approach in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Single-lung transplantation is technically impossible in CF patients because bilateral pleural adhesions from recurrent infections prevent safe unilateral pneumonectomy","B":"The hypersecretory phenotype of CF affects all mucosal surfaces, including the trachea and bronchi proximal to any anastomosis, necessitating bilateral removal to prevent mucus obstruction","C":"CF transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR) dysfunction persists in any remaining native airway epithelium and would contaminate mucus produced by the transplanted lung via retrograde flow","D":"CF-related cor pulmonale creates a fixed elevation of pulmonary vascular resistance that can only be corrected by bilateral lung replacement restoring normal total pulmonary vascular cross-section","E":"CF causes chronic infection of both lungs; a retained native lung would act as a reservoir of pathogenic organisms that could seed the new graft and cause infection and failure"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In cystic fibrosis, chronic bilateral suppurative infection — most commonly with Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Burkholderia cepacia complex — colonizes both lungs irreversibly. If a single lung transplant were performed, the remaining native CF lung would harbor these organisms and continuously expose the immunosuppressed transplant recipient to a high bacterial inoculum. Burkholderia cepacia complex is particularly concerning because it is resistant to many antibiotics and is associated with a rapidly fatal necrotizing pneumonia ('cepacia syndrome') in transplant recipients. This is why bilateral chronic pulmonary infection — seen in CF and bronchiectasis — is an absolute indication for bilateral lung transplantation rather than single-lung transplant. By contrast, conditions such as COPD, IPF, and PAH can be managed with single-lung transplantation because no residual infection reservoir remains.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — Lung transplant: bilateral required when bilateral chronic infection present (CF, bronchiectasis) — removes infection reservoir. Single-lung transplant: benefits 2 recipients per donor but only appropriate for non-infectious conditions (COPD, IPF, PAH). Most common indications overall: COPD > IPF > CF > PAH. Median survival: 6 years; better with bilateral transplant and younger age.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — the clinical features of CF, including chronic Pseudomonas and B. cepacia infection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Lung Transplantation — Indications, Complications, Pathological Features, and Morphology","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old man undergoes single-lung transplantation for end-stage COPD. Four months postoperatively, he develops fever, dyspnea, and new bilateral infiltrates. A transbronchial biopsy is obtained. Histopathology shows perivascular mononuclear infiltrates with lymphocytes and plasma cells surrounding small vessels and bronchiolar submucosa without evidence of organisms on special stains. Which of the following best describes this process?","choices":{"A":"Hyperacute rejection — preformed anti-donor antibodies bind vascular endothelium immediately post-anastomosis, activating complement and causing thrombotic occlusion of graft vessels within hours","B":"Bronchiolitis obliterans syndrome — fibroproliferative obliteration of small airways by collagen deposition representing chronic allograft dysfunction that is irreversible once established","C":"Primary graft dysfunction — ischemia-reperfusion injury from the transplant procedure itself causing diffuse alveolar damage in the first 72 hours after transplantation","D":"Acute cellular rejection — CD4+ and CD8+ T-cell attack on the donor lung's vascular endothelium and airway epithelium, typically weeks to months after lung transplantation","E":"Post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder — EBV-driven oligoclonal B-cell proliferation forming a mass within the allograft parenchyma in the setting of T-cell immunosuppression"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acute cellular rejection occurs in virtually all transplant recipients to some degree, typically appearing weeks to months after surgery, though it can occur years later or when immunosuppression is decreased. The hallmark morphology is perivascular and/or peribronchiolar mononuclear infiltrates — predominantly lymphocytes and plasma cells, with few neutrophils and eosinophils — surrounding small vessels and/or in airway submucosa. This represents T-lymphocyte recognition of MHC antigens on donor endothelium and epithelium. Clinical presentation (fever, dyspnea, infiltrates) overlaps significantly with pneumonia, requiring biopsy for definitive diagnosis. Unlike chronic rejection/BOS (which is irreversible fibrosis), acute cellular rejection generally responds to augmented immunosuppression (high-dose corticosteroids). Hyperacute rejection would occur immediately post-transplant and is now largely prevented by crossmatching.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — Acute rejection: timing = weeks to months (or any time immunosuppression ↓). Morphology = perivascular + peribronchiolar lymphocytes/plasma cells. Diagnosis = transbronchial biopsy (same presentation as infection — biopsy required). Treatment = IV methylprednisolone pulse. Acute airway rejection = presumed forerunner of BOS/chronic rejection. Compare: chronic rejection (BOS) = irreversible, fibrotic small airway obliteration; ≥50% of patients by 3–5 years.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 2 — pneumonia in the immunocompromised host, the key differential after transplant","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hyperacute rejection would occ","B":"Unlike chronic rejection/BOS ("}},{"id":"sdl39_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Lung Transplantation — Indications, Complications, Pathological Features, and Morphology","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with a history of bilateral lung transplantation for idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis 4 years ago presents with progressive cough and dyspnea over 8 months. PFTs show an irreversible decline in FEV1 from 87% predicted (at 1 year post-transplant) to 51% predicted (current). High-resolution CT shows air trapping and bronchial wall thickening. Bronchoscopic biopsy samples are negative for infection. Which of the following best characterizes the underlying pathological process?","choices":{"A":"Recurrence of idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis in the transplanted lung — a rare phenomenon in which the original fibrosing process re-establishes in the donor organ due to circulating profibrotic mediators from the recipient","B":"Bronchiolitis obliterans syndrome (BOS) — patchy fibroproliferative obliteration of small airways by fibrous scar tissue representing chronic lung allograft rejection; irreversible and associated with progressive respiratory failure","C":"Obliterative bronchitis from a new Mycoplasma pneumoniae superinfection — the FEV1 decline reflects obstructive small airway inflammation that will improve with macrolide therapy","D":"Acute-on-chronic rejection — recent reduction in tacrolimus trough levels has triggered a new episode of T-cell–mediated endothelialitis superimposed on stable chronic allograft dysfunction","E":"Lymphocytic bronchiolitis — a less severe form of airway rejection characterized by submucosal lymphocytic infiltrates that is reversible with corticosteroid augmentation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bronchiolitis obliterans syndrome (BOS) is the major manifestation of chronic lung allograft rejection and represents a significant problem in at least half of all lung transplant recipients by 3–5 years posttransplant. Pathologically, it is characterized by patchy partial or complete obliteration of small airways (bronchioles) by subepithelial fibrosis — fibroblasts and fibromyxoid tissue progressively occlude the airway lumen. The patchy distribution makes transbronchial biopsy unreliable for diagnosis, so BOS is often diagnosed clinically by the FEV1 decline. The FEV1 decline is irreversible — BOS cannot be reversed once established, though progression may be slowed. Long-term, bronchiectasis and pulmonary fibrosis may develop. BOS is distinct from acute cellular rejection, which is lymphocyte-predominant infiltration (not fibrosis) and is at least partially reversible. The clinical triad — chronic FEV1 decline, air trapping on CT, absence of infection — strongly supports BOS.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — BOS (chronic rejection): present in ≥50% of patients by 3–5 years. Morphology = fibrotic obliteration of bronchioles (patchy → difficult transbronchial biopsy diagnosis). Clinical = progressive FEV1 decline, irreversible. Prognosis = poor; cannot be reversed; progression may be slowed. Associated with: prior acute airway rejection episodes, CMV pneumonitis, community respiratory virus infections. Distinguished from acute rejection by: chronicity, fibrosis (not lymphocytes), irreversibility.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 4 — FEV1 and FEV1/FVC decline with airway obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Lung Cancer Risk Factors — Tobacco, Industrial Hazards, Air Pollution, and Molecular Carcinogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with a 40 pack-year smoking history (2 packs/day for 20 years) is diagnosed with squamous cell lung carcinoma. During counseling, his oncologist explains the quantitative relationship between his smoking history and lung cancer risk. Which of the following best represents the magnitude of lung cancer risk elevation in habitual heavy smokers compared to nonsmokers?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 5-fold greater risk — corresponding to the relative risk conferred by environmental (secondhand) smoke exposure accumulated over a lifetime of occupational and household exposure","B":"Approximately 25-fold greater risk — the relative risk conferred by occupational asbestos exposure alone in nonsmoking workers with 20+ years of insulation work","C":"Approximately 10-fold greater risk — representing the relative risk elevation attributable to first-degree family history of lung cancer independent of shared smoking behavior","D":"Approximately 200-fold greater risk — the exponential amplification seen only in patients with polymorphic CYP1A1 ultra-rapid tobacco carcinogen activation variants","E":"Approximately 60-fold greater risk — reflecting a nearly linear correlation between pack-years of cigarette exposure and lung cancer frequency across all histologic subtypes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The quantitative smoking-lung cancer relationship is one of the most replicated findings in oncologic epidemiology. Habitual heavy smokers (approximately 2 packs/day for 20 years = 40 pack-years) have a roughly 60-fold greater risk of developing lung cancer compared to lifelong nonsmokers. This risk follows a nearly linear correlation with pack-years — doubling pack-years approximately doubles risk. About 80% of all lung cancers occur in current or recent former smokers. Critically, however, only 10–15% of smokers develop lung cancer over their lifetime, demonstrating that additional genetic susceptibility factors (e.g., polymorphic P-450 carcinogen-activating enzymes, DNA repair capacity) modulate absolute risk. The 5-fold figure is more consistent with asbestos exposure in nonsmokers; the 25-fold asbestos figure is incorrect (it is 5-fold for non-smokers; the 55-fold figure applies to asbestos-exposed smokers). Secondhand smoke risk is much lower (estimated ~3000 deaths/year in U.S. nonsmokers) but still nonzero.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — Smoking risk: 60× risk in heavy habitual smokers (2 PPD × 20 years) vs. nonsmokers. ~80% of lung cancers in smokers/recent ex-smokers. Nearly linear pack-year correlation. Only 10–15% of smokers develop cancer — genetic cofactors required. Secondhand smoke: no safe level; ~3000 U.S. nonsmoker deaths/year. Cessation: ↓ risk over time but never fully baseline; persistent genomic changes in bronchial epithelium.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 5 — squamous cell carcinoma, a subtype closely tied to smoking","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Lung Cancer Risk Factors — Tobacco, Industrial Hazards, Air Pollution, and Molecular Carcinogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"Two brothers each smoked two packs a day for 30 years. One develops squamous cell carcinoma of the lung at 61; the other, now 75, has never had lung cancer. Robbins notes that lung cancer develops in only 10% to 15% of smokers and attributes part of this variation to inherited differences. Which inherited difference is cited as increasing a smoker's lung cancer risk?","choices":{"A":"Germline loss of one α1-antitrypsin allele, PiMZ, allowing protease damage that promotes carcinoma","B":"P-450 monooxygenase variants with a greater capacity to activate procarcinogens in cigarette smoke","C":"CFTR carrier status that thickens bronchial mucus and prolongs carcinogen contact with the epithelium","D":"HLA-DRB1 alleles that impair T-cell surveillance of mutated bronchial epithelial cells","E":"Inherited activating EGFR mutations, which explain most lung cancers arising in heavy smokers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Robbins explains that the mutagenic effect of carcinogens in smoke is modified by genetic variants: many procarcinogens are converted into carcinogens by the highly polymorphic P-450 monooxygenase system, and smokers with P-450 variants that have an increased capacity to activate procarcinogens in cigarette smoke incur a greater risk; similarly, individuals whose lymphocytes show more chromosomal breakage after exposure to tobacco carcinogens (mutagen sensitivity genotype) have a greater than 10-fold higher risk, presumably from variation in DNA repair genes. α1-Antitrypsin deficiency is linked to panacinar emphysema, not cited as the lung cancer modifier. CFTR carrier status and HLA-DRB1 alleles are not the variants Robbins names (HLA-DRB1 subtypes are discussed with Goodpasture syndrome). EGFR mutations are somatic, not inherited, and characterize adenocarcinomas of never-smokers rather than most smokers' cancers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Smoking risk is dose-related (≈60× for 2 packs/day × 20 years), yet only 10%–15% of smokers develop lung cancer; P-450 variants that activate procarcinogens and a mutagen-sensitivity (DNA repair) genotype raise risk. Other risks: asbestos (5×; 55× with smoking), uranium (4× nonsmoking miners; ~10× smoking miners), radon, secondhand smoke (~3000 U.S. deaths/yr). COMLEX: Cessation lowers risk but it may never return to baseline.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 6 — small cell carcinoma, the other subtype most strongly linked to smoking","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"α1-Antitrypsin deficiency is l"}},{"id":"sdl39_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Lung Cancer Risk Factors — Tobacco, Industrial Hazards, Air Pollution, and Molecular Carcinogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with a 30 pack-year smoking history quit smoking 10 years ago. She asks her pulmonologist whether her lung cancer risk has returned to that of a lifelong nonsmoker. Which of the following best characterizes the trajectory of lung cancer risk following smoking cessation?","choices":{"A":"Risk fully normalizes to nonsmoker baseline within 15 years of cessation, as normal stem cell turnover in the bronchial epithelium replaces all carcinogen-damaged cells with genetically intact progenitors","B":"The risk falls substantially after cessation but never returns to that of a lifelong nonsmoker, because carcinogen-induced genetic changes persist in the bronchial epithelium of former smokers for many years","C":"Risk paradoxically increases in the first 3 years after cessation due to withdrawal of nicotine's pro-apoptotic effect on pre-neoplastic bronchial cells, then steadily declines to nonsmoker level by year 10","D":"Risk remains permanently elevated at the same level as active smoking throughout life, because asbestos-like chrysotile fibers from cigarette filters persist in the lung parenchyma long after inhalation ceases","E":"Risk reduction after cessation is only seen for squamous cell carcinoma and small cell carcinoma; adenocarcinoma risk continues to rise after cessation due to KRAS mutation accumulation in type II pneumocytes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Smoking cessation is the most powerful intervention to reduce lung cancer risk, but risk reduction is incomplete and gradual. After cessation, the risk of lung cancer decreases progressively over time (Robbins Ch 15). However, the risk never fully returns to that of a lifelong nonsmoker, because carcinogen-induced genomic alterations — including mutations in TP53, KRAS, chromosomal deletions at 3p and 9p (CDKN2A locus) — persist in bronchial epithelial cells and basal stem cells of former smokers. These cells carry a 'field cancerization' signature that remains biologically active as a cancer substrate. Histologic studies of former smokers' bronchial biopsies show persistent squamous metaplasia and dysplasia years after cessation. Women appear more susceptible to tobacco carcinogens than men, achieving a greater relative risk per pack-year. The key clinical message: cessation reduces risk substantially (and should always be encouraged), but former smokers still warrant lung cancer screening (LDCT) according to USPSTF guidelines based on pack-year history and time since quitting.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — Cessation: risk ↓ over time but never baseline; genomic field cancerization persists. Women: more susceptible to tobacco carcinogens per pack-year. Pipe/cigar smokers: elevated but modest risk vs. cigarettes. Secondhand smoke: no safe level, ~3000 U.S. nonsmoker deaths/year. Field cancerization concept: widespread carcinogen-induced mutations in histologically normal-appearing epithelium surrounding cancers — explains multifocal and bilateral tumor development.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 3 — the molecular changes that tobacco carcinogens leave in the bronchial epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q07","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Lung Transplantation — Indications, Complications, Pathological Features, and Morphology","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man who received bilateral lung transplantation 8 months ago for cystic fibrosis presents with fever, dry cough, and bilateral ground-glass opacities on CT chest. He is CMV donor-positive/recipient-negative (D+/R–). Bronchoscopy with BAL reveals cytopathic effect showing 'owl-eye' inclusions in alveolar macrophages and epithelial cells. Which of the following best describes the timeline and pathogen most consistent with this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Cytomegalovirus (CMV) pneumonitis — major opportunistic infections peak 3–12 months posttransplant; D+/R– serostatus carries the highest risk because the recipient lacks CMV immunity and meets donor-derived latent virus","B":"Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia — this presentation at 8 months posttransplant indicates that TMP-SMX prophylaxis was not prescribed; owl-eye inclusions are a classic histologic feature of P. jirovecii cysts on GMS stain","C":"Bacterial pneumonia from Staphylococcus aureus — the most common infection in the immediate post-transplant period (first 2 weeks); bilateral ground-glass opacities are typical of early hematogenous S. aureus seeding from the anastomotic site","D":"Invasive pulmonary aspergillosis — Aspergillus species produce owl-eye inclusion bodies in macrophages when the hyphae undergo phagocytosis; the 8-month timeframe is beyond the highest-risk window for Aspergillus after neutrophil recovery","E":"Epstein-Barr virus pneumonitis — EBV reactivation causes a mononucleosis-like interstitial pneumonia within the allograft; owl-eye inclusions represent EBV-transformed B-lymphoblasts rather than viral cytopathic effect in epithelial cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"CMV pneumonitis is the classic opportunistic infection in the 3–12 month post-transplant window and represents the most important viral complication in solid organ transplantation. The 'owl-eye' intranuclear inclusion surrounded by a clear halo, within enlarged cells (cytomegaly), is pathognomonic for CMV infection and appears in alveolar macrophages, pneumocytes, and endothelial cells. D+/R– serostatus carries the highest risk because the recipient has no pre-existing antibodies to CMV and will encounter reactivating virus from the donor organ under immunosuppression. Prophylaxis with ganciclovir (or valganciclovir) has dramatically reduced CMV severity and frequency, and donor-recipient matching is now routinely performed. P. jirovecii is prevented with TMP-SMX prophylaxis and does NOT produce owl-eye inclusions (P. jirovecii organisms stain with GMS/silver stains as cup-shaped cysts). Bacterial infections predominate in the first few weeks. Aspergillus grows as hyphae and is not characterized by owl-eye inclusions. The infection timeline framework — bacterial (early) → CMV/Aspergillus/P. jirovecii (3–12 months) — is a classic boards concept.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — Post-transplant infection timeline: early (weeks) = bacterial. Critical window (3–12 mo) = CMV pneumonitis (owl-eye inclusions, D+/R– highest risk, ganciclovir prophylaxis); P. jirovecii (TMP-SMX prevents); Aspergillus/Candida (anastomotic site + lung). CMV: may occur less severely/rarely with prophylaxis; resistant strains emerging. EBV: infrequent B-cell lymphoma within allograft (separate from pneumonitis). SDL 43 Obj 2 — immunocompromised host pneumonia framework.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 43 Obj 2 — Immunocompromised host pneumonia: classification syndromes (CAP/HAP/Aspiration/Immunocompromised) + key opportunistic pathogens in post-transplant recipients","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Bacterial infections predomina","D":"Aspergillus grows as hyphae an"}},{"id":"sdl39_q08","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Lung Transplantation — Indications, Complications, Pathological Features, and Morphology","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with bilateral lung transplantation 5 years ago for COPD presents with progressive exertional dyspnea over 3 years. Spirometry shows an irreversible decline in FEV1 from 90% predicted (1 year posttransplant) to 48% predicted. Bronchoscopy with BAL is negative for organisms. An open-lung biopsy shows two bronchioles: the bronchiole on the left retains a patent lumen lined by intact epithelium, while the bronchiole on the right has its lumen nearly completely obliterated by dense fibroblastic and fibromyxoid connective tissue; the surrounding alveolar parenchyma is relatively preserved in both areas. Which of the following most accurately characterizes the prognosis and management of this pathological finding?","choices":{"A":"Bronchiolitis obliterans is fully reversible with high-dose intravenous methylprednisolone pulse therapy; the fibromyxoid tissue represents organizing fibrin that liquefies under steroid treatment, restoring airway patency within 4–6 weeks","B":"Bronchiolitis obliterans is a manifestation of Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection in immunocompromised hosts; azithromycin produces both antimicrobial benefit and anti-inflammatory effects that reliably normalize FEV1 within 3 months in transplant recipients","C":"The patchy distribution of lesions indicates that only approximately 50% of airways are affected; the unaffected airways will hypertrophy and expand to compensate, allowing FEV1 to stabilize without pharmacologic intervention","D":"Fibromyxoid airway plugging in transplant recipients represents cryptogenic organizing pneumonia (COP) rather than rejection; unlike bronchiolitis obliterans, COP is characteristically steroid-responsive and reversible with a 3–6 month prednisolone course","E":"Bronchiolitis obliterans (chronic rejection) is irreversible once fibrous obliteration is established; azithromycin or immunosuppression changes may slow progression, and it is the leading cause of late post-transplant mortality"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Bronchiolitis obliterans (BO), the pathological lesion underlying bronchiolitis obliterans syndrome (BOS), is characterized by subepithelial fibroproliferative tissue partially or completely obliterating bronchiolar lumens. Once mature collagenous fibrosis replaces the normal bronchiolar epithelium and submucosa, the structural destruction is permanent — the airway cannot regenerate. This irreversibility is a defining feature that separates BO from other airway lesions. Management focuses on slowing progression: azithromycin has anti-inflammatory properties beyond its antibiotic action and is used long-term; extracorporeal photopheresis, inhaled cyclosporine, and adjustment of baseline immunosuppression have shown modest benefit. BOS is the leading cause of death beyond 1 year posttransplant and represents the most significant barrier to improving long-term transplant outcomes. The patchy distribution of BO is diagnostically challenging but does not imply compensatory capacity — affected airways are lost permanently. COP (organizing pneumonia) is a clinically and morphologically distinct entity that IS steroid-responsive; it has an alveolar/peribronchiolar distribution of Masson bodies (intraluminal polyps), not bronchiolar fibrotic obliteration.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — BOS key facts: (1) Present in ≥50% of patients by 3–5 years. (2) Pathology = fibrous obliteration of bronchioles (patchy, transbronchial biopsy low sensitivity). (3) IRREVERSIBLE — cannot be reversed once established. (4) Progression may be slowed: azithromycin (anti-inflammatory), extracorporeal photopheresis. (5) Leading cause of late post-transplant mortality. Distinguish from COP: COP = Masson bodies in alveoli/peribronchiolar, steroid-responsive, reversible.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 43 Obj 2 — Immunocompromised host pneumonia: classification syndromes (CAP/HAP/Aspiration/Immunocompromised) + key opportunistic pathogens in post-transplant recipients","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Once mature collagenous fibros","D":"COP (organizing pneumonia) is "}},{"id":"sdl39_q09","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Lung Transplantation — Indications, Complications, Pathological Features, and Morphology","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man who received a single-lung transplant 2 years ago for idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis presents with a right upper lobe mass on CT chest. PET shows metabolic activity. Surgical resection reveals a large B-cell lymphoma within the transplanted lung. EBV in situ hybridization is strongly positive in the tumor cells. Which of the following best describes the pathogenesis of this complication?","choices":{"A":"Maintenance T-cell immunosuppression abrogates the cytotoxic T-lymphocyte surveillance that normally controls EBV-infected B lymphocytes, so EBV-driven B-cell proliferation proceeds unchecked within the allograft","B":"Donor-derived B lymphocytes harboring integrated EBV provirus migrate from the transplanted lung into the mediastinal lymphatics and undergo malignant transformation at the anastomotic site due to local ischemia-reperfusion mutagenesis","C":"Long-term tacrolimus therapy directly mutates the BCL2 gene in recipient B lymphocytes, causing EBV-independent B-cell lymphoma in the allograft; EBV seropositivity is a coincidental finding in immunocompromised patients","D":"EBV infects type II pneumocytes in the allograft, integrating its genome and driving EGFR pathway activation; the resulting lymphoepithelioma-like carcinoma is EBV-positive but arises from epithelial rather than B-cell precursors","E":"Azathioprine metabolites are directly incorporated into EBV viral DNA replication machinery, causing defective EBV virions that transform bystander B cells through an abortive lytic cycle independent of host immune surveillance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder (PTLD) arises because T-cell immunosuppression (calcineurin inhibitors, anti-metabolites) prevents normal cytotoxic T-lymphocyte (CTL) surveillance of EBV-infected B cells. In immunocompetent individuals, EBV establishes latent infection in B lymphocytes, and EBV-specific CTLs maintain tight control, preventing B-cell outgrowth. Under immunosuppression, this surveillance is lost, and EBV-driven B-cell proliferation can proceed unchecked — progressing from polyclonal hyperplasia to oligoclonal and monoclonal lymphoma (typically diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, DLBCL). Robbins describes it as an infrequent complication that most often arises within the lung allograft. The lung allograft is a common site because it contains donor lymphocytes and is a primary site of EBV reactivation. Treatment strategies include reducing immunosuppression (when possible), rituximab (anti-CD20), and chemotherapy for aggressive disease. PTLD is infrequent but clinically important.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — EBV-associated B-cell lymphoma (PTLD): infrequent complication; arises within lung allograft. Pathogenesis: ↓ T-cell immune surveillance (immunosuppression) → EBV-driven B-cell outgrowth. Morphology: EBV+ DLBCL. Treatment: ↓ immunosuppression + rituximab ± chemotherapy. Broader PTLD spectrum: polyclonal hyperplasia → oligoclonal → monoclonal lymphoma. Also test: EBV associations across Robbins = Burkitt lymphoma (t(8;14), MYC), NPC (nasopharyngeal carcinoma), PTLD, EBV+ gastric carcinoma, lymphoepithelioma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 43 Obj 2 — Immunocompromised host pneumonia: classification syndromes (CAP/HAP/Aspiration/Immunocompromised) + key opportunistic pathogens in post-transplant recipients","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q10","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Lung Cancer Risk Factors — Tobacco, Industrial Hazards, Air Pollution, and Molecular Carcinogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old male nonsmoker living in a rural area of Pennsylvania is diagnosed with squamous cell lung carcinoma. He has no occupational exposures, no family history of lung cancer, and has never smoked. An epidemiologist suspects a residential environmental exposure. His home has a basement with granite foundation walls, and household radon testing shows a markedly elevated level. Which of the following best explains the carcinogenic mechanism of this exposure?","choices":{"A":"Radon gas is chemically reactive and alkylates DNA bases in bronchial epithelial cells via nucleophilic addition; the resulting alkyl-guanine adducts mispair during replication and produce G→T transversions identical to those seen in tobacco-related lung cancer","B":"Radon decays to stable lead-210, a heavy metal that accumulates in macrophage lysosomes; chronic macrophage activation causes sustained TGF-β and IL-6 release, creating a fibroproliferative microenvironment that supports carcinogen-independent oncogenesis","C":"Radon-induced ionization of alveolar surfactant generates peroxidized lipid species (lipid hydroperoxides) that diffuse into bronchial epithelial nuclei and form DNA adducts with the structural properties of polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbon adducts from tobacco","D":"Radon-222, a radioactive noble gas from uranium-238 decay in soil, collects in enclosed spaces; its short-lived solid decay products (polonium-218, polonium-214) emit alpha particles that deposit on bronchial epithelium and cause DNA double-strand breaks","E":"Radon is a noble gas with no direct carcinogenic activity; its epidemiologic association with lung cancer in miners reflects confounding by silica dust and diesel exhaust co-exposure in uranium mines, without an independent radon-specific mechanism"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Radon-222 is a naturally occurring, odorless, colorless radioactive noble gas produced by the radioactive decay of uranium-238, which is abundant in granite and certain soil types. Because radon is a gas, it diffuses out of the soil and can accumulate in enclosed spaces — particularly well-insulated basements and lower floors. The carcinogenic hazard comes not from radon itself, but from its short-lived solid decay products — primarily polonium-218 and polonium-214 — which emit high-energy alpha particles. When inhaled, these alpha-emitting decay products deposit directly on bronchial epithelium. Alpha particles, despite limited penetrating power, transfer enormous energy per unit path length (high linear energy transfer, LET), causing extensive double-strand DNA breaks, chromosomal deletions, and complex genomic rearrangements in bronchial epithelial cells. This is the same mechanism by which uranium miner lung cancer occurs at higher radon concentrations occupationally. Robbins notes the concern that low-level exposure in well-insulated homes in areas with naturally high radon may also contribute to lung cancer.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — Radon: ubiquitous radioactive gas (222Rn from 238U decay in soil/granite). Alpha particle emission from decay products → bronchial epithelial DNA double-strand breaks. Epidemiology: uranium miners (occupational); well-insulated residential basements (environmental). Unlike tobacco, radon causes lung cancer in never-smokers. SDL 40 Obj 3 — carcinogen-driven TP53/CDKN2A/3p deletions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 3 — Lung cancer histologic subtypes and molecular features: TP53/RB mutations in SCLC; EGFR/ALK/BRAF in adenocarcinoma; carcinogen-driven genomic changes in SCC","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q11","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Lung Cancer Risk Factors — Tobacco, Industrial Hazards, Air Pollution, and Molecular Carcinogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A pathologist examines sequential bronchoscopic biopsies from a 61-year-old current smoker (55 pack-years) taken from the right main bronchus over a 10-year surveillance period. The biopsies show: Year 1 — normal pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium; Year 3 — basal cell hyperplasia; Year 6 — squamous metaplasia; Year 8 — squamous dysplasia (moderate); Year 10 — carcinoma in situ with TP53 overexpression. Which of the following best describes the relationship between smoking exposure and this histologic progression?","choices":{"A":"The progression is driven entirely by epigenetic silencing of CDKN2A via promoter hypermethylation induced by nicotine metabolites; TP53 overexpression represents a compensatory transcriptional response to hypermethylation rather than a somatic mutation","B":"Squamous metaplasia represents a protective adaptation — goblet cells replace ciliated cells to increase mucus production and trap carcinogens before they reach basal cells; TP53 overexpression at the carcinoma in situ stage indicates that this protective response has been overwhelmed","C":"Smoke carcinogens (polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons, nitrosamines) activated by polymorphic CYP P-450 enzymes form DNA adducts, so TP53 and CDKN2A (9p21) mutations accumulate; bronchial epithelial change correlates nearly linearly with pack-year exposure","D":"The progression from metaplasia to carcinoma in situ takes a fixed 15 years regardless of pack-year exposure, because each step requires clonal selection of a single driver mutation; the intensity of smoking only affects the probability of initiating basal cell hyperplasia, not the rate of subsequent steps","E":"TP53 overexpression at the carcinoma in situ stage indicates that p53 is performing its normal tumor-suppressive function by arresting DNA-damaged cells; the cells would only progress to invasive cancer if a concurrent RB deletion inactivated the second cell-cycle checkpoint"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This sequence — normal epithelium → basal cell hyperplasia → squamous metaplasia → squamous dysplasia → carcinoma in situ → invasive SCC — represents the well-characterized stepwise carcinogenic pathway in the central airways of smokers. Cigarette smoke contains over 60 known carcinogens, most importantly polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (e.g., benzo[a]pyrene) and tobacco-specific nitrosamines (e.g., NNK). These procarcinogens are bioactivated by polymorphic CYP P-450 enzymes (particularly CYP1A1, CYP1B1) in bronchial epithelial cells, forming reactive electrophilic metabolites that form covalent DNA adducts. Critically, there is a near-linear correlation between pack-year exposure and the extent of these histologic changes throughout the bronchial tree — field cancerization. Early molecular events include loss of 3p (containing tumor suppressor loci), loss of 9p21 (CDKN2A/p16), and TP53 mutations. TP53 overexpression on IHC reflects mutant p53 protein accumulation (mutant p53 is not degraded normally and accumulates in nuclei) — it is a marker of TP53 mutation, NOT normal p53 function. TP53 overexpression is seen in 10–50% of squamous dysplasias and 60–90% of squamous cell carcinoma in situ.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — Squamous carcinogenesis sequence: basal hyperplasia → squamous metaplasia → dysplasia (mild/moderate/severe) → CIS → invasive SCC. Early molecular events: 3p loss, 9p21 (CDKN2A) loss, 17p (TP53) loss. TP53 overexpression IHC = accumulation of MUTANT p53 (not normal function). CYP P-450 polymorphisms → variable carcinogen activation → variable cancer risk. CDKN2A (p16) mutations in 65% of SCC. FGFR1 amplification common in SCC. SDL 40 Obj 3 → full molecular profile by subtype.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 3 — Lung cancer histologic subtypes and molecular features: TP53/RB mutations in SCLC; EGFR/ALK/BRAF in adenocarcinoma; carcinogen-driven genomic changes in SCC","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"TP53 overexpression on IHC ref","E":"TP53 overexpression on IHC ref"}},{"id":"sdl39_q12","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Lung Cancer Risk Factors — Tobacco, Industrial Hazards, Air Pollution, and Molecular Carcinogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old East Asian woman who has never smoked presents with a 2.8-cm peripheral lung adenocarcinoma discovered incidentally on CT. Molecular profiling is ordered. Which of the following molecular profiles is most characteristic of lung adenocarcinoma in never-smoking East Asian females, and what is the clinical significance of identifying this mutation?","choices":{"A":"RB1 homozygous deletion — the defining molecular feature of all adenocarcinomas arising in never-smokers regardless of ethnicity; loss of RB function drives cell cycle dysregulation through CDK4/6-independent E2F transcriptional activation","B":"KRAS G12C mutation — the most common oncogenic driver in Western smokers (30% of adenocarcinomas); enriched in East Asian never-smokers due to higher dietary exposure to aflatoxin B1, which produces G→T transversions preferentially at codon 12 of KRAS","C":"EGFR activating mutation (exon 19 deletion or exon 21 L858R) — about 10–15% of lung adenocarcinomas overall but enriched in never-smoking Asian women; it predicts response to EGFR tyrosine kinase inhibitors (erlotinib, gefitinib, osimertinib)","D":"ALK rearrangement (EML4-ALK) — identified in 30–40% of never-smoking adenocarcinomas in Asian females due to a chromosome 2p inversion that is activated by non-tobacco oxidative stress; predicts response to crizotinib but not EGFR inhibitors","E":"TP53 gain-of-function R248W mutation — occurs almost exclusively in never-smokers because this hotspot is generated by deamination of methylcytosine at CpG dinucleotides rather than carcinogen adduct formation; predicts worse prognosis but no available targeted therapy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Lung adenocarcinoma in never-smokers has a fundamentally different molecular landscape than smoking-related lung cancers. EGFR activating mutations — most commonly exon 19 deletions and exon 21 L858R point mutations — are present in approximately 10–15% of all lung adenocarcinomas but are dramatically enriched in the never-smoking East Asian female population. This demographic clustering reflects a distinct carcinogenic pathway not driven by tobacco carcinogens. The clinical significance is profound: EGFR-mutant adenocarcinomas are exquisitely sensitive to EGFR tyrosine kinase inhibitors (TKIs) — erlotinib, gefitinib (1st generation), afatinib (2nd generation), and osimertinib (3rd generation, preferred because it also covers T790M resistance mutation). Molecular testing (next-generation sequencing) is now standard of care for all advanced adenocarcinomas. KRAS mutations are actually LESS common in never-smokers and are essentially absent in EGFR-mutant tumors. ALK rearrangements occur in 3–5% of all adenocarcinomas (not 30–40%) and also concentrate in younger never-smokers, but at much lower frequency than EGFR mutations. RB1 loss is characteristic of SCLC, not adenocarcinoma.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — Never-smoker adenocarcinoma: EGFR mutations (10–15% overall; enriched in Asian, female, never-smoker); KRAS mutations rare (present in ~30% of smoker adenocarcinomas, nearly absent in never-smokers); ALK rearrangement (3–5%); ROS1, RET, MET less common. EGFR TKI therapy: erlotinib/gefitinib/osimertinib; osimertinib preferred (covers T790M resistance). SDL 40 Obj 3 → full molecular subtype breakdown including TP53/RB in SCLC, EGFR/ALK/BRAF in adenocarcinoma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 3 — Lung cancer histologic subtypes and molecular features: TP53/RB mutations in SCLC; EGFR/ALK/BRAF in adenocarcinoma; carcinogen-driven genomic changes in SCC","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"KRAS mutations are actually LE"}}],"40":[{"id":"sdl40_q01","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Major Histologic Subtypes of Lung Cancer — Molecular Features (TP53, RB, EGFR, ALK, BRAF, KRAS) and Field Carcinogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"A 62-year-old male smoker (45 pack-years) is diagnosed with small cell lung carcinoma. His oncologist orders tumor molecular profiling. Which of the following genetic alterations is most characteristic of small cell carcinoma and distinguishes it from other lung cancer subtypes at the molecular level?","choices":{"A":"Activating mutations in EGFR (exon 19 deletion or exon 21 L858R) predicting sensitivity to tyrosine kinase inhibitors; EGFR mutations define the most targetable molecular subtype of lung cancer","B":"Amplification of FGFR1 (fibroblast growth factor receptor 1) and early CDKN2A/p16 inactivation at chromosome 9p21 — mutations that accumulate in the squamous carcinogenesis sequence and are specific to squamous cell carcinoma","C":"Near-universal biallelic inactivation of both TP53 and RB, the highest mutational burden among all lung cancer subtypes, frequent loss of chromosome 3p, and amplification of MYC-family oncogenes — a combination virtually absent in adenocarcinoma","D":"Oncogenic KRAS G12C mutations (roughly 30% of cases) acting downstream of receptor tyrosine kinases; KRAS mutations are nearly absent in never-smoker adenocarcinomas but common in smoker-related adenocarcinomas","E":"EML4-ALK rearrangement from a chromosome 2p inversion producing a constitutively active fusion kinase; ALK rearrangements occur in 3–5% of all adenocarcinomas and predict response to crizotinib and alectinib"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Small cell carcinoma (SCLC) has a distinctive and aggressive molecular profile dominated by near-universal inactivation of both TP53 and RB — both key cell-cycle checkpoints. RB inactivation is particularly noteworthy: acquisition of RB loss-of-function mutations can drive non-small cell carcinomas to transform into SCLC, underscoring RB's critical role in maintaining the non-neuroendocrine differentiation state. SCLC also carries the highest mutational burden among lung cancers, reflecting its extreme tobacco carcinogen exposure dependency (only ~1% occurs in nonsmokers). Chromosome 3p loss is an early event seen even in histologically normal bronchial epithelium of smokers. MYC-family amplification drives the rapid cell cycling and neuroendocrine phenotype. EGFR and ALK alterations are adenocarcinoma-specific. KRAS is a smoker-adenocarcinoma driver. FGFR1 and CDKN2A/p16 are SCC-specific.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — SCLC molecular: TP53 + RB (near-universal biallelic inactivation); MYC amplification; 3p loss (also in histologically normal epithelium). Highest mutational burden of lung cancers. RB loss = critical event → non-SCLC can transform to SCLC via RB mutation. Compare: Adenocarcinoma = EGFR (10–15%), KRAS (30%), ALK (3–5%); SCC = TP53, CDKN2A, FGFR1. Never-smoker adeno = EGFR enriched, KRAS absent.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 2 — tobacco smoking, to which small cell carcinoma is tied","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"EGFR and ALK alterations are a","D":"KRAS is a smoker-adenocarcinom","E":"EGFR and ALK alterations are a"}},{"id":"sdl40_q02","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Major Histologic Subtypes of Lung Cancer — Molecular Features (TP53, RB, EGFR, ALK, BRAF, KRAS) and Field Carcinogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"Robbins notes that about one-third of lung adenocarcinomas carry gain-of-function mutations in growth factor receptor signaling. Most of the listed drivers encode receptor tyrosine kinases, but some encode serine/threonine kinases or other signaling molecules that lie downstream of those receptors. Which of the following drivers encodes a downstream serine/threonine kinase rather than a receptor tyrosine kinase?","choices":{"A":"BRAF","B":"EGFR","C":"ALK","D":"ROS1","E":"RET"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Robbins lists the receptor tyrosine kinase drivers of adenocarcinoma as EGFR (10% to 15% of tumors in White people and a higher percentage of nonsmoking Asian females), ALK (3% to 5%), ROS1 (1%), MET (2% to 5%), and RET (1% to 2%); other tumors have gain-of-function mutations in serine/threonine kinases, BRAF (2%) and PI3K (2%), or in KRAS (roughly 30%), all of which encode signaling molecules downstream of the receptor tyrosine kinases. EGFR, ALK, ROS1, and RET are receptor tyrosine kinases, and these drivers matter because many can be targeted with specific inhibitors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Adenocarcinoma drivers — RTKs: EGFR (10%–15%; more in nonsmoking Asian women), ALK (3%–5%), ROS1 (1%), MET (2%–5%), RET (1%–2%); downstream: KRAS (~30%), BRAF (2%), PI3K (2%). SCC: TP53, CDKN2A (p16, 65%), FGFR1 amplification, 3p/9p/17p loss. SCLC: TP53 + RB, MYC amplification. COMLEX: Order molecular profiling on adenocarcinoma because many drivers are targetable.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 6 — pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis, another lung lesion driven by activating BRAF mutations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Major Histologic Subtypes of Lung Cancer — Molecular Features (TP53, RB, EGFR, ALK, BRAF, KRAS) and Field Carcinogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonary pathologist examining bronchial biopsies from a 58-year-old current smoker notes that histologically normal-appearing bronchial epithelium at sites distant from the primary tumor harbors early molecular alterations. Which of the following best characterizes the field carcinogenesis phenomenon and its molecular basis in tobacco-related lung carcinogenesis?","choices":{"A":"Field carcinogenesis is mediated by paracrine signaling from an established primary tumor — transformed cells secrete tumor-derived exosomes carrying oncogenic microRNAs that reprogram adjacent normal epithelium to develop secondary mutations","B":"Field carcinogenesis in the lung reflects clonal expansion of a single transformed cell that migrates via the airway lumen to colonize histologically normal-appearing epithelium; all tumors in the same patient arise from the same original clone regardless of histologic subtype","C":"The 'field effect' in the lung is an epigenetic phenomenon only — promoter hypermethylation silences tumor suppressor genes without producing detectable DNA sequence alterations; classic mutational analysis of TP53 and RB does not detect field changes","D":"Tobacco carcinogens mutagenize large areas of the respiratory mucosa simultaneously; 3p deletions and CDKN2A (9p21) loss occur in histologically normal bronchial epithelial cells of smokers — providing a genetically unstable 'fertile field' from which carcinoma can emerge at multiple foci","E":"The field effect preferentially affects type II pneumocytes and Club (Clara) cells rather than bronchial basal cells because these peripheral airway cells have higher CYP1A1 expression and directly contact inhaled tobacco carcinogens in alveolar spaces"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Field carcinogenesis (field effect) in the lung is the concept that tobacco carcinogens simultaneously mutagenize large areas of the respiratory mucosa, creating a molecularly altered but histologically normal-appearing 'field' from which multiple independent tumors can arise. Key early molecular events — particularly loss of heterozygosity at chromosome 3p and at 9p21 (CDKN2A locus) — are detectable by molecular analysis in histologically normal bronchial epithelium of smokers even without visible cancer. This explains: (1) why synchronous and metachronous lung cancers occur in the same patient, (2) why carcinoma in situ may exist at multiple sites, and (3) why the surgical margins may appear histologically normal yet harbor pre-cancerous cells at molecular analysis. This finding also underscores why lung cancer screening (LDCT) is applied to at-risk populations rather than monitoring a single known lesion — the entire mucosal surface is at elevated risk.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — Field carcinogenesis: 3p loss and CDKN2A (9p21) deletions in histologically normal mucosa of smokers. Explains multifocal/synchronous tumors. Early events: 3p > 9p (CDKN2A) > 17p (TP53) > squamous metaplasia → dysplasia → CIS → invasive SCC. SCLC: 3p loss also in histologically normal epithelium. Clinical implication: entire lung at risk, not just one lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 2 — tobacco and other risk factors for lung cancer","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Adenocarcinoma — Pathologic and Morphologic Features, Location, Affected Populations, and TTF-1 Expression","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman — a light smoker (10 pack-years) who quit 15 years ago — has a 2.1-cm peripheral lung nodule discovered on CT. Surgical resection reveals a gray-white firm mass with central anthracotic pigmentation and pleural puckering. Histology shows gland-forming tumor cells with positive TTF-1 immunostaining. Which of the following best describes the histologic classification and epidemiologic context of this tumor?","choices":{"A":"Squamous cell carcinoma — the most common lung carcinoma in adult males and the subtype most strongly associated with central/hilar location; TTF-1 is characteristically negative, while p40 and p63 mark squamous differentiation","B":"Large cell carcinoma — an undifferentiated tumor defined by the absence of glandular, squamous, or neuroendocrine features on light microscopy; TTF-1 positivity in a large cell tumor would redefine it as adenocarcinoma by immunophenotype","C":"Carcinoid tumor — arising from neuroendocrine cells of the bronchial epithelium; carcinoids preferentially occur in younger nonsmokers, have organoid histology and uniform nuclear features, and express synaptophysin and chromogranin rather than TTF-1","D":"Pulmonary adenocarcinoma — now the most common primary lung cancer in both sexes and usually peripheral; TTF-1 positivity confirms adenocarcinoma lineage, reflecting origin from TTF-1-expressing peripheral airway and alveolar cells","E":"Adenocarcinoma in situ (AIS) — a preinvasive lesion with pure lepidic growth (no stromal invasion), limited to ≤3 cm; the presence of pleural puckering and firm central scarring indicates early stromal invasion, which excludes the AIS classification"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pulmonary adenocarcinoma has surpassed squamous cell carcinoma to become the most common lung cancer in both sexes, a trend attributed to changes in cigarette composition (leading to deeper inhalation and greater exposure of peripheral airways), increased female smoking rates, and possibly the recognition that many never-smoker tumors are adenocarcinoma. Adenocarcinomas characteristically arise in the peripheral lung, often as subpleural masses with central scarring and pleural puckering (due to desmoplastic reaction). TTF-1 (thyroid transcription factor 1) is expressed by the majority of adenocarcinomas (Robbins Ch 15); it is the most clinically useful positive immunomarker for pulmonary adenocarcinoma and helps distinguish it from metastatic carcinomas of non-pulmonary origin. Squamous cell carcinoma is TTF-1 negative (p40/p63 positive). Large cell carcinoma, by definition, lacks any differentiation markers — TTF-1 positivity reclassifies it. AIS has no invasion, so pleural puckering/scarring would be inconsistent.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — Adenocarcinoma: now most common lung cancer in both sexes (50%). Peripheral location; TTF-1+ (majority); central scar with anthracosis + pleural puckering. Growth patterns: lepidic, acinar, papillary, micropapillary, solid. TTF-1: distinguishes from SCC (TTF-1−; p40/p63+) and many metastases. EGFR/ALK drivers in never-smoker subpopulation. SDL 39 Obj 2 established carcinogen exposure context.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — the alveolar epithelium of the peripheral lung, where adenocarcinomas usually arise","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Large cell carcinoma, by defin","E":"AIS has no invasion, so pleura"}},{"id":"sdl40_q05","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Adenocarcinoma — Pathologic and Morphologic Features, Location, Affected Populations, and TTF-1 Expression","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old never-smoking East Asian woman undergoes resection of a 2.2-cm peripheral lung nodule. The pathology report describes 'a lesion consisting entirely of neoplastic cells crawling along pre-existing alveolar septa in a lepidic growth pattern without evidence of stromal, vascular, or pleural invasion.' The mass measures less than 3 cm. Which of the following best classifies this lesion and predicts its prognosis?","choices":{"A":"Atypical adenomatous hyperplasia (AAH) — the most common precursor lesion of adenocarcinoma, defined as a ≤5 mm focus of dysplastic cells lining mildly fibrotic alveolar walls; AAH is the smallest and least-progressed precursor lesion in the adenocarcinoma sequence","B":"Adenocarcinoma in situ (AIS) — defined as a ≤3 cm lesion with pure lepidic growth and no stromal/vascular/pleural invasion; it has an excellent prognosis after complete resection because it has not invaded beyond the alveolar walls","C":"Mucinous adenocarcinoma — defined by the presence of tall columnar mucin-containing cells growing along alveolar walls; tends to spread aerogenously to form satellite nodules in other lobes, resulting in a prognosis significantly worse than non-mucinous lepidic lesions","D":"Invasive lepidic adenocarcinoma — defined as a tumor with lepidic-predominant growth but with an invasive component measuring greater than 5 mm; the presence of any invasion beyond 5 mm excludes AIS and microinvasive adenocarcinoma classifications","E":"Microinvasive adenocarcinoma — a lesion with lepidic-predominant growth that has a small invasive component of ≤5 mm; this lesion has an excellent prognosis and is distinct from AIS by the presence of small foci of stromal invasion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The WHO classification of adenocarcinoma uses the extent of invasion as the critical determinant of classification and prognosis. Adenocarcinoma in situ (AIS) is defined strictly as a lesion ≤3 cm with PURE lepidic growth (tumor cells growing along alveolar walls without destroying the underlying framework) and NO stromal, vascular, or pleural invasion. By definition, there is no invasive focus. AIS has an excellent prognosis after complete surgical resection. Moving along the spectrum: Atypical adenomatous hyperplasia (AAH) is ≤5 mm; AIS is >5 mm and ≤3 cm with pure lepidic pattern; Microinvasive adenocarcinoma is ≤3 cm with ≤5 mm invasion; Invasive adenocarcinoma has >5 mm invasion. The lepidic pattern itself is noninvasive — it is the only noninvasive growth pattern for adenocarcinoma — but can be present around the periphery of invasive tumors (mixed pattern).","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — Adenocarcinoma preinvasive spectrum: AAH (≤5 mm, dyplastic pneumocytes) → AIS (≤3 cm, pure lepidic, no invasion, excellent prognosis) → Microinvasive adenocarcinoma (≤3 cm, lepidic-predominant, ≤5 mm invasion, excellent prognosis) → Invasive adenocarcinoma (>5 mm invasion). Lepidic = only noninvasive growth pattern for adeno. AIS formerly called 'bronchioloalveolar carcinoma' (BAC) — now retired term.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 9 — pulmonary hamartoma, the benign peripheral nodule in the differential","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Moving along the spectrum: Aty","E":"Moving along the spectrum: Aty"}},{"id":"sdl40_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Adenocarcinoma — Pathologic and Morphologic Features, Location, Affected Populations, and TTF-1 Expression","batch":1,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman is found to have a 4-cm peripheral lung mass. CT shows multifocal involvement with a ground-glass haze throughout the right lower lobe, and multiple bilateral pulmonary nodules. Bronchoscopy reveals mucoid secretions. Histology shows tall columnar cells with apical mucin growing along alveolar walls in a lepidic pattern, forming satellite nodules in adjacent lobes. Which of the following best characterizes the clinical behavior of this subtype compared with non-mucinous adenocarcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Mucinous adenocarcinoma carries a better prognosis than non-mucinous adenocarcinoma because the mucin production indicates well-differentiated goblet cell-like histology; goblet cell differentiation correlates with lower invasive potential and better response to EGFR inhibitors","B":"The multifocal presentation with bilateral nodules confirms this is not primary lung adenocarcinoma but rather metastatic colorectal carcinoma with a mucinous phenotype; primary mucinous adenocarcinoma always presents as a solitary nodule","C":"Mucinous adenocarcinoma tends to spread aerogenously through the airway, forming satellite nodules and consolidating entire lobes; this aerogenous dissemination makes surgical cure less likely than for typical non-mucinous peripheral adenocarcinoma of similar size","D":"Mucinous adenocarcinoma is defined by EGFR exon 20 insertion mutations which cause constitutive kinase activation and aerogenous spread via a distinct mucin-secretion signaling cascade; first-line treatment is osimertinib","E":"The lepidic growth pattern in this setting represents adenocarcinoma in situ (AIS) regardless of tumor size; the presence of satellite nodules reflects multicentricity of preinvasive lesions rather than aerogenous metastasis of an invasive carcinoma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mucinous adenocarcinoma is a distinct subtype characterized by tall columnar cells with abundant apical mucin growing along alveolar walls (lepidic pattern). Unlike non-mucinous AIS or lepidic adenocarcinomas that tend to form discrete nodules, mucinous adenocarcinoma has a marked propensity for aerogenous spread — the mucin-producing cells travel through the airways to seed new foci in adjacent and distant lung segments, resulting in satellite nodules, lobar consolidation (resembling lobar pneumonia radiographically), and bilateral multifocal disease. This dissemination pattern significantly reduces the likelihood of cure by surgical resection compared with non-mucinous peripheral adenocarcinomas of the same size. The typical presentation can mimic pneumonia on imaging. Mucinous adenocarcinomas are also less likely to harbor targetable EGFR mutations and more commonly have KRAS mutations.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — Mucinous adenocarcinoma: tall columnar mucin-rich cells; lepidic growth; aerogenous spread → satellite nodules, lobar consolidation (mimics pneumonia). Less amenable to surgery; worse prognosis than non-mucinous lepidic. KRAS mutations common; EGFR uncommon. Imaging can look like lobar pneumonia. Compare: Standard peripheral adeno = solitary subpleural nodule with pleural puckering; mucinous = multifocal/consolidating.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 4 — the lobar consolidation pattern that mucinous adenocarcinoma can mimic","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"This dissemination pattern sig","D":"Mucinous adenocarcinomas are a"}},{"id":"sdl40_q07","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Squamous Cell Carcinoma — Pathologic and Morphologic Features, Location, Affected Populations, and Molecular Profile","batch":1,"stem":"A 67-year-old male smoker (50 pack-years) presents with a 3-month history of hemoptysis, productive cough, and right-sided wheeze. He has lost 6 kg. Sputum cytology shows an orange-staining, keratinized cell with a markedly hyperchromatic, irregularly contoured nucleus is identified among normal-sized neutrophils. Which of the following best identifies this cell and predicts the most likely primary location of the underlying tumor?","choices":{"A":"An adenocarcinoma cell — adenocarcinomas shed columnar gland-forming cells or single cells with eccentric nuclei in sputum; the orange keratinization (positive Papanicolaou staining for keratin) is not a feature of adenocarcinoma, which lacks squamous differentiation","B":"A small cell carcinoma cell — SCLC cells are small with scant cytoplasm, finely granular chromatin, and nuclear molding; keratinization and eosinophilic cytoplasm are not features of SCLC, which lacks squamous differentiation","C":"A reactive type II pneumocyte — type II pneumocyte hyperplasia in the setting of lung injury shows nuclear enlargement and prominent nucleoli but not keratinization or the degree of nuclear hyperchromasia and irregularity seen in malignant squamous cells","D":"A large cell carcinoma cell — large cell carcinoma is defined by the absence of squamous or glandular differentiation on light microscopy; if keratinization is identified, the tumor is reclassified as squamous cell carcinoma regardless of its size","E":"A keratinized squamous carcinoma cell consistent with squamous cell carcinoma — the tumor most likely arises centrally from segmental or subsegmental bronchi in the hilar region, preceded by a sequence of squamous metaplasia → dysplasia → carcinoma in situ → invasion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sputum cytology can detect squamous cell carcinoma because SCC cells shed into the airway lumen, particularly when centrally located. The key cytologic feature of squamous carcinoma cells is keratinization — these cells stain orange with the Papanicolaou (PAP) stain due to their keratin content, forming dyskeratotic (individually keratinized) cells with dense, hyperchromatic, irregularly contoured nuclei. This is pathognomonic of squamous differentiation. The large size compared with surrounding neutrophils confirms malignancy. SCC predominantly arises centrally from the bronchial epithelium, making sputum cytology a particularly useful diagnostic modality (unlike peripheral adenocarcinoma, which rarely sheds cells into central airways). The sequence — squamous metaplasia → dysplasia (mild/moderate/severe) → carcinoma in situ → invasive SCC — occurs in the bronchial mucosa of smokers and may take years.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — SCC sputum cytology: orange-staining keratinized cells (Pap stain); large hyperchromatic irregular nuclei; compare to neutrophil size. SCC location: central/hilar (segmental or subsegmental bronchi) in most cases; peripheral SCC incidence increasing. Sputum cytology: best for central endobronchial tumors (SCC > SCLC) — adenocarcinoma peripheral, not easily shed. Keratin = squamous differentiation on IHC → p40/p63 positive; TTF-1 negative.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 7 — PTHrP-driven hypercalcemia, the paraneoplastic syndrome of squamous cell carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q08","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Squamous Cell Carcinoma — Pathologic and Morphologic Features, Location, Affected Populations, and Molecular Profile","batch":1,"stem":"A 65-year-old man with a 40 pack-year smoking history develops post-obstructive pneumonia. CT chest shows a central hilar mass obstructing the right upper lobe bronchus with distal atelectasis. Bronchoscopy reveals a gray-white endobronchial mass with a cauliflower-like surface. Biopsy shows malignant cells with intercellular bridges and focal keratin pearl formation. Which of the following best characterizes the histologic features and immunoprofile of this tumor?","choices":{"A":"Small cell carcinoma — arises from bronchial epithelium in major bronchi and may obstruct airways; SCLC cells are small with scant cytoplasm and finely granular chromatin; keratinization and intercellular bridges are not features of SCLC","B":"Adenosquamous carcinoma — defined by the presence of both adenocarcinoma (gland-forming or TTF-1+ components) and squamous cell carcinoma in the same tumor in proportions exceeding 10% of each; the case lacks any glandular or TTF-1-positive component","C":"Adenocarcinoma with squamous metaplasia — reactive squamous changes in adjacent bronchial epithelium are a common finding next to central lung tumors; the intercellular bridges and squamous pearls in the biopsy are from the reactive adjacent epithelium, not the tumor","D":"Large cell carcinoma — an undifferentiated tumor with large cells and prominent nucleoli; it lacks the defining features of squamous (keratin pearls, intercellular bridges) or glandular differentiation, making it the diagnosis when squamous features are absent","E":"Squamous cell carcinoma — characterized by keratinization (squamous pearls or individual keratinized cells) and intercellular bridges on H&E; immunostaining: p40 and p63 positive (squamous markers), TTF-1 negative; CDKN2A/p16 mutated in 65% of cases"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) is the quintessential centrally-arising, smoking-related lung carcinoma. Its two defining histologic features are: (1) keratinization — forming squamous pearls (concentric whorls of keratin) or individual cells with eosinophilic cytoplasm; and (2) intercellular bridges (desmosomes visible at the light microscopic level). By immunohistochemistry, SCC is positive for p40 (the most specific squamous marker) and p63, and negative for TTF-1. p40 is now preferred over p63 because p63 can also stain some adenocarcinomas. SCC's central location makes it accessible to bronchoscopy, and obstructive complications (atelectasis, post-obstructive pneumonia, abscess) are classic presentations. CDKN2A/p16 is mutated in 65% of SCC. The distinction between SCC and poorly differentiated adenocarcinoma requires IHC (p40 vs. TTF-1) when morphology is ambiguous.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — SCC IHC: p40+ (best squamous marker), p63+, TTF-1−. H&E features: squamous pearls, intercellular bridges, keratinization. Location: central/hilar (segmental/subsegmental bronchi). Associations: smoking (most strongly), asbestos synergy. Molecular: TP53, CDKN2A (65%), FGFR1 amplification. Metastases: outside thorax later than other types. Post-obstructive complications: atelectasis, pneumonia, abscess distal to endobronchial mass.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 3 — obstructive (resorption) atelectasis distal to a blocked bronchus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q09","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Squamous Cell Carcinoma — Pathologic and Morphologic Features, Location, Affected Populations, and Molecular Profile","batch":1,"stem":"An oncologist is comparing the molecular profiles of a newly diagnosed squamous cell lung carcinoma (SCC) versus a lung adenocarcinoma (ADC) from two different patients to counsel each on treatment options. Which of the following correctly distinguishes the key molecular features of SCC from ADC in selecting targeted therapy?","choices":{"A":"SCC and adenocarcinoma share the same driver mutations because both arise from bronchial epithelial precursors; the primary distinction for targeted therapy is tumor stage rather than histologic subtype or molecular profile","B":"SCC carries TP53 mutations (most tumors), CDKN2A/p16 loss (65%), and FGFR1 amplification in a subset but lacks the targetable EGFR, ALK, and ROS1 alterations of adenocarcinoma, so platinum-based chemotherapy remains the backbone","C":"SCC most commonly harbors EGFR exon 19 deletions in smokers, and erlotinib is the preferred first-line therapy for central squamous carcinomas; the peripheral location of EGFR-mutant adenocarcinoma makes surgical resection preferable to TKIs","D":"SCC has actionable FGFR1 amplification in 100% of cases, and FGFR1 inhibitors (erdafitinib) have replaced platinum-based chemotherapy as first-line therapy for all stages of squamous cell lung carcinoma","E":"ALK rearrangements occur in 15–20% of squamous cell carcinomas and are the most therapeutically relevant driver for crizotinib therapy; they arise from chromosome 2p inversions triggered by tobacco-derived benzo[a]pyrene adducts on the ALK gene"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The contrast between the actionable molecular targets in adenocarcinoma versus SCC is one of the most clinically important distinctions in thoracic oncology. Adenocarcinoma has multiple validated therapeutic targets: EGFR (10–15%; TKI therapy), ALK (3–5%; crizotinib/alectinib), ROS1 (1%), BRAF V600E (dabrafenib + trametinib), RET, MET — all with FDA-approved targeted therapies. SCC has TP53, CDKN2A/p16, and FGFR1 amplification, but FGFR1 inhibitors have not yet replaced chemotherapy as standard frontline therapy and respond in only a subset. Critically, EGFR mutations are rare in SCC, and ALK rearrangements are essentially absent in SCC (they are adenocarcinoma-specific). This means that unlike adenocarcinoma, SCC treatment relies primarily on platinum-based chemotherapy ± checkpoint immunotherapy (anti-PD-1/PD-L1), and targeted therapy is not currently offered based on routine SCC molecular profiling alone.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 + clinical context — SCC vs ADC molecular comparison: SCC = TP53 (most tumors; p53 overexpression in 60–90% of CIS), CDKN2A/p16 (65%), FGFR1 (subset); NO EGFR, NO ALK. ADC = EGFR (10–15%), ALK (3–5%), KRAS (30%), BRAF (2%), ROS1 (1%), RET, MET — all targetable. Checkpoint therapy: both SCC and ADC respond to PD-1/PD-L1 inhibition in subsets. For boards: EGFR mutations = adenocarcinoma; RB + TP53 dual = SCLC; p40/p63 = squamous IHC marker.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 9 — inflammatory myofibroblastic tumor, another ALK-rearranged lung tumor","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Critically, EGFR mutations are","D":"SCC has TP53, CDKN2A/p16, and ","E":"Critically, EGFR mutations are"}},{"id":"sdl40_q10","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Small Cell Carcinoma — Pathologic and Morphologic Features, Neuroendocrine Markers, and Electron Microscopy","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with a 35 pack-year smoking history presents with a 6-week history of cough, weight loss, and confusion. CT chest shows a perihilar mass with bilateral mediastinal adenopathy. Serum sodium is 122 mEq/L. PET shows widespread metastatic disease in the liver and adrenal glands. Bronchoscopic biopsy is obtained. Which of the following histologic subtypes, if confirmed on pathology, has the most aggressive clinical behavior and the most limited utility of surgical resection as primary treatment?","choices":{"A":"Adenocarcinoma — the most common lung cancer subtype; peripheral tumors often present at earlier stages than central tumors; EGFR-mutant adenocarcinomas have an excellent initial response to TKIs, and surgical resection is the preferred curative approach for stage I–II disease","B":"Small cell carcinoma — it is the most aggressive lung cancer subtype, virtually always metastatic at presentation, with no known preinvasive phase and a doubling time that ensures widespread dissemination before clinical detection; surgery is generally not offered, and systemic chemotherapy (platinum + etoposide) is the mainstay","C":"Large cell carcinoma — an undifferentiated tumor that presents similarly to adenocarcinoma; large cell carcinoma and SCLC have different biology, as large cell lacks all neuroendocrine differentiation; large cell carcinoma is treated like non-small cell carcinoma with surgical resection in early stages","D":"Large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma — a high-grade neuroendocrine tumor that expresses neuroendocrine markers (chromogranin, synaptophysin) and behaves aggressively; it is distinct from SCLC, which has smaller cells and less cytoplasm, but shares neuroendocrine markers and aggressive behavior","E":"Squamous cell carcinoma — although central tumors may cause hemoptysis and obstruction, SCC metastasizes outside the thorax later in its course than other subtypes; hyponatremia from SIADH would be atypical for SCC, which more commonly causes hypercalcemia via PTHrP secretion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Small cell carcinoma (SCLC) is unique among lung cancers for its almost invariably metastatic presentation and the futility of surgery as primary treatment. SCLC grows rapidly with the highest mitotic rate of any lung carcinoma. There is no known preinvasive or in situ phase — by the time clinical symptoms appear, the disease is virtually always widespread. Stage grouping for SCLC simplifies to 'limited disease' (confined to ipsilateral hemithorax) vs. 'extensive disease' (beyond one hemithorax — vast majority at presentation). Chemotherapy (platinum + etoposide) ± immunotherapy is the standard treatment. Hyponatremia in this context reflects SIADH from ectopic ADH secretion — the most common paraneoplastic electrolyte disorder in SCLC, consistent with its neuroendocrine differentiation and capacity for ectopic hormone production.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — SCLC: most aggressive lung cancer; virtually always metastatic at presentation; no preinvasive phase. Cell size < 3× lymphocyte diameter. Surgery generally not curative. Tx: platinum + etoposide ± checkpoint therapy. Staging: limited vs. extensive (not TNM). Paraneoplastic syndromes: SIADH (hyponatremia), ectopic ACTH (Cushing), Lambert-Eaton (proximal muscle weakness ↑ with repetitive stimulation). SDL 41 Obj 7 → full paraneoplastic coverage.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 7 — SIADH and the other paraneoplastic syndromes of lung cancer","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q11","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Small Cell Carcinoma — Pathologic and Morphologic Features, Neuroendocrine Markers, and Electron Microscopy","batch":1,"stem":"A pathologist receives a CT-guided core biopsy from a 61-year-old smoker with a perihilar lung mass and widespread mediastinal lymphadenopathy. The H&E shows small cells with scant cytoplasm, nuclear molding, and a finely granular 'salt-and-pepper' chromatin pattern. No keratinization or gland formation is seen. Which of the following panel of immunohistochemical markers would confirm neuroendocrine differentiation and definitively distinguish this tumor from squamous cell carcinoma or adenocarcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Chromogranin A, synaptophysin, and CD56 (NCAM) — the canonical neuroendocrine immunomarkers; in addition to these, TTF-1 is often positive in SCLC, and p40/p63 (squamous markers) and TTF-1-associated adenocarcinoma glandular markers are absent","B":"p40, p63, and CK5/6 — the squamous differentiation markers; SCLC may show focal positivity for p63 (a less specific squamous marker), but p40 and CK5/6 positivity is characteristic of SCC, not SCLC; CD56 and chromogranin would be negative in SCC","C":"TTF-1, napsin A, and CK7 — the adenocarcinoma panel; TTF-1 is paradoxically expressed in SCLC (~80%), but napsin A (a lysosomal aspartyl protease expressed in pneumocytes) is consistently negative in SCLC; napsin A distinguishes adenocarcinoma from SCLC when TTF-1 alone is ambiguous","D":"Calretinin, WT-1, and D2-40 — the mesothelial marker panel used to distinguish mesothelioma from metastatic carcinoma in the pleura; these markers are uniformly negative in both SCLC and other primary lung carcinomas","E":"S-100 protein, HMB-45, and melan-A — markers of melanocytic differentiation used in suspected melanoma; while metastatic melanoma to the lung can mimic small round cell tumors, SCLC does not express melanocytic markers"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The neuroendocrine differentiation of SCLC is confirmed by the three canonical neuroendocrine markers: chromogranin A (a secretory granule membrane protein), synaptophysin (a synaptic vesicle membrane glycoprotein), and CD56/NCAM (neural cell adhesion molecule). These markers are positive in the vast majority of SCLC cases and also in carcinoid tumors, large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma, and other high-grade neuroendocrine carcinomas of the lung. TTF-1 is often positive in SCLC as well — this is a useful point because TTF-1 positivity in a small round blue cell tumor of the lung without glandular features or squamous features should raise SCLC as a diagnosis (not just adenocarcinoma). Napsin A, by contrast, is negative in SCLC, helping to distinguish it from adenocarcinoma when TTF-1 is positive in both. Electron microscopy shows dense-core neurosecretory granules (~100 nm diameter) in ~two-thirds of SCLC cases.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — SCLC IHC: chromogranin A+, synaptophysin+, CD56/NCAM+, TTF-1 often +; napsin A− (distinguishes from adeno when TTF-1+). EM: dense-core neurosecretory granules (100 nm) in 2/3. Distinguish: SCLC (small cells, nuclear molding, scant cytoplasm) vs. large cell NEC (large cells, prominent nucleoli, same neuroendocrine markers). Mitotic count invariably high in SCLC. Carcinoid: organoid pattern, low mitotic rate (Robbins: typical < 2, atypical 2–10 mitoses per 10 HPF), same markers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 7 — the ectopic hormone syndromes produced by neuroendocrine small cell carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Napsin A, by contrast, is nega"}},{"id":"sdl40_q12","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Small Cell Carcinoma — Pathologic and Morphologic Features, Neuroendocrine Markers, and Electron Microscopy","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonary pathologist receives a biopsy from a 70-year-old smoker's central lung mass. The H&E shows large cells with prominent nucleoli, moderate cytoplasm, and neuroendocrine morphology (organoid pattern, palisading). Chromogranin, synaptophysin, and CD56 are all positive. Ki-67 is 60%. The cells are larger than three times the diameter of a resting lymphocyte. Which of the following best distinguishes this tumor from classic small cell carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"This tumor is a variant of small cell carcinoma called 'large cell SCLC' — small cell carcinoma shows a spectrum of cell sizes, and any SCLC with cells exceeding 3× the lymphocyte diameter is reclassified as 'large cell SCLC' and treated identically to typical SCLC with platinum-etoposide chemotherapy","B":"This tumor is an atypical carcinoid — atypical carcinoids are defined by mitotic rates of 2–10 per 2 mm² and focal necrosis; a Ki-67 of 60% and neuroendocrine morphology in a central location are consistent with atypical carcinoid in an older smoker","C":"This tumor is large cell carcinoma (undifferentiated) — neuroendocrine marker expression is an incidental finding in undifferentiated large cell carcinoma and does not change its classification, which rests on light microscopy alone","D":"Large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma (LCNEC) — defined by large cell size (>3× lymphocyte diameter), prominent nucleoli, and abundant cytoplasm combined with neuroendocrine morphology and markers; it is a discrete entity from SCLC with a distinct prognosis (≈30% vs ≈5% 5-year survival)","E":"The positive neuroendocrine markers reclassify this as combined small cell carcinoma — by definition, any lung carcinoma that expresses two or more neuroendocrine markers is classified as combined small cell carcinoma regardless of cell size"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma (LCNEC) is a high-grade neuroendocrine malignancy that must be distinguished from SCLC because the two differ in behavior and prognosis despite sharing neuroendocrine markers. The critical distinction is cell size and cytologic features: SCLC cells are small (≤3× lymphocyte diameter), have scant cytoplasm, nuclear molding, and absent or inconspicuous nucleoli. LCNEC cells are large (>3× lymphocyte diameter), have abundant cytoplasm, and prominent nucleoli — while both show neuroendocrine morphology (organoid growth, palisading) and express chromogranin, synaptophysin, and CD56. Robbins gives 5-year survivals of about 30% for LCNEC and 5% for SCLC; resectable LCNEC is managed surgically like non-small cell carcinoma, while advanced disease is often treated with platinum-etoposide. Large cell carcinoma (truly undifferentiated) differs from LCNEC in that it shows no neuroendocrine morphology or marker expression; neuroendocrine morphology plus markers moves a large cell tumor into the LCNEC category, whereas true large cell carcinoma is an undifferentiated tumor without lineage markers. Atypical carcinoid has only 2–10 mitoses per 10 high-power fields and/or foci of necrosis (Robbins Ch 15) — a far lower proliferative rate than the 60% Ki-67 here.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — LCNEC vs SCLC: Same neuroendocrine markers (chromogranin, synaptophysin, CD56). SCLC: small cells ≤3× lymphocyte, scant cytoplasm, nuclear molding, inconspicuous nucleoli. LCNEC: large cells >3× lymphocyte, abundant cytoplasm, prominent nucleoli. Prognosis: 5-year survival ≈30% LCNEC vs ≈5% SCLC; resectable LCNEC → surgery as for NSCLC; SCLC = platinum + etoposide. Mitoses: SCLC and LCNEC high; atypical carcinoid 2–10 per 10 HPF; typical carcinoid < 2 per 10 HPF (Robbins). Large cell carcinoma (true): no markers, no morphology, undifferentiated — treated as NSCLC.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 8 — typical and atypical carcinoids on the same neuroendocrine spectrum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q13","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Major Histologic Subtypes of Lung Cancer — Molecular Features (TP53, RB, EGFR, ALK, BRAF, KRAS) and Field Carcinogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A pathologist reviews bronchoscopic biopsies from a 60-year-old male smoker (55 pack-years) during a surveillance protocol. One biopsy shows squamous cells with complete loss of nuclear polarity, marked nuclear hyperchromasia, pleomorphism, and mitotic figures occupying the full thickness of the epithelium — without penetration of the basement membrane. Which of the following accurately characterizes the molecular stage and clinical significance of this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Squamous dysplasia (moderate grade) — moderate dysplasia involves the lower two-thirds of the epithelial thickness but preserves nuclear polarity in the upper one-third; the absence of any preserved nuclear polarity across the full epithelial thickness defines CIS rather than moderate dysplasia","B":"Atypical adenomatous hyperplasia (AAH) — the first step in the adenocarcinoma precursor sequence; AAH is a small focus (≤5 mm) of dysplastic pneumocytes lining mildly thickened alveolar walls with preserved nuclear polarity and minimal pleomorphism, not involving bronchial epithelium","C":"Adenocarcinoma in situ (AIS) — a preinvasive adenocarcinoma ≤3 cm with lepidic growth pattern; AIS cells are columnar/cuboidal growing along alveolar walls (not bronchial columnar epithelium) and represent an adenocarcinoma lineage, not a squamous precursor","D":"Squamous carcinoma in situ (CIS) — TP53 mutations occur in 60–90% of CIS lesions, and CDKN2A/p16 loss is already established; the basement membrane remains intact, but this stage immediately precedes invasion; CIS may persist as a preinvasive field lesion for years before becoming invasive SCC","E":"Invasive squamous cell carcinoma — invasion is defined by tumor cells penetrating through the basement membrane into the stroma; without basement membrane disruption, the correct classification remains carcinoma in situ regardless of how severe the cytologic abnormalities appear"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The squamous carcinogenesis sequence — basal cell hyperplasia → squamous metaplasia → mild/moderate/severe dysplasia → carcinoma in situ (CIS) → invasive SCC — is one of the best-characterized carcinogenic progressions in oncology. This biopsy shows CIS: full-thickness cytologic atypia with nuclear hyperchromasia, pleomorphism, mitoses, and complete loss of polarity, but the basement membrane remains intact (by definition). This is the final preinvasive stage. Key molecular events already established at CIS include TP53 mutations (60–90% of CIS lesions), CDKN2A/p16 loss, and chromosomal deletions at 3p and 9p21. CIS is identified by sputum cytology or bronchoscopy with biopsy. The critical clinical point: CIS may persist for years without progressing to invasive SCC, but cannot be distinguished from those that will progress by histology alone — molecular tools are needed but not yet clinically validated for this purpose.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — CIS (carcinoma in situ): full-thickness atypia, no invasion; immediately precedes invasive SCC. Key molecular events: 3p loss (early), CDKN2A/9p loss (early), TP53 overexpression/mutation (60–90% in CIS). Compare: Mod. dysplasia = lower 2/3 atypia. CIS = full-thickness. Invasion = basement membrane penetration. AIS = adenocarcinoma lineage (lepidic, alveolar). AAH = adenocarcinoma precursor, ≤5 mm, alveolar walls.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 2 — Lung cancer risk factors: tobacco carcinogens, field cancerization, CYP P-450 carcinogen activation → accumulated genomic alterations in bronchial epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q14","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Major Histologic Subtypes of Lung Cancer — Molecular Features (TP53, RB, EGFR, ALK, BRAF, KRAS) and Field Carcinogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old White male former smoker (30 pack-years, quit 5 years ago) is diagnosed with stage IIIB lung adenocarcinoma. Molecular profiling returns: KRAS G12C mutation positive, EGFR/ALK/ROS1/BRAF negative. Which of the following best describes the therapeutic significance of the KRAS G12C mutation in this patient, compared with the scenario of an EGFR-mutant adenocarcinoma in a never-smoker?","choices":{"A":"KRAS G12C is not actionable — no FDA-approved targeted therapies exist for any KRAS mutation in lung cancer; patients with KRAS-mutant adenocarcinoma are treated exclusively with platinum-based chemotherapy ± checkpoint immunotherapy without molecular targeting","B":"KRAS G12C and EGFR mutations frequently co-occur in the same tumor — patients harboring both mutations are treated with combination EGFR TKI (osimertinib) plus KRAS G12C inhibitor (sotorasib) to achieve synthetic lethality","C":"KRAS G12C mutation confers resistance to all checkpoint immunotherapy agents (anti-PD-1, anti-PD-L1) by upregulating tumor PD-L1 expression through MEK/ERK pathway activation; these patients should receive sotorasib monotherapy and avoid checkpoint inhibitors","D":"KRAS G12C mutations are equally distributed between smoker and never-smoker adenocarcinomas; the mutation reflects an intrinsic susceptibility of KRAS codon 12 to spontaneous nucleotide misincorporation during replication that is independent of carcinogen exposure","E":"KRAS G12C is targetable with sotorasib, which locks mutant KRAS in its inactive GDP-bound state; responses are less durable than with EGFR TKIs, and KRAS mutation is common in smoker adenocarcinomas (~30%) but rare in never-smokers"},"correct":"E","explanation":"KRAS mutations are present in approximately 30% of lung adenocarcinomas and are most common in Western smoker populations; they are essentially absent in never-smoker adenocarcinomas where EGFR and ALK predominate. KRAS G12C specifically — a glycine-to-cysteine substitution at codon 12 — is the most common single KRAS mutation and, critically, has a unique reactive cysteine that allows direct small molecule covalent inhibition. Sotorasib (AMG-510) was the first FDA-approved direct KRAS inhibitor for KRAS G12C-mutant NSCLC after prior therapy. Adagrasib is a second approved KRAS G12C inhibitor. These drugs lock mutant KRAS in its inactive GDP-bound state, preventing GTP loading and downstream signaling. Responses are clinically meaningful but less durable than EGFR TKI responses, and resistance mechanisms (secondary KRAS mutations, bypass signaling) emerge. KRAS and EGFR mutations are mutually exclusive in the same tumor.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 + clinical update — KRAS G12C: covalent inhibitors = sotorasib, adagrasib. KRAS prevalence: ~30% smoker adenocarcinoma; nearly absent in never-smokers. Mutually exclusive with EGFR and ALK. Compare to EGFR TKIs: KRAS inhibitor responses shorter duration. SDL 40 molecular hierarchy: EGFR (never-smoker; durable TKI response) vs. KRAS G12C (smoker; sotorasib) vs. SCC (FGFR1, no established targeted agent). TP53 + RB = SCLC molecular signature.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 2 — Lung cancer risk factors: tobacco-induced field carcinogenesis; EGFR enrichment in never-smokers; KRAS G12C in smoker adenocarcinomas","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Sotorasib (AMG-510) was the fi","B":"KRAS and EGFR mutations are mu"}},{"id":"sdl40_q15","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Major Histologic Subtypes of Lung Cancer — Molecular Features (TP53, RB, EGFR, ALK, BRAF, KRAS) and Field Carcinogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 56-year-old current smoker undergoes bronchoscopic surveillance. Biopsies are taken from five sites: the right main bronchus (normal-appearing mucosa), right upper lobe bronchus (squamous metaplasia), right lower lobe bronchus (moderate dysplasia), a separate right upper lobe nodule (carcinoma in situ), and the left main bronchus (normal-appearing mucosa). Molecular analysis using comparative genomic hybridization reveals loss of heterozygosity at 3p21–22 in all five samples. Which of the following best interprets this molecular finding and its implications?","choices":{"A":"3p loss is a late event that occurs only after tumor invasion; its presence in histologically normal mucosa suggests that the normal-appearing biopsy sites have been inadvertently contaminated by exfoliated tumor cells from the carcinoma in situ lesion during bronchoscopy","B":"Chromosome 3p contains the EGFR gene; loss of 3p in normal-appearing mucosa reflects an EGFR gene deletion that predicts future EGFR-mutant adenocarcinoma at these sites and indicates a never-smoker molecular profile despite heavy tobacco exposure","C":"The presence of 3p loss in morphologically normal tissue at multiple sites confirms that the carcinoma in situ lesion has already metastasized to these sites via airway dissemination (aerogenous spread); total pneumonectomy is the only curative option","D":"3p loss in only normal and metaplastic tissue but not in the dysplastic and carcinoma in situ samples would indicate field carcinogenesis; the presence of 3p loss throughout all samples instead suggests a single clonal event followed by clonal expansion to all five sites","E":"3p loss is a very early genomic event in tobacco carcinogenesis, present in histologically normal bronchial epithelium of smokers before visible atypia; finding it at all five sites demonstrates widespread field carcinogenesis and marks the whole airway as at-risk"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Chromosome 3p deletions are among the earliest genomic events detected in tobacco carcinogenesis, consistently found in histologically normal bronchial epithelium of smokers well before any architectural atypia is visible under the microscope. The chromosome 3p region contains multiple tumor suppressor gene candidates (including RASSF1A and FHIT). The detection of the same 3p loss in morphologically normal, metaplastic, dysplastic, and carcinoma in situ samples from multiple sites in the same patient's airway epithelium is a direct demonstration of field carcinogenesis: the entire mucosal field is molecularly mutagenized by chronic tobacco carcinogen exposure, creating a broad substrate from which independent carcinomas can emerge at any point. This does not indicate clonal spread from one site to another, nor does it represent contamination — the finding is expected and documented in the molecular epidemiology of smoking-related lung cancer. Importantly, 3p loss alone does not cause malignancy; additional driver mutations (TP53, CDKN2A, RAS) must accumulate for carcinogenesis to proceed.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — 3p loss: EARLIEST genomic event in squamous carcinogenesis; found in histologically NORMAL bronchial epithelium of smokers. Confirms field carcinogenesis. Full sequence of early events: 3p → 9p21 (CDKN2A) → 17p (TP53) → architectural atypia. Clinical implication: explains bilateral/multifocal tumors in smokers, surgical margin molecular positivity, continued cancer risk after resection. SCLC also shows 3p loss in normal epithelium.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 2 — Carcinogen-induced genomic alterations; pack-year exposure correlation; field cancerization with squamous precursor lesion progression","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"This does not indicate clonal ","D":"This does not indicate clonal "}},{"id":"sdl40_q16","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Adenocarcinoma — Pathologic and Morphologic Features, Location, Affected Populations, and TTF-1 Expression","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman with a 10 pack-year smoking history (quit 20 years ago) presents with a 2.8-cm peripheral right lower lobe nodule showing pleural puckering on CT. Resection shows a subpleural mass with central anthracotic pigmentation and pleural puckering; histology shows gland formation, and the tumor cells show nuclear positivity for thyroid transcription factor-1 (TTF-1). Which of the following best explains why TTF-1 positivity in this tumor type is diagnostically and therapeutically significant?","choices":{"A":"TTF-1 is a marker of neuroendocrine differentiation and its positivity in this peripheral tumor indicates that the adenocarcinoma has undergone neuroendocrine transformation; TTF-1+ peripheral nodules should be treated with platinum-etoposide chemotherapy rather than EGFR TKIs regardless of EGFR mutation status","B":"TTF-1 is expressed in all subtypes of primary lung cancer and in most metastatic carcinomas to the lung; its primary diagnostic value is in distinguishing primary lung carcinoma from malignant mesothelioma (which is TTF-1 negative), not in subtype classification","C":"TTF-1 positivity in this peripheral tumor type confirms metastatic thyroid carcinoma rather than primary lung cancer; because TTF-1 is a thyroid transcription factor, its expression in a pulmonary nodule should prompt thyroid ultrasound and FNA before attributing the lesion to a primary lung adenocarcinoma","D":"TTF-1 is a lineage transcription factor needed for lung and thyroid development; it is expressed in the majority of lung adenocarcinomas (and many small cell carcinomas) but not in squamous cell carcinoma, so with gland formation it confirms pulmonary adenocarcinoma and directs EGFR/ALK/KRAS testing","E":"TTF-1 drives EGFR protein expression by binding the EGFR gene promoter; TTF-1+ adenocarcinomas have uniformly activating EGFR mutations regardless of smoking history or ethnicity, making EGFR mutation testing unnecessary in TTF-1+ tumors"},"correct":"D","explanation":"TTF-1 (NKX2-1) is a homeodomain transcription factor required for the development of the thyroid, lung, and portions of the diencephalon. In the lung, it is expressed in peripheral airway cells (Club cells, type II pneumocytes) — the cellular origin of adenocarcinoma. TTF-1 positivity in the majority of lung adenocarcinomas is diagnostically critical in several scenarios: (1) distinguishing primary pulmonary adenocarcinoma from SCC (TTF-1 negative) or from pleural mesothelioma (TTF-1 negative); (2) identifying a lung primary in a patient with unknown primary carcinoma (metastatic sites TTF-1+ → lung origin); and (3) triggering appropriate molecular workup (EGFR, ALK, KRAS, ROS1, BRAF) because targetable mutations are concentrated in adenocarcinoma. Notably, TTF-1 is also often positive in SCLC (making napsin A the discriminating marker for adenocarcinoma vs. SCLC when TTF-1 is ambiguous). TTF-1 does NOT directly cause or predict EGFR mutations.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — TTF-1: lineage marker for pulmonary (and thyroid) origin. Positive in: most lung adenocarcinomas; many SCLC; thyroid carcinoma. Negative in: SCC (p40/p63+); mesothelioma (calretinin, WT-1, D2-40+); most non-pulmonary carcinomas. Napsin A: positive in adenocarcinoma, negative in SCLC — distinguishes adeno from SCLC when TTF-1+ in both. TTF-1 stains the nuclei of gland-forming adenocarcinoma cells.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 7 — Paraneoplastic syndromes by subtype: adenocarcinoma (hypertrophic osteoarthropathy, migratory thrombophlebitis) vs. SCLC (SIADH, ACTH, Lambert-Eaton)","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"TTF-1 does NOT directly cause "}},{"id":"sdl40_q17","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Adenocarcinoma — Pathologic and Morphologic Features, Location, Affected Populations, and TTF-1 Expression","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man is found to have a 3.5-cm right upper lobe adenocarcinoma. Final surgical pathology shows a peripheral mass with lepidic-predominant growth, central scarring, and a 7-mm focus of acinar invasion at the center. The pathologist classifies the lesion as a 'lepidic-predominant invasive adenocarcinoma.' Which of the following most accurately describes how the extent of invasion in this classification system determines prognosis?","choices":{"A":"The invasion exceeds 5 mm, excluding microinvasive adenocarcinoma; lepidic-predominant invasive adenocarcinoma has a worse prognosis than MIA (≤5 mm invasion) or AIS (no invasion), but a better prognosis than solid- or micropapillary-predominant adenocarcinoma","B":"Any degree of stromal invasion regardless of size categorizes the tumor as frankly invasive adenocarcinoma with the same prognosis as solid-pattern carcinoma; the lepidic component has no prognostic significance once any invasion is documented","C":"The 7-mm invasion focus qualifies this lesion as microinvasive adenocarcinoma (MIA) because MIA is defined as invasion ≤10 mm; this patient has an excellent prognosis equivalent to AIS with near-100% 5-year survival after resection","D":"Lepidic-predominant adenocarcinoma is a radiologic, not pathologic, designation; pathologically, any tumor with invasion is classified as 'invasive adenocarcinoma, acinar type' regardless of the proportion of lepidic growth pattern, and the lepidic component has no bearing on the pathologic stage","E":"Since the tumor is 3.5 cm total with only 7 mm of invasion, it qualifies as T1mi (minimally invasive adenocarcinoma) in the TNM staging system; T1mi tumors have near-100% 5-year survival and do not require adjuvant chemotherapy regardless of lymph node status"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The adenocarcinoma preinvasive/invasive classification system is invasion-centric. The thresholds are: AIS = 0 mm invasion (pure lepidic ≤3 cm) → excellent prognosis; Microinvasive adenocarcinoma (MIA/T1mi) = >0 but ≤5 mm invasion (≤3 cm total, lepidic-predominant) → far better prognosis than invasive carcinoma of the same size (Robbins Ch 15); Lepidic-predominant invasive adenocarcinoma = >5 mm invasion or >3 cm total — this is frankly invasive and has significantly worse prognosis than MIA, though the lepidic growth pattern does confer slightly better prognosis than predominantly solid or micropapillary subtypes. In this case, 7 mm of invasion exceeds the 5 mm MIA threshold, making this a lepidic-predominant invasive adenocarcinoma — not MIA. The overall tumor size (3.5 cm) also exceeds the MIA limit of 3 cm. Prognostically, micropapillary and solid growth patterns within adenocarcinoma confer the worst prognosis; lepidic pattern confers the best among invasive tumors.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — Invasion thresholds: AIS = 0 mm (pure lepidic, ≤3 cm); MIA (T1mi) = >0 to ≤5 mm invasion, ≤3 cm total; Invasive = >5 mm or >3 cm. Prognosis: AIS (excellent) → MIA (far better than invasive) → lepidic-predominant invasive > acinar > papillary > micropapillary ≈ solid. Micropapillary and solid patterns: worst prognosis among adenocarcinoma patterns. TNM: T1mi = MIA; T1a-c based on tumor size ≤3 cm; T2 = 3–5 cm.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 7 — Paraneoplastic syndromes: hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy and Trousseau syndrome most associated with adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Prognostically, micropapillary","C":"In this case, 7 mm of invasion","E":"The overall tumor size (3.5 cm"}},{"id":"sdl40_q18","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Adenocarcinoma — Pathologic and Morphologic Features, Location, Affected Populations, and TTF-1 Expression","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman who underwent right lower lobectomy for a 1.8-cm adenocarcinoma 3 years ago (T1b N0 M0; lepidic-predominant invasive pattern) now presents with a new 1.5-cm nodule in the left upper lobe. CT-guided biopsy shows gland-forming adenocarcinoma, TTF-1 positive. Which of the following clinical factors would most strongly distinguish a new primary lung adenocarcinoma from a metachronous metastasis from the original tumor?","choices":{"A":"A new primary lung adenocarcinoma is supported by a distinct molecular driver mutation profile (e.g., different EGFR exon mutation, new ALK fusion not present in the original tumor) or different histologic subtype; a metastasis would show concordant molecular features with the original tumor on comprehensive NGS panel","B":"TTF-1 positivity in the new nodule confirms it is a metastasis from the original lung adenocarcinoma because all pulmonary adenocarcinomas share TTF-1 expression; a new primary from a non-pulmonary site (e.g., breast, colon) would be TTF-1 negative","C":"The time interval of 3 years between resection and new nodule confirms this is a new primary tumor; metachronous metastases from lung adenocarcinoma universally appear within 12 months of resection, and any lesion appearing after 18 months represents a new independent primary","D":"The bilateral location (right lobe original tumor, left lobe new nodule) categorically excludes hematogenous metastasis; pulmonary metastases can only spread within the same lung via aerogenous routes, not contralaterally via hematogenous circulation","E":"A tumor with a lepidic-predominant pattern that recurs as a solid nodule has definitely undergone EMT and clonal evolution into a more invasive phenotype; molecular concordance between the original and new tumor is irrelevant because epigenetic reprogramming alters the mutation profile during metastatic progression"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Distinguishing a new primary lung adenocarcinoma from a metachronous metastasis is a common and clinically important challenge in thoracic oncology. Molecular profiling via next-generation sequencing (NGS) is the most reliable approach: if the new nodule shares the same driver mutation and clonal architecture as the original tumor, it is likely a metastasis; if the driver mutation profile is different (or negative when the original was positive), it is more likely a new primary. TTF-1 positivity confirms pulmonary lineage but cannot distinguish a new primary from a metastasis because both would be TTF-1+. Time interval alone is insufficient — metastases can appear years later (adrenal, brain metastases from lung cancer are common years after resection). Bilateral location does not exclude metastasis — pulmonary metastases spread hematogenously and can involve both lungs. Clinical, radiologic, and molecular data together guide the determination.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 + clinical context — New primary vs. metastasis: Molecular concordance (NGS) = gold standard. TTF-1+ in both = cannot distinguish. Time interval = not definitive. Adrenal involvement: >50% of all lung cancer metastases at autopsy. Liver (30–50%), brain (20%), bone (20%). Clinical staging: M1b = single extrathoracic metastasis; M1c = multiple. Importance: new primary may be resectable (curative intent); metastasis = systemic disease (palliative/systemic therapy).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 10 — Why lung is common metastatic site; adenocarcinoma growth patterns in metastases (lepidic-like pattern mimicking primary AIS)","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"TTF-1 positivity confirms pulm"}},{"id":"sdl40_q19","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Squamous Cell Carcinoma — Pathologic and Morphologic Features, Location, Affected Populations, and Molecular Profile","batch":2,"stem":"A 69-year-old male smoker has a central (hilar) mass compressing and invading contiguous bronchial parenchyma. Biopsy shows prominent whorled keratin deposits (squamous pearls) and intercellular bridges. Which of the following correctly identifies this tumor type and characterizes its pattern of spread compared with adenocarcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Adenocarcinoma — although adenocarcinoma occasionally arises centrally, the central hilar mass with direct bronchial invasion and the histologic pattern of concentric keratin whorls (squamous pearls) confirm a gland-forming tumor with focal keratinization, which is the pattern of adenosquamous carcinoma rather than pure adenocarcinoma","B":"Small cell carcinoma — SCLC frequently arises at the hilum and compresses bronchi without forming obstructive endobronchial masses; the described histology shows large cells with keratinization and intercellular bridges, features incompatible with SCLC's small cell size and absence of squamous differentiation","C":"Squamous cell carcinoma — arises centrally from segmental or subsegmental bronchi near the hilum and shows keratinization (squamous pearls) and intercellular bridges; SCC metastasizes outside the thorax later in its course than adenocarcinoma or SCLC, which disseminate early","D":"Large cell carcinoma — large cell carcinoma may be central or peripheral, and its undifferentiated cells can mimic squamous morphology at low power; squamous pearls and intercellular bridges indicate squamous differentiation, which reclassifies the tumor as squamous cell carcinoma if they are identified on biopsy","E":"Carcinoid tumor — carcinoids arise in central bronchi and produce endoluminal masses; histologically they show organoid nesting of uniform cells with 'salt-and-pepper' chromatin and do not form squamous pearls or intercellular bridges or show the cytologic atypia described"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This is a classic presentation of squamous cell carcinoma (SCC). The central/hilar mass invading contiguous parenchyma reflects SCC's origin from major bronchial epithelium (segmental/subsegmental bronchi), in contrast to adenocarcinoma's typical peripheral location. The biopsy shows the defining histologic features: squamous pearls (concentric laminated keratin whorls) and intercellular bridges (desmosomes visible at the light microscopic level between adjacent tumor cells with abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm). These features confirm squamous differentiation and, in the appropriate clinical context, establish the diagnosis of SCC without requiring IHC. The clinical behavior note about spread timing is a key boards point: SCC tends to remain locoregional longer, metastasizing outside the thorax later than adenocarcinoma or SCLC. This contributes to SCC's slightly more favorable prognosis when surgically resectable, despite its aggressive local behavior.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — SCC pathology: Central/hilar location; squamous pearls + intercellular bridges. Spread: metastasizes outside thorax LATER than adeno or SCLC → slightly better prognosis when resectable. Complications of central location: hemoptysis, post-obstructive pneumonia, atelectasis, abscess. IHC: p40+, TTF-1−. Squamous pearl = concentric keratin whorls; intercellular bridges = desmosomes between adjacent cells.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 2 — Squamous carcinogenesis sequence: tobacco carcinogens → basal cell hyperplasia → squamous metaplasia → dysplasia → CIS → invasive SCC","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q20","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Squamous Cell Carcinoma — Pathologic and Morphologic Features, Location, Affected Populations, and Molecular Profile","batch":2,"stem":"An oncologist orders molecular profiling on a resected squamous cell carcinoma from a 70-year-old male smoker. The report returns: TP53 mutation (missense, R175H), CDKN2A biallelic deletion, FGFR1 amplification (5 copies), chromosome 3p deletion, and negative for EGFR, ALK, KRAS, and BRAF mutations. Which of the following correctly characterizes the clinical relevance of the CDKN2A/p16 deletion in this tumor?","choices":{"A":"CDKN2A encodes p14/ARF, which stabilizes p53 by sequestering MDM2; CDKN2A deletion therefore eliminates p53 regulation rather than affecting RB; the combination of CDKN2A deletion and TP53 mutation produces a dual tumor suppressor loss similar to the genetic profile of SCLC","B":"CDKN2A encodes p16/INK4A, a CDK4/6 inhibitor that keeps RB in its active, hypophosphorylated form; deletion (65% of SCC) frees CDK4/6 to phosphorylate RB and release E2F, driving the cell cycle — a pathway under study for CDK4/6 inhibitors (palbociclib)","C":"CDKN2A deletion is diagnostic of squamous cell carcinoma and distinguishes it from adenocarcinoma, which uniformly retains CDKN2A expression; CDKN2A testing can replace p40/p63 immunohistochemistry as the primary method for squamous cell subtype classification","D":"CDKN2A mutations are causally related to melanoma and sporadic pancreatic cancer but are not relevant to lung squamous carcinogenesis; the 65% prevalence figure in the Robbins text refers to CDKN2B (p15), a related CDK inhibitor, not CDKN2A/p16","E":"CDKN2A deletion in SCC is a late event that occurs only after the tumor has become invasive; its presence in carcinoma in situ lesions is an artifact of sampling from tumor-contaminated adjacent tissue rather than evidence of early carcinogenesis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"CDKN2A encodes two distinct tumor suppressor proteins via alternative reading frames: p16/INK4A (from exon 1α) and p14/ARF (from exon 1β). In squamous cell carcinoma of the lung, the clinically relevant CDKN2A product is p16/INK4A. p16 functions as a CDK4/6 inhibitor: it binds CDK4 and CDK6 and prevents them from phosphorylating RB. When p16 is lost (by deletion, mutation, or epigenetic silencing), CDK4/6 becomes constitutively active, phosphorylating and inactivating RB and releasing E2F transcription factors to drive S-phase entry and uncontrolled cell proliferation. CDKN2A loss/mutation occurs in 65% of SCC cases and is one of the earliest molecular events (detectable in CIS). CDK4/6 inhibitors (palbociclib, ribociclib, abemaciclib) are being studied in CDKN2A-deleted/RB-intact NSCLC. Note: p14/ARF (the alternative product of the same gene) does stabilize p53 via MDM2 sequestration — both products are relevant in oncology but p16/CDK4/6/RB is the primary SCC pathway.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — CDKN2A/p16: CDK4/6 inhibitor; loss → hyperphosphorylated RB → E2F release → G1/S progression. Deleted in 65% of SCC (and most melanomas, pancreatic Ca). Early event: detectable in CIS. Compare: CDKN2A also encodes p14/ARF (stabilizes p53 via MDM2 sequestration). SCC molecular profile: TP53 (60–90% of CIS), CDKN2A/p16 (65%), FGFR1 amplification, 3p loss (early). RB inactivation also occurs in SCLC (biallelic TP53 + RB) but by different mechanism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 2 — Squamous carcinogenesis: field cancerization, CYP P-450 activation; pack-year dose response; tobacco carcinogen DNA adducts → TP53/CDKN2A mutations","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"CDKN2A loss/mutation occurs in","E":"CDKN2A loss/mutation occurs in"}},{"id":"sdl40_q21","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Squamous Cell Carcinoma — Pathologic and Morphologic Features, Location, Affected Populations, and Molecular Profile","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old male former smoker presents with 3 months of right shoulder and medial arm pain radiating to the 4th and 5th fingers (ulnar distribution), right-sided miosis, ptosis, and anhidrosis (Horner syndrome), and hemoptysis. CT chest shows a 4.5-cm right superior sulcus (apical) mass. Bronchoscopic biopsy shows squamous cell carcinoma. Which of the following best explains the mechanism by which this tumor produces the neurological findings?","choices":{"A":"Paraneoplastic limbic encephalitis — SCC can produce anti-NMDAR antibodies that cross-react with sympathetic ganglia and brachial plexus neurons, producing a paraneoplastic syndrome clinically indistinguishable from direct tumor invasion; the absence of brain MRI findings excludes CNS paraneoplastic syndrome","B":"Superior vena cava (SVC) syndrome — SVC compression by a right-sided paratracheal or anterior mediastinal mass causes bilateral arm swelling and neck vein distension; ptosis and miosis from SVC syndrome reflect increased intracranial pressure causing third cranial nerve compression rather than Horner syndrome","C":"Recurrent laryngeal nerve invasion — the left recurrent laryngeal nerve loops around the aortic arch; a right apical mass that compresses the right recurrent laryngeal nerve would produce vocal cord paralysis and hoarseness but not the brachial plexopathy or Horner syndrome constellation described","D":"Phrenic nerve invasion — direct diaphragmatic paralysis from phrenic nerve compression by a right superior sulcus mass produces ipsilateral shoulder pain via referred pain through the C3–C5 dermatomal distribution; ptosis and miosis are seen when diaphragmatic elevation increases intrathoracic pressure against the eye","E":"Pancoast tumor — apical squamous cell carcinoma invades the lower brachial plexus (C8–T1 nerve roots → ulnar-distribution pain) and the cervicothoracic (stellate) ganglion and adjacent sympathetic chain (interrupting sympathetic supply to the ipsilateral eye and face → Horner syndrome: miosis, ptosis, anhidrosis)"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A Pancoast tumor is any lung carcinoma (most commonly SCC) arising in the superior pulmonary sulcus (apex of the lung, just above the first rib). The superior sulcus is anatomically in proximity to the lower brachial plexus (C8, T1, T2), the sympathetic chain, and the cervical sympathetic ganglion. The resulting syndrome — Pancoast syndrome or superior sulcus syndrome — consists of: (1) shoulder and arm pain in the C8-T1 distribution (medial arm and hand, 4th and 5th fingers — ulnar nerve territory) from brachial plexus invasion; and (2) Horner syndrome (miosis, ptosis, anhidrosis, enophthalmos) from interruption of the oculosympathetic pathway through the cervicothoracic (stellate) ganglion. Hemoptysis reflects the underlying central airway involvement. Pancoast tumors are staged as T3 or T4 depending on invasion depth. Treatment often involves trimodality: concurrent chemoradiation followed by surgical resection (when resectable).","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — Pancoast tumor: apex lung carcinoma invading lower brachial plexus (C8-T1) + sympathetic chain. Pancoast syndrome: ipsilateral Horner (miosis/ptosis/anhidrosis/enophthalmos) + ulnar distribution pain (4th/5th finger). Most common subtype: SCC. Staging: T3 (chest wall involvement, superior sulcus) or T4 (vertebral body invasion). Treatment: chemoradiation → surgical resection (when resectable). Compare: SVC syndrome = bilateral arm edema + facial plethora from SVC compression, not Horner.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 7 — Paraneoplastic and local invasion syndromes: SVC syndrome, Pancoast/Horner syndrome, recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy from lung cancer local extension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl40_q22","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Small Cell Carcinoma — Pathologic and Morphologic Features, Neuroendocrine Markers, and Electron Microscopy","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old male smoker presents with confusion, severe hyponatremia (Na 118 mEq/L), and a perihilar mass with widespread mediastinal metastases. Biopsy demonstrates small deeply basophilic cells with scant cytoplasm and high nucleus-to-cytoplasm ratio, nuclear molding, high mitotic rate, extensive necrosis, and basophilic encrustation of vascular walls by DNA from necrotic tumor cells. Which of the following best identifies the morphologic finding within the vascular walls and links it to the appropriate tumor type?","choices":{"A":"Fibrin thrombi — hypercoagulability from SCLC-associated Trousseau syndrome deposits fibrin thrombi in tumor vessels; basophilic vascular wall encrustation represents organized fibrin rather than DNA, and its presence should trigger anticoagulation therapy","B":"Hemosiderin-laden macrophages — chronic microhemorrhage into the tumor deposits hemosiderin in vessel walls that stains blue-gray on H&E; the Azzopardi appearance reflects repeated subclinical hemorrhage from fragile tumor vasculature rather than DNA deposition","C":"The Azzopardi effect — basophilic encrustation of vessel walls by DNA released from rapidly necrotic small cell carcinoma tumor cells; characteristic of (though not exclusive to) SCLC and a reflection of its very high proliferative rate and extensive necrosis","D":"Calcification — dystrophic calcium deposition (due to local pH changes in necrotic tumor) deposits in vessel walls as blue-granular material on H&E; this finding characterizes carcinoid tumors and well-differentiated neuroendocrine tumors rather than SCLC","E":"Psammoma bodies — concentric calcium deposits forming around necrotic cell remnants; psammoma bodies are characteristically seen in papillary thyroid carcinoma, papillary mesothelioma, and papillary serous ovarian carcinoma, not in SCLC"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The Azzopardi effect is a distinctive histological finding in small cell carcinoma: basophilic (hematoxylinophilic) encrustation of vascular walls by DNA liberated from extremely rapidly dividing tumor cells that undergo necrosis and release their nuclear contents. Because SCLC has the highest mitotic rate and most extensive necrosis of any lung cancer, large amounts of DNA are released, which then deposit on the negatively charged collagen of nearby vessel walls. This gives a blue streaky or granular appearance to vessel walls on H&E without the granular crystalline quality of calcium. The Azzopardi effect is pathognomonic for consideration in the right context but can also be seen in other high-grade neuroendocrine carcinomas and lymphomas. The clinical context (hyponatremia from SIADH — ectopic ADH secretion from neuroendocrine SCLC cells) reinforces the SCLC diagnosis. SCLC is the lung cancer subtype most commonly associated with paraneoplastic syndromes due to its neuroendocrine differentiation and capacity for ectopic hormone production.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — Azzopardi effect: basophilic DNA encrustation of vessel walls from necrotic SCLC cells; characteristic of SCLC (highest mitotic rate, most necrosis). Morphology: small cells, scant cytoplasm, nuclear molding, finely granular 'salt-and-pepper' chromatin, absent/inconspicuous nucleoli. SCLC paraneoplastics (SDL 41 Obj 7): SIADH (hyponatremia), ectopic ACTH (Cushing), Lambert-Eaton (VGCC antibodies). SCLC = most common cause of paraneoplastic syndromes among lung cancers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 7 — SCLC paraneoplastic syndromes: SIADH (hyponatremia), ectopic ACTH (Cushing syndrome), Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome; most common paraneoplastic-associated subtype","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"This gives a blue streaky or g"}},{"id":"sdl40_q23","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Small Cell Carcinoma — Pathologic and Morphologic Features, Neuroendocrine Markers, and Electron Microscopy","batch":2,"stem":"A 65-year-old male heavy smoker presents to his neurologist with 4 months of progressive proximal limb weakness, worst at the beginning of activity but improving with repetitive muscle use. Deep tendon reflexes are diminished at rest but transiently improve after exercise. CT chest shows a 3.5-cm central lung mass. EMG demonstrates facilitation of compound muscle action potential amplitude with repetitive high-frequency stimulation. Which of the following best identifies the paraneoplastic mechanism and its anatomical target in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Eaton-Lambert syndrome from a non-lung carcinoma — LEMS is exclusively associated with gynecologic malignancies (ovarian carcinoma); in a male smoker with a central lung mass and LEMS, the correct primary tumor origin is squamous cell carcinoma rather than SCLC, since SCC causes more paraneoplastic neurological syndromes","B":"Myasthenia gravis — IgG autoantibodies against postsynaptic nicotinic acetylcholine receptors (AChR); MG is associated with thymoma, not SCLC; weakness in MG worsens with repetition (fatigable) and does not improve with high-frequency stimulation, distinguishing it from Lambert-Eaton syndrome","C":"Paraneoplastic cerebellar degeneration — anti-Hu (ANNA-1) antibodies from SCLC target NOVA proteins in Purkinje cells; the neurological syndrome is ataxia, dysarthria, nystagmus, and gait instability — not proximal muscle weakness or neuromuscular junction abnormalities","D":"Ectopic ACTH syndrome — SCLC cells secrete pro-opiomelanocortin (POMC) precursor which is cleaved to ACTH; cortisol excess causes proximal myopathy that resembles LEMS; the paradoxical improvement with repetition and EMG facilitation are not features of steroid myopathy","E":"Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (LEMS) — IgG autoantibodies against presynaptic P/Q-type voltage-gated calcium channels, which SCLC expresses as part of its neuroendocrine differentiation; reduced Ca²⁺ entry lowers acetylcholine release, so proximal weakness improves with repeated effort (facilitation)"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (LEMS) is the prototypical presynaptic neuromuscular junction disorder caused by SCLC. SCLC cells, because of their neuroendocrine differentiation, express P/Q-type voltage-gated calcium channels (VGCCs) — the same channels present at presynaptic nerve terminals of the neuromuscular junction. The immune system generates IgG autoantibodies against these VGCCs. These antibodies cross-react with VGCCs at the NMJ, reducing Ca²⁺ entry and impairing acetylcholine (ACh) vesicle fusion and release. The result is proximal muscle weakness (particularly hip girdle and thigh). The hallmark is the facilitation phenomenon: with repeated muscle stimulation, Ca²⁺ accumulates presynaptically, transiently improving ACh release → temporary strength improvement. EMG shows low-amplitude CMAP at rest that increases (facilitates) with high-frequency repetitive stimulation — opposite of myasthenia gravis (which shows decremental response). LEMS is a classic paraneoplastic syndrome of SCLC (First Aid 2025), though it can also occur without cancer.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 + neurology — LEMS: anti-VGCC (P/Q-type) IgG → presynaptic Ca²⁺ entry ↓ → ACh release ↓. Features: proximal weakness, hyporeflexia, IMPROVES with repetition (facilitation). EMG: incremental (facilitating) response at high-frequency stimulation. Associated: SCLC (paraneoplastic). Compare MG: anti-AChR → postsynaptic → DECREMENTAL EMG → worsens with repetition → associated with THYMOMA. SDL 41 Obj 7: full paraneoplastic table by tumor subtype.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 7 — SCLC: Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (anti-P/Q-type VGCC antibodies); ectopic ACTH Cushing syndrome; SIADH and inappropriate ADH secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"EMG shows low-amplitude CMAP a"}},{"id":"sdl40_q24","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Small Cell Carcinoma — Pathologic and Morphologic Features, Neuroendocrine Markers, and Electron Microscopy","batch":2,"stem":"An oncologist discusses with a pathology resident the importance of distinguishing small cell carcinoma from large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma (LCNEC) on biopsy. Both tumors express chromogranin, synaptophysin, and CD56. Which of the following best identifies the definitive distinguishing feature between SCLC and LCNEC, and explains why this distinction is clinically critical?","choices":{"A":"Cell size and cytology — SCLC cells are ≤3× a lymphocyte with scant cytoplasm, nuclear molding, and inconspicuous nucleoli; LCNEC cells are larger with abundant cytoplasm and prominent nucleoli; SCLC gets platinum-etoposide, while resectable LCNEC is managed surgically","B":"Neuroendocrine marker expression level — SCLC shows diffuse and strong chromogranin, synaptophysin, and CD56 positivity (>80% of cells), while LCNEC shows focal and weak positivity (<30% of cells); the percentage of neuroendocrine marker-positive cells differentiates the two entities","C":"Ki-67 proliferative index — SCLC has Ki-67 >90% while LCNEC has Ki-67 between 50–75%; this Ki-67 threshold reliably separates the two entities and is sufficient on its own to make the distinction without reference to cell size, cytologic detail, or clinical staging","D":"The presence or absence of the Azzopardi effect — DNA encrustation of vessel walls is pathognomonic for SCLC and completely absent in LCNEC; if the Azzopardi effect is present, the tumor is SCLC regardless of cell size; if absent, the diagnosis is LCNEC","E":"Electron microscopy dense-core granule size — SCLC contains dense-core granules of 60–80 nm diameter, while LCNEC contains larger dense-core granules of 150–200 nm; this size difference on EM is definitive and cannot be assessed by light microscopy or IHC alone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The distinction between SCLC and LCNEC is one of the most critical in thoracic pathology because it directly determines treatment. Both tumors are high-grade neuroendocrine carcinomas and express the same neuroendocrine markers (chromogranin, synaptophysin, CD56), and both have high Ki-67 proliferative indices — Ki-67 alone does not reliably separate them. The definitive distinction is: CELL SIZE AND CYTOLOGY. SCLC: small cells (≤3× lymphocyte diameter), extremely scant cytoplasm, nuclear molding (adjacent nuclei indent each other), finely granular 'salt-and-pepper' chromatin, absent or inconspicuous nucleoli. LCNEC: large cells (>3× lymphocyte diameter), moderate-to-abundant cytoplasm, prominent nucleoli, neuroendocrine architectural pattern (organoid, palisading, rosetting). Treatment consequence: SCLC → platinum + etoposide ± checkpoint therapy (no surgery for extensive disease); LCNEC → surgery if resectable, as for non-small cell carcinoma; advanced LCNEC is often treated with platinum-etoposide. This distinction cannot be skipped and must be based on morphology despite identical IHC profiles.","boardPrep":"Robbins Ch. 15 — SCLC vs. LCNEC: Both = chromogranin+, synaptophysin+, CD56+, high Ki-67. Distinction: CELL SIZE — SCLC ≤3× lymphocyte (scant cytoplasm, nuclear molding, no nucleoli); LCNEC >3× lymphocyte (abundant cytoplasm, prominent nucleoli). Treatment: SCLC = platinum-etoposide (rarely surgical); resectable LCNEC = surgery as for NSCLC. Azzopardi effect: seen in SCLC, less characteristic of LCNEC but not pathognomonic. Dense-core granules: ~100 nm in SCLC (EM, 2/3 of cases) — not reliably sized by subtype.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 7 — Paraneoplastic syndromes: SCLC (SIADH, ectopic ACTH, LEMS) vs. SCC (hypercalcemia from PTHrP) vs. adenocarcinoma (hypertrophic osteoarthropathy)","isHighYield":true}],"41":[{"id":"sdl41_q01","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Paraneoplastic Syndromes and Systemic Manifestations Associated with Lung Carcinomas","batch":1,"stem":"A 61-year-old male with a confirmed central lung mass and biopsy showing small cell carcinoma presents with truncal obesity, proximal muscle weakness, hypokalemia, and hyperglycemia. Serum ACTH is markedly elevated and does not suppress with high-dose dexamethasone. Which of the following best explains this constellation of findings?","choices":{"A":"Primary adrenal cortisol-secreting adenoma producing autonomous cortisol with suppressed ACTH","B":"Ectopic PTHrP secretion by neoplastic cells activating PTH receptors on renal tubules and osteoclasts","C":"Ectopic ACTH production by the small cell carcinoma cells, driving bilateral adrenal hyperplasia and cortisol excess","D":"Pituitary corticotroph adenoma causing bilateral adrenal hyperplasia with markedly elevated ACTH","E":"Ectopic ADH secretion by neoplastic cells impairing free water excretion at the renal collecting duct"},"correct":"C","explanation":"SCLC is the subtype most strongly associated with ectopic hormone production. Ectopic ACTH (by neoplastic cells) drives bilateral adrenal cortical hyperplasia and excess cortisol — producing Cushing syndrome. Key distinguishing features: markedly elevated ACTH (excludes primary adrenal adenoma, which suppresses ACTH) and failure to suppress with dexamethasone (differentiates ectopic source from pituitary Cushing's disease, which typically suppresses with high-dose dexamethasone). SCC is associated with PTHrP (hypercalcemia), not ACTH. ADH (ectopic) from SCLC causes SIADH (hyponatremia), not Cushing syndrome.","boardPrep":"SCLC → ectopic ACTH (Cushing syndrome) + ectopic ADH (SIADH). SCC → PTHrP (hypercalcemia). Any subtype → serotonin/bradykinin (carcinoid syndrome if neuroendocrine differentiation present). ACTH markedly elevated + no dexamethasone suppression = ectopic source. Always pair the syndrome with the correct histologic subtype.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 6 — small cell carcinoma's neuroendocrine features","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"SCC is associated with PTHrP (","E":"ADH (ectopic) from SCLC causes"}},{"id":"sdl41_q02","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Paraneoplastic Syndromes and Systemic Manifestations Associated with Lung Carcinomas","batch":1,"stem":"A 67-year-old male with a 50 pack-year history presents with a central hilar mass on imaging. Laboratory work reveals a corrected serum calcium of 13.1 mg/dL, suppressed PTH, and elevated PTHrP. Which of the following histologic subtypes of lung carcinoma is most likely responsible for this paraneoplastic syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Small cell carcinoma — PTHrP-mediated hypercalcemia is the dominant paraneoplastic syndrome of SCLC; most cases of ectopic hormone production in lung cancer arise from neuroendocrine subtypes","B":"Adenocarcinoma — TTF-1 expression in adenocarcinoma drives ectopic PTHrP transcription via a lepidic-pattern growth mechanism, making it the most common subtype to cause humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy","C":"Squamous cell carcinoma — PTHrP secretion is the most common cause of malignancy-associated hypercalcemia among lung cancers and is predominantly associated with squamous cell carcinoma of the lung","D":"Large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma — the neuroendocrine secretory granules characteristic of LCNEC contain preformed PTHrP that is constitutively released regardless of serum calcium levels","E":"Carcinoid tumor — serotonin and bradykinin secretion by carcinoid cells also activate calcium-sensing receptors on renal tubules, causing hypercalcemia indistinguishable from PTHrP-mediated hypercalcemia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"PTHrP activates PTH receptors on osteoclasts and renal tubules, increasing bone resorption and calcium reabsorption, producing the humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy (HHM). Suppressed intact PTH plus elevated PTHrP confirms the paraneoplastic (non-PTH) etiology. SCC is the subtype most strongly associated with PTHrP-mediated hypercalcemia. SCLC is instead associated with ectopic ACTH and ADH. The 'any subtype can produce any hormone' caveat exists but predominant associations are SCLC → ACTH/ADH; SCC → PTHrP.","boardPrep":"PTHrP + suppressed PTH + hypercalcemia = HHM → most common cause of paraneoplastic hypercalcemia → SCC. Serum PTH: suppressed (PTHrP-driven hypercalcemia suppresses parathyroid PTH secretion). Contrast with osteolytic metastasis: also causes hypercalcemia but via local bone destruction, not PTHrP. 24-hr urine calcium elevated in both.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 5 — the morphology and central location of squamous cell carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"SCLC is instead associated wit"}},{"id":"sdl41_q03","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Paraneoplastic Syndromes and Systemic Manifestations Associated with Lung Carcinomas","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old male with biopsy-proven small cell lung carcinoma presents with bilateral proximal limb-girdle weakness that improves with repeated muscle contraction. Deep tendon reflexes are diminished. Electromyography demonstrates a facilitating response with high-frequency stimulation. Which of the following best characterizes the mechanism of this neuromuscular syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Anti-GQ1b antibodies targeting ganglioside epitopes on peripheral nerve nodes of Ranvier — causing acute ascending motor paralysis indistinguishable from Guillain-Barré syndrome","B":"Anti-AChR antibodies targeting postsynaptic nicotinic receptors — blocking ACh binding and accelerating receptor internalization; produces decremental EMG response worsening with repetitive stimulation","C":"Anti-MuSK antibodies disrupting postsynaptic receptor clustering — most common in seronegative myasthenia gravis; associated with oculobulbar predominance rather than proximal limb weakness","D":"Anti-P/Q-type VGCC autoantibodies targeting presynaptic calcium channels — reducing ACh vesicle release; distinguishable from myasthenia gravis by facilitation with repetitive stimulation","E":"Anti-Hu (ANNA-1) antibodies targeting RNA-binding proteins in dorsal root ganglia neurons — producing a purely sensory peripheral neuropathy with severe proprioceptive loss"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (LEMS) is caused by IgG autoantibodies directed against P/Q-type voltage-gated calcium channels (VGCC) at the presynaptic terminal. Without adequate calcium influx, ACh-containing vesicle release is impaired. Key distinguishing features from myasthenia gravis: (1) LEMS improves with repeated contraction (facilitation) — because repetitive nerve firing builds up residual calcium at the presynaptic terminal; (2) MG worsens with repetition (decremental response); (3) DTRs are characteristically reduced in LEMS. LEMS strongly predicts underlying SCLC (First Aid 2025 lists it among SCLC paraneoplastic syndromes). Anti-Hu causes sensory neuropathy/encephalomyelitis in SCLC — different syndrome.","boardPrep":"LEMS = anti-P/Q-type VGCC (presynaptic) → impaired ACh vesicle release → proximal weakness + reduced DTRs + facilitation with repetition. SCLC most common association. Compare: MG = anti-AChR (postsynaptic) → decremental EMG + ocular/bulbar predominance + no facilitation. Anti-Hu = sensory neuropathy. Anti-GQ1b = Miller-Fisher/GBS variant.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 6 — small cell carcinoma, the tumor behind Lambert-Eaton syndrome","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Key distinguishing features fr","E":"Anti-Hu causes sensory neuropa"}},{"id":"sdl41_q04","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Carcinoid Tumors — Pathologic and Morphologic Features, Location, and Affected Populations","batch":1,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman who has never smoked is found to have a 2.8-cm endobronchial mass on bronchoscopy performed for evaluation of recurrent hemoptysis. Biopsy demonstrates a low-grade malignant neoplasm composed of organoid nests of uniform cells with small, rounded nuclei and moderate cytoplasm. Which of the following best characterizes the epidemiology of this tumor type?","choices":{"A":"Represents 1–5% of all lung tumors; most patients are younger than 60 years; males and females are affected equally; 20–40% of patients are nonsmokers, reflecting non-tobacco pathogenesis","B":"Represents 30–40% of all lung tumors; equal sex distribution; incidence is highest in never-smoking East Asian women due to unique environmental carcinogen exposure patterns","C":"Represents 15–20% of all lung tumors; peak incidence is in the seventh decade; strongly male-predominant; nearly all cases occur in individuals with more than 30 pack-years of smoking history","D":"Represents less than 0.5% of all lung tumors; exclusively affects post-menopausal women; no association with tobacco exposure; arises exclusively from bronchial neuroendocrine cells in the distal airway","E":"Represents 10–15% of all lung tumors; strongly male-predominant; virtually all patients are heavy smokers; occurs almost exclusively in individuals older than 65 years"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Carcinoid tumors are low-grade malignant neuroendocrine neoplasms accounting for 1–5% of all lung tumors. Distinguishing epidemiologic features: occur in a younger population (most younger than 60 years, younger than other lung cancers), affect males and females equally (unlike most other lung cancers), and notably 20–40% of patients are nonsmokers — reflecting their non-tobacco-related pathogenesis. This contrasts sharply with SCLC (near-universal smoking association), SCC (very strong smoking association), and adenocarcinoma (strongest smoking association but enriched in never-smokers compared to SCC and SCLC).","boardPrep":"Carcinoid: 1–5% of lung tumors, younger than 60, equal sex, 20–40% nonsmokers. Typical carcinoid = <2 mitoses/10 HPF + no necrosis → 95% 5-year survival. Atypical = 2–10 mitoses/10 HPF or necrosis → 70% 5-year survival. Compare: SCLC = 5% 5-year survival; LCNEC = 30%. All are neuroendocrine but vastly different biologic behavior.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 6 — small cell carcinoma, the high-grade end of the neuroendocrine spectrum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q05","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Carcinoid Tumors — Pathologic and Morphologic Features, Location, and Affected Populations","batch":1,"stem":"A pathologist reviewing a resected pulmonary neuroendocrine tumor counts 6 mitotic figures per 10 high-power fields and identifies focal areas of necrosis. The tumor cells are uniform with moderate cytoplasm and small nucleoli. Immunohistochemistry is positive for chromogranin and synaptophysin. Which of the following grading criteria most accurately classifies this tumor and predicts its prognosis?","choices":{"A":"Typical carcinoid — defined by fewer than 2 mitoses per 10 high-power fields and absence of necrosis; associated with approximately 95% 5-year survival; Ki67 index is the primary grading criterion per WHO classification","B":"Small cell carcinoma — defined by more than 10 mitoses per 10 high-power fields, diffuse necrosis, scant cytoplasm, absent nucleoli, and nuclear molding; associated with less than 5% 5-year survival","C":"Large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma — defined by a mitotic rate greater than 10 per 10 high-power fields with neuroendocrine architecture but cell size greater than 3 lymphocytes in diameter; associated with approximately 30% 5-year survival","D":"Carcinoid tumorlet — defined by fewer than 5 mitoses per 10 high-power fields with focal necrosis; a hyperplastic lesion seen in areas of airway scarring that does not require surgical resection","E":"Atypical carcinoid — defined by 2–10 mitoses per 10 high-power fields and/or foci of necrosis; intermediate between typical carcinoid and the high-grade carcinomas, with approximately 70% 5-year survival"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Grading of pulmonary carcinoids is based on morphologic criteria alone — unlike GI neuroendocrine tumors, Ki67 is NOT currently required. The mitotic threshold: <2/10 HPF without necrosis = typical carcinoid (95% 5-year survival); 2–10/10 HPF or necrosis = atypical carcinoid (70% 5-year survival). This case has 6 mitoses and necrosis → atypical carcinoid. SCLC would have >10 mitoses, scant cytoplasm, nuclear molding, no nucleoli, and extensive necrosis — morphologically distinct. LCNEC also has >10 mitoses but larger cells. Tumorlets are <5mm hyperplastic nests in scarred/inflamed areas — not true neoplasms.","boardPrep":"Typical carcinoid: <2 mitoses/10 HPF, no necrosis → 95% survival. Atypical carcinoid: 2–10 mitoses/10 HPF OR necrosis → 70% survival. LCNEC: >10 mitoses, large cells → 30% survival. SCLC: >10 mitoses, small cells, nuclear molding → 5% survival. Ki67 NOT required for pulmonary carcinoid grading (contrasts with GI NETs).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 3 — the histologic classification of lung tumors, including the neuroendocrine group","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"This case has 6 mitoses and ne","C":"LCNEC also has >10 mitoses but"}},{"id":"sdl41_q06","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Carcinoid Tumors — Pathologic and Morphologic Features, Location, and Affected Populations","batch":1,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman with a known bronchial carcinoid tumor presents with episodic flushing, watery diarrhea, and cyanosis that occur unpredictably throughout the day. Urinary 5-HIAA is markedly elevated. Which of the following best explains the pathophysiology of this syndrome and why it occurs without hepatic metastases in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Serotonin secreted by the pulmonary carcinoid is metabolized to 5-HIAA in the lung parenchyma; elevated urinary 5-HIAA in the absence of hepatic metastasis confirms that peripheral tissues metabolize serotonin more rapidly than the liver","B":"Bradykinin secreted by the tumor acts on pulmonary endothelial cells to produce vasoactive prostacyclin, which is absorbed through the bronchial circulation and directly activates systemic mast cells to release histamine","C":"Serotonin and bradykinin secreted by the pulmonary carcinoid drain directly into the pulmonary veins and reach the systemic circulation without first-pass hepatic metabolism — producing carcinoid syndrome even without any hepatic involvement","D":"GI carcinoids require hepatic metastases to cause carcinoid syndrome because serotonin from portal-drained GI tumors undergoes first-pass metabolism; pulmonary carcinoids follow the same rule and always require hepatic involvement","E":"PTHrP secreted by pulmonary carcinoid cells activates serotonin transporter proteins on systemic endothelial cells, amplifying serotonin signaling and producing flushing indistinguishable from classic carcinoid syndrome"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The key distinction: GI carcinoids drain into the portal circulation → serotonin undergoes extensive first-pass hepatic metabolism → carcinoid syndrome only develops once hepatic metastases overwhelm the liver's metabolic capacity. Pulmonary carcinoids drain directly into the pulmonary veins → enter the left heart and systemic circulation directly, bypassing hepatic metabolism → can produce carcinoid syndrome WITHOUT hepatic metastases. Approximately 10% of bronchial carcinoids produce carcinoid syndrome. Elevated urinary 5-HIAA (serotonin metabolite) confirms serotonin overproduction. Bradykinin co-secretion contributes to flushing and bronchospasm.","boardPrep":"Pulmonary carcinoid → drains into pulmonary veins (systemic) → carcinoid syndrome WITHOUT hepatic mets (~10% of bronchial carcinoids). GI carcinoid → portal circulation → requires hepatic mets to overwhelm first-pass metabolism. Markers: urine 5-HIAA (serotonin metabolite). Classic triad: episodic diarrhea, flushing, cyanosis/bronchospasm. Treatment: somatostatin analogues (octreotide) for symptomatic control.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — the pulmonary veins that drain straight to the left heart, letting tumor secretions bypass the liver","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q07","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Miscellaneous Lung Tumors — Hamartoma, LAM, Inflammatory Myofibroblastic Tumor, and Lymphomas","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old asymptomatic male has a well-circumscribed 2.2-cm pulmonary nodule discovered incidentally on chest CT for preoperative clearance. Serial imaging over 3 years shows no growth. Resection shows islands of cartilage and fat separated by clefts lined by ciliated respiratory epithelium. Which of the following best characterizes the molecular pathogenesis and pathologic classification of this lesion?","choices":{"A":"A true developmental malformation arising from maldifferentiation of the primitive foregut endoderm with random admixture of mesenchymal and epithelial elements; no clonal chromosomal aberrations are present and the lesion has no malignant potential","B":"A clonal mesenchymal neoplasm harboring chromosomal aberrations at 6p21 or 12q14-q15 with entrapped non-neoplastic respiratory epithelium — distinguishable from chondroma by the presence of the epithelial component","C":"An EBV-driven clonal B-cell proliferation that recruits reactive stromal cells, forming cartilaginous metaplasia and adipose tissue through epigenetic reprogramming of lung fibroblasts by viral latency proteins","D":"A reactive hyperplastic nodule arising from myofibroblastic cells harboring ALK rearrangement at chromosome 2p23; the cartilaginous component results from metaplastic differentiation of ALK-positive spindle cells under TGF-β signaling","E":"A TSC2-deficient perivascular epithelioid cell tumor (PEComa) with mTOR pathway activation; the adipose and smooth muscle components are neoplastic, while the cartilage represents a reactive stromal response to mTOR-driven angiogenesis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Despite the traditional name 'hamartoma' — implying a non-neoplastic developmental anomaly — pulmonary hamartoma is in fact a clonal neoplasm. Cytogenetic studies demonstrate chromosomal aberrations involving either 6p21 (HMGA1 locus) or 12q14-q15 (HMGA2 locus) in the mesenchymal component. The epithelial clefts represent entrapped non-neoplastic respiratory epithelium — not a neoplastic component. The critical distinction from chondroma: chondroma is a pure cartilaginous neoplasm with NO epithelial component. Both are benign. Hamartoma is the most common benign lung tumor (often called 'coin lesion'). LAM has TSC2/mTOR involvement but presents very differently. Inflammatory myofibroblastic tumor has ALK rearrangement but is a spindle cell tumor, not cartilaginous.","boardPrep":"Hamartoma: most common benign lung tumor; incidental coin lesion; cartilage + fat + smooth muscle + entrapped ciliated epithelium; clonal (6p21 or 12q14-q15 in mesenchymal component); the epithelial component is ENTRAPPED (non-neoplastic). Key distinction: chondroma = cartilage only, NO epithelium. Popcorn calcification pattern on CT is classic for hamartoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 4 — peripheral adenocarcinoma, the malignant coin lesion in the differential","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"LAM has TSC2/mTOR involvement "}},{"id":"sdl41_q08","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Miscellaneous Lung Tumors — Hamartoma, LAM, Inflammatory Myofibroblastic Tumor, and Lymphomas","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman presents with progressive exertional dyspnea and spontaneous right pneumothorax. Chest CT shows diffuse bilateral thin-walled cystic changes throughout both lungs. Serum VEGF-D is elevated. She has no history of smoking. Which of the following best describes the molecular driver and pathogenesis of her underlying pulmonary condition?","choices":{"A":"ALK rearrangement at chromosome 2p23 driving proliferation of spindle-shaped myofibroblasts throughout the pulmonary interstitium; mTOR inhibitors are ineffective and ALK inhibitors produce sustained remission","B":"EGFR L858R activating mutation in peripheral alveolar type II cells driving lepidic growth and progressive replacement of alveolar walls, producing cystic airspaces from mucinous secretion into terminal bronchioles","C":"CDKN2A/p16 methylation silencing the RB pathway in bronchiolar reserve cells, producing small airway obstruction and panlobular emphysema phenotypically indistinguishable from alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","D":"HMB-45-negative smooth muscle cell hyperplasia driven by estrogen receptor signaling in the absence of TSC2 mutation; occurs exclusively in post-menopausal women after estrogen replacement therapy","E":"TSC2 loss-of-function mutation causing unrestrained mTOR pathway activation in perivascular epithelioid cells — which express both melanocytic and smooth muscle markers — producing cystic lung destruction, lymphatic obstruction, and pneumothorax"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lymphangioleiomyomatosis (LAM) is caused by loss-of-function mutations in TSC2 (tuberin), a negative regulator of mTOR. Without tuberin, mTOR is constitutively active, driving proliferation of perivascular epithelioid cells (PEComas) that express both melanocytic markers (HMB-45 positive) and smooth muscle markers. These cells infiltrate the lung interstitium, obstruct lymphatics, and destroy alveolar walls — producing cystic emphysema-like spaces. Spontaneous pneumothorax is common from rupture of subpleural cysts. LAM almost exclusively affects premenopausal women, implicating estrogen as a co-driver (lesional cells express estrogen receptors). Treatment: mTOR inhibitors (sirolimus/everolimus) slow progression but require indefinite therapy; lung transplant is the only cure.","boardPrep":"LAM: young premenopausal women, bilateral pulmonary cysts, spontaneous pneumothorax, TSC2 mutation, mTOR hyperactivation, perivascular epithelioid cells (HMB-45+, SMA+). Elevated serum VEGF-D is a diagnostic biomarker. Treatment: sirolimus/everolimus (mTOR inhibitors) — slow but do not halt progression. Only cure = lung transplant. Associated with tuberous sclerosis complex.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 6 — pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis, the other cystic lung disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl41_q09","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Miscellaneous Lung Tumors — Hamartoma, LAM, Inflammatory Myofibroblastic Tumor, and Lymphomas","batch":1,"stem":"A 12-year-old boy with fever, productive cough, and chest pain is found on CT to have a 4-cm well-defined peripheral right lower lobe mass with calcification. Bronchoscopy-guided biopsy shows proliferating spindle-shaped fibroblasts and myofibroblasts admixed with lymphocytes and plasma cells. FISH analysis reveals an ALK gene rearrangement. Which of the following most accurately describes this tumor and its targeted therapy options?","choices":{"A":"Pleuropulmonary blastoma with DICER1 mutation — the most common primary malignant lung tumor of childhood; composed of blastematous stroma and immature epithelium; ALK rearrangement is a secondary event that predicts favorable response to crizotinib","B":"Inflammatory myofibroblastic tumor with ALK rearrangement at chromosome 2p23 — a rare spindle-cell tumor with a lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate, more common in children; ALK inhibitors (crizotinib or alectinib) have produced sustained responses","C":"Nodular lymphoid hyperplasia driven by EBV-positive B-cell proliferation with reactive myofibroblastic stroma; ALK FISH positivity reflects cross-hybridization with EBV-encoded EBER sequences during the immunohistochemical work-up","D":"Synovial sarcoma with SYT-SSX fusion arising from pleural mesothelial cells and secondarily invading lung parenchyma; the spindle cell morphology with lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate mimics inflammatory myofibroblastic tumor but lacks true ALK protein expression","E":"Carcinoid tumorlet with ALK amplification — a hyperplastic lesion always measuring less than 5 mm; detected only incidentally at autopsy; ALK amplification in tumorlets reflects copy number instability, not a therapeutic target"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Inflammatory myofibroblastic tumor (IMT) is the diagnosis: rare, more common in children, presenting with systemic symptoms (fever, cough, chest pain — sometimes called 'plasma cell granuloma'), peripheral mass with possible calcification (~25% of cases). Histology: spindle-shaped fibroblasts/myofibroblasts with lymphoplasmacytic inflammation. ALK rearrangement (chromosome 2p23) occurs in a subset of IMTs and predicts response to ALK inhibitors (crizotinib, alectinib). DICER1 mutations characterize pleuropulmonary blastoma — different entity with blastematous components. EBV drives lymphomatoid granulomatosis, not IMT. Synovial sarcoma has SYT-SSX fusion and biphasic spindle+glandular pattern. Carcinoid tumorlets are small, inconsequential nests of neuroendocrine cells found in scarred or inflamed lung, not a 4-cm spindle-cell mass with lymphocytes and plasma cells.","boardPrep":"Inflammatory myofibroblastic tumor (IMT): children, fever + cough + peripheral mass ± calcification; spindle fibroblasts/myofibroblasts + lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate; ALK rearrangement at 2p23 in a subset → targetable with ALK inhibitors. Distinguish from: carcinoid (organoid nests, neuroendocrine IHC), hamartoma (cartilage + fat + epithelium), pleuropulmonary blastoma (blastematous stroma, DICER1 mutation).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 3 — ALK rearrangement in lung adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"EBV drives lymphomatoid granul","E":"Carcinoid tumorlets are small,"}},{"id":"sdl41_q10","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Metastatic Disease — Why the Lung is the Most Common Site and Which Tumors Spread There","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best explains why the lungs serve as the most common site of metastatic spread for carcinomas and sarcomas arising throughout the body?","choices":{"A":"The lungs receive the entire cardiac output through the pulmonary arterial circulation, acting as the first capillary bed that blood-borne tumor emboli from systemic venous tributaries must traverse before reaching the left heart","B":"Pulmonary macrophages (alveolar macrophages) actively phagocytose tumor microemboli but release growth factors that paradoxically promote local tumor cell survival and implantation within the alveolar walls","C":"The pulmonary interstitium is uniquely rich in fibronectin and laminin isoforms that serve as exclusive adhesion ligands for circulating tumor cells, providing an implantation advantage not available in other organ capillary beds","D":"Hepatic portal drainage routes the blood from all solid organs through the liver first, leaving the pulmonary circulation as the secondary filtering site that captures only hepatic-escape tumor emboli","E":"Low pulmonary arterial oxygen tension (PaO2 ≈ 40 mmHg) in mixed venous blood creates a hypoxic niche that selectively promotes tumor cell survival through HIF-1α-mediated metabolic adaptation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The lung's anatomical position in the circulatory system explains its pre-eminence as a metastatic site. All systemic venous drainage (from the vena cava) enters the right heart and is pumped through the pulmonary arterial circulation — meaning the lungs are literally the first capillary bed that hematogenous tumor emboli encounter after entering systemic veins. Tumor cells shed into any systemic vein (renal, thyroid, bone, soft tissue, etc.) will be trapped in pulmonary capillaries before reaching any other organ. The liver is also a major metastatic site, but primarily from portal-drained organs (GI tract, pancreas, colon). For systemic tumors (sarcomas, renal cell, thyroid, breast), the lung is the primary filter.","boardPrep":"Lung = most common metastatic site because: all systemic venous blood → right heart → pulmonary circulation → first capillary bed for hematogenous emboli. Portal-drained tumors (colorectal, gastric, pancreatic) → liver first, then lung. Systemic tumors (sarcoma, RCC, thyroid, breast) → lung first. Both carcinomas AND sarcomas metastasize to lung.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — the pulmonary circulation, which receives the entire cardiac output","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The liver is also a major meta"}},{"id":"sdl41_q11","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Metastatic Disease — Why the Lung is the Most Common Site and Which Tumors Spread There","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with a history of right nephrectomy for renal cell carcinoma 6 years ago presents with progressive dyspnea. Chest CT shows 15 bilateral discrete pulmonary nodules ranging from 0.5 to 4 cm, predominantly in the peripheral lung. Which of the following best characterizes the macroscopic pattern of metastatic involvement demonstrated here?","choices":{"A":"Lymphangitic carcinomatosis — thickening of bronchovascular bundles and interlobular septa producing a reticular pattern on CT; nodules are absent because tumor spreads exclusively via lymphatic channels without forming discrete masses","B":"Miliary pattern — innumerable uniform tiny nodules (1–3 mm) diffusely distributed throughout both lungs in a random distribution; results from lymphatic rather than hematogenous spread and is most characteristic of thyroid carcinoma","C":"Endobronchial metastasis — tumor implants within major bronchial lumens, producing a central obstructive pattern with lobar atelectasis and post-obstructive consolidation rather than peripheral nodules","D":"Cannonball metastases — multiple discrete peripheral nodules of varying sizes scattered throughout all lobes, representing hematogenous arterial implantation of tumor emboli in the peripheral pulmonary parenchyma","E":"Pleural carcinomatosis — diffuse nodular pleural thickening with circumferential encasement of the lung; discrete parenchymal nodules are absent because seeding occurs via transpleural lymphatic channels rather than hematogenous arterial emboli"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The cannonball pattern — multiple discrete peripheral nodules of varying sizes in all lobes — is the classic gross manifestation of hematogenous pulmonary metastases. Tumor emboli travel via the pulmonary arteries and implant in the peripheral capillary bed (where flow slows), producing peripheral nodules. RCC is among the most characteristic tumors to produce cannonball metastases, given its propensity for renal vein invasion and hematogenous spread. Lymphangitic carcinomatosis represents a different pattern (interstitial/perilymphatic thickening, no discrete masses) — more common with breast, gastric, and pancreatic carcinomas. Miliary pattern: uniform tiny nodules — thyroid carcinoma or lymphoma. Cannonball: discrete, peripheral, varying sizes — sarcoma, RCC, colorectal.","boardPrep":"Pulmonary metastasis patterns: (1) Cannonball — multiple discrete peripheral nodules of varying sizes → hematogenous; classic for RCC, sarcoma, colorectal. (2) Lymphangitic carcinomatosis — interstitial thickening, Kerley B lines, no discrete masses → breast, gastric, pancreatic. (3) Miliary — uniform 1–3 mm nodules → thyroid carcinoma, lymphoma. (4) Solitary nodule — adenocarcinoma mets, must exclude primary. (5) Endobronchial — renal, breast, colon.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — embolic spread through the pulmonary arteries, the route tumor emboli share with thromboemboli","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lymphangitic carcinomatosis re"}},{"id":"sdl41_q12","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Metastatic Disease — Why the Lung is the Most Common Site and Which Tumors Spread There","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with bilateral pulmonary nodules is found to have a primary tumor on further workup. Which of the following primary tumor types most commonly metastasize to the lung, accounting for the majority of pulmonary metastatic deposits encountered clinically?","choices":{"A":"Carcinomas and sarcomas arising anywhere in the body may metastasize to the lung — breast carcinoma, colorectal carcinoma, renal cell carcinoma, and uterine sarcoma are among the most common sources of pulmonary metastatic deposits","B":"Primary brain tumors — particularly glioblastoma multiforme — account for the majority of pulmonary metastases because the breakdown of the blood-brain barrier releases glioma cells into cerebral sinuses that drain into the internal jugular vein","C":"Hematopoietic malignancies — particularly chronic lymphocytic leukemia and multiple myeloma — account for the majority of bilateral pulmonary nodules in adults because malignant lymphoid cells home to pulmonary lymphatic channels via CXCR4 signaling","D":"Neuroendocrine tumors of the gastrointestinal tract (carcinoid tumors) account for the majority of pulmonary metastatic deposits because of their unique capacity to invade mesenteric lymphatic vessels and reach the thoracic duct","E":"Primary hepatocellular carcinoma is the dominant source of pulmonary metastases worldwide, as hepatic venous drainage directly enters the inferior vena cava and right heart, bypassing all other potential metastatic sites"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Carcinomas and sarcomas from virtually any primary site can metastasize to the lung. The most clinically common sources include: breast carcinoma (one of the most common causes of bilateral pulmonary nodules in women), colorectal carcinoma, renal cell carcinoma (classic cannonball pattern), uterine sarcoma/leiomyosarcoma, and thyroid carcinoma. Esophageal carcinoma and mediastinal lymphoma can also involve the lung by direct extension. Hematopoietic malignancies can involve the lung (lymphoma, leukemia) but usually as part of widespread systemic disease rather than discrete metastatic nodules. HCC does metastasize to the lung but is not the dominant source globally when considering all tumor types.","boardPrep":"Common sources of pulmonary metastases: breast, colorectal, renal cell carcinoma, uterine sarcoma, thyroid carcinoma, osteosarcoma (very high predilection for lung mets). Direct extension: esophageal carcinoma and mediastinal lymphoma. Recognize clinical clue: young adult with bilateral lung nodules + history of bone pain → osteosarcoma lung mets (osteosarcoma has very high pulmonary metastatic rate).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 → Obj 1 — pleural effusions, where metastatic implants produce serous or serosanguineous fluid","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"HCC does metastasize to the lu"}},{"id":"sdl41_q13","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Paraneoplastic Syndromes and Systemic Manifestations Associated with Lung Carcinomas","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old male smoker with a centrally located lung mass confirmed as small cell carcinoma presents with fatigue, mild nausea, and muscle cramps, with a normal neurologic examination. Laboratory values reveal serum sodium of 119 mEq/L, serum osmolality 255 mOsm/kg, urine osmolality 520 mOsm/kg, and urine sodium 62 mEq/L. Adrenal function tests, thyroid function, and volume status are normal. Which of the following represents the most appropriate initial management for this paraneoplastic complication?","choices":{"A":"Aggressive isotonic saline infusion at 1–2 mL/kg/hr to rapidly correct the sodium deficit — targeting a rise of 8–10 mEq/L within the first 2 hours to prevent seizures and cerebral edema from hypo-osmolar encephalopathy","B":"Fludrocortisone supplementation to restore aldosterone-mediated sodium retention — correcting the primary mineralocorticoid deficiency responsible for urinary sodium wasting in ectopic ACTH-secreting tumors","C":"Fluid restriction to 800–1000 mL/day as first-line treatment for euvolemic hyponatremia from ectopic ADH secretion — with concurrent initiation of systemic chemotherapy targeting the SCLC to reduce ectopic ADH production","D":"Potassium chloride supplementation as the primary intervention — because the underlying cause is hypokalemia-driven pseudohyponatremia from tumor-secreted factors that activate the cellular Na-K-ATPase exchanger in excess","E":"Emergent hemodialysis to correct the hyponatremia within 4 hours — indicated whenever serum sodium falls below 125 mEq/L in the setting of confirmed malignancy, regardless of the rate of decline or degree of symptomatology"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This presentation is classic SIADH from ectopic ADH secretion by SCLC: euvolemic hyponatremia (low serum Na, low serum osmolality), inappropriately concentrated urine (high urine osmolality > serum osmolality), elevated urine sodium (>20 mEq/L), normal thyroid and adrenal function. Management: fluid restriction is first-line for chronic/moderate SIADH. Hypertonic saline is reserved for severe symptomatic cases (seizures, coma), since isotonic saline can worsen SIADH hyponatremia when the urine is this concentrated, and hypertonic saline must be given slowly to avoid osmotic demyelination syndrome (\"from low to high, your pons will die,\" First Aid 2025). Aggressive rapid correction risks osmotic demyelination (central pontine myelinolysis). Fludrocortisone is used in cerebral salt wasting (hypovolemic), not SIADH. Treating the underlying SCLC with chemotherapy addresses the root cause.","boardPrep":"SIADH: low serum Na + low serum osmolality + urine osmolality > serum osmolality + urine Na >20 + euvolemia + normal TSH/cortisol. SCLC most common paraneoplastic cause. Tx: (1) Fluid restriction (chronic, moderate); (2) Hypertonic saline (acute severe with neuro symptoms) — correct slowly; (3) Vaptans (tolvaptan — V2 receptor antagonist) for refractory cases. Rapid correction → osmotic demyelination syndrome.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 6 — SCLC: ectopic hormone production (ACTH, ADH) as the subtype with highest paraneoplastic syndrome prevalence; SCLC paraneoplastic syndromes are a high-yield Step 1 pairing","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hypertonic saline is reserved ","B":"Fludrocortisone is used in cer"}},{"id":"sdl41_q14","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Paraneoplastic Syndromes and Systemic Manifestations Associated with Lung Carcinomas","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old male smoker with biopsy-confirmed lung adenocarcinoma presents with bilateral symmetric joint pain and swelling involving the wrists, ankles, and knees. On examination, clubbing of the digits is present bilaterally. Plain radiographs of the forearms reveal periosteal new bone formation along the radial and ulnar shafts. Serum PTHrP and calcium are normal. Rheumatoid factor and ANA are negative. Which of the following best characterizes the pathophysiology of this connective tissue syndrome?","choices":{"A":"PTHrP-mediated periosteal activation — excess PTHrP from the lung adenocarcinoma activates PTH receptors on periosteal osteoblasts throughout the appendicular skeleton, producing symmetrical cortical thickening independent of serum calcium elevation","B":"Rheumatoid arthritis triggered by tumor-associated neoantigens — PD-L1 overexpression on the tumor surface activates autoreactive T cells that cross-react with synovial collagen type II, producing a symmetrical polyarthritis indistinguishable from early RA","C":"Hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy — tumor-associated release of VEGF and PDGF drives digital clubbing, periosteal new bone formation along long-bone diaphyses, and synovial inflammation; it resolves with treatment of the underlying lung cancer","D":"Metastatic periosteal invasion from hematogenous spread of adenocarcinoma cells to the long bone marrow — periosteal lifting by subperiosteal metastatic deposits produces radiographic periosteal new bone formation identical to hypertrophic osteoarthropathy","E":"Paraneoplastic gout from rapid tumor cell nucleic acid turnover — adenocarcinoma cells release hypoxanthine and xanthine that overwhelm hepatic xanthine oxidase capacity; uric acid crystal deposition in joints produces periarticular erosions and periosteal reaction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy (HPO) is a paraneoplastic connective tissue syndrome producing the classic triad: (1) clubbing of digits, (2) periosteal new bone formation (typically along diaphyses of long bones — visible on radiograph), and (3) symmetric arthropathy (painful joint swelling). The pathophysiology is not fully elucidated, but VEGF and PDGF released by the tumor stimulate periosteal vascularity and osteoblast activation. Normal PTHrP and calcium exclude PTHrP-mediated bone disease. Negative RF/ANA excludes RA. HPO resolves when the primary tumor is treated — a hallmark feature. It is more commonly associated with adenocarcinoma than other subtypes.","boardPrep":"Hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy: clubbing + periosteal new bone (long bone diaphyses) + symmetric arthropathy. Pathophysiology: VEGF/PDGF-driven periosteal stimulation. Most associated with adenocarcinoma. Resolves with tumor treatment. Distinguished from PTHrP hypercalcemia: HPO has normal calcium and PTHrP. Clubbing alone can be seen with any lung cancer or chronic hypoxemia — HPO requires the full triad with periosteal reaction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 4–5 — Adenocarcinoma and SCC local invasion syndromes: SVC syndrome from mediastinal compression, Pancoast syndrome from apical invasion; hypertrophic osteoarthropathy is a systemic paraneoplastic manifestation distinct from local invasion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Normal PTHrP and calcium exclu","B":"Negative RF/ANA excludes RA."}},{"id":"sdl41_q15","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Paraneoplastic Syndromes and Systemic Manifestations Associated with Lung Carcinomas","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman with recently diagnosed lung adenocarcinoma presents with acute left leg swelling and tenderness. Duplex ultrasound confirms left femoral deep vein thrombosis. Three weeks later, after initiating anticoagulation with warfarin (INR 2.5), she develops a new right superficial thrombophlebitis. CT pulmonary angiography also reveals bilateral subsegmental pulmonary emboli. Which of the following best characterizes the paraneoplastic mechanism responsible for this patient's recurrent thromboembolic disease?","choices":{"A":"Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome — the lung adenocarcinoma triggers de novo production of anti-β2-glycoprotein I antibodies that cross-react with phospholipid-binding proteins on platelet membranes, producing an acquired thrombophilic state indistinguishable from primary APS","B":"Trousseau syndrome — tumor-derived tissue factor, mucins, and cancer procoagulant directly activate platelets and the coagulation cascade, producing a hypercoagulable state that characteristically migrates and recurs despite therapeutic warfarin anticoagulation","C":"Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia type II — warfarin therapy converts thromboembolic protection to a net procoagulant state by depleting protein C faster than vitamin K-dependent clotting factors, triggering platelet factor 4-antibody complexes at thrombosis sites","D":"Disseminated intravascular coagulation from circulating tumor microemboli — systemic thrombin activation by tumor-derived plasminogen activator inhibitor 1 consumes all clotting factors simultaneously, paradoxically producing both thrombosis and hemorrhage at different vascular sites","E":"Factor V Leiden mutation unmasked by the hypermetabolic state of malignancy — tumor-induced hyperthermia denatures activated protein C, eliminating the anticoagulant function that normally inhibits mutant factor V and preventing venous thrombosis recurrence"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Trousseau syndrome (migratory thrombophlebitis) is a paraneoplastic hypercoagulable state caused by tumor secretion of tissue factor (TF), mucins, and cancer procoagulant — substances that directly activate factor X, platelets, and the coagulation cascade. The defining clinical feature is migratory recurrence: DVT or thrombophlebitis develops, is treated, then recurs at a new site — often despite therapeutic anticoagulation with vitamin K antagonists. LMWH (low molecular weight heparin) is more effective than warfarin for cancer-associated thrombosis. Trousseau syndrome is most strongly associated with mucin-secreting adenocarcinomas (lung, pancreas, GI). Antiphospholipid syndrome is a distinct acquired thrombophilia with its own antibody profile. HIT would require prior heparin exposure. DIC produces both clotting and bleeding simultaneously.","boardPrep":"Trousseau syndrome: migratory thrombophlebitis in malignancy → tumor TF/mucin → platelet and coagulation cascade activation. Warfarin often INEFFECTIVE (recurrence despite therapeutic INR). Use LMWH for cancer-associated VTE. Most common with mucin-secreting adenocarcinomas (lung, pancreas). Classic boards presentation: recurrent DVT/PE at multiple sites despite anticoagulation in a patient with known or occult malignancy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 1 — Pleural effusions: exudative effusion from pleural metastases; Trousseau syndrome causing PE can also produce pleural hemorrhagic effusion; hypercoagulability in malignancy is a systemic paraneoplastic manifestation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Antiphospholipid syndrome is a","C":"HIT would require prior hepari"}},{"id":"sdl41_q16","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Carcinoid Tumors — Pathologic and Morphologic Features, Location, and Affected Populations","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman presents with recurrent hemoptysis and two episodes of post-obstructive pneumonia over 18 months. Sputum cytology is negative for malignant cells. Bronchoscopy reveals a spherical, smooth-surfaced, pink endobronchial mass at the right main bronchus. Biopsy shows a polypoid mass filling the bronchial lumen, composed of organoid nests of cells with small rounded nuclei, stippled chromatin, and moderate pale cytoplasm, separated by delicate fibrovascular stroma. Which of the following additional diagnostic findings would most definitively confirm the neuroendocrine lineage of this tumor?","choices":{"A":"Focal areas of squamous metaplasia within the tumor nests on light microscopy — a feature of high-grade squamous differentiation in SCC that can superficially resemble the organoid nesting pattern of carcinoid tumors","B":"Cytoplasmic mucin vacuoles highlighted by PAS-diastase staining — confirming the glandular (adenocarcinoma) lineage of the endobronchial mass based on mucin-secreting cell differentiation","C":"Lymphovascular invasion and greater than 10 mitotic figures per 10 high-power fields — morphologic criteria that upgrade any lung neuroendocrine tumor from carcinoid to small cell carcinoma, which is the definitive criterion for malignant transformation","D":"Dense-core neurosecretory granules on electron microscopy — the ultrastructural hallmark of neuroendocrine differentiation (abundant in carcinoids; seen in about two-thirds of small cell carcinomas)","E":"Positive TTF-1 immunostaining in greater than 80% of tumor cells — indicating transcription factor expression exclusive to primary lung origin and confirming that the endobronchial mass is not a metastasis from an extrapulmonary carcinoid"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The morphology (organoid nests, stippled 'salt-and-pepper' chromatin, moderate cytoplasm, delicate stroma) is diagnostic of a neuroendocrine tumor. Dense-core neurosecretory granules on electron microscopy are the ultrastructural hallmark of neuroendocrine differentiation — abundant in carcinoids and seen in about two-thirds of small cell carcinomas (Robbins). By immunohistochemistry, chromogranin, synaptophysin, and CD56 confirm neuroendocrine lineage. Squamous metaplasia and mucin vacuoles are specific to SCC and adenocarcinoma respectively. Mitotic rate >10/HPF + nuclear molding + scant cytoplasm would characterize SCLC, not carcinoid. TTF-1 is often expressed in SCLC and some carcinoids but is NOT exclusive to lung origin (thyroid also expresses TTF-1).","boardPrep":"Carcinoid IHC panel: chromogranin+, synaptophysin+, CD56+, Ki67 low. EM: dense-core neurosecretory granules. Morphology: organoid/trabecular/palisading nests, uniform cells, stippled 'salt-and-pepper' chromatin (neuroendocrine nuclear pattern), delicate fibrovascular stroma. Central location → endobronchial polypoid mass. Collar-button lesion = bronchial wall penetration with peribronchial tumor. Mostly amenable to resection (Robbins); 5-yr survival 95% (typical) or 70% (atypical).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 6 — SCLC as the aggressive end of the neuroendocrine spectrum; contrast SCLC (>10 mitoses, nuclear molding, 5% survival) with carcinoid tumors (organoid architecture, low mitoses, 70–95% survival)","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Mitotic rate >10/HPF + nuclear"}},{"id":"sdl41_q17","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Carcinoid Tumors — Pathologic and Morphologic Features, Location, and Affected Populations","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old male undergoes resection of a 3.2-cm central pulmonary neuroendocrine tumor. Pathology demonstrates 8 mitoses per 10 high-power fields, focal necrosis, and prominent nucleoli. Chromogranin and synaptophysin are positive. The patient asks about the difference in outcomes between his tumor grade and a typical carcinoid. Which of the following most accurately summarizes the comparative 5-year survival rates across the spectrum of pulmonary neuroendocrine tumors?","choices":{"A":"Typical carcinoid — approximately 75%; atypical carcinoid — approximately 55%; large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma — approximately 40%; small cell carcinoma — approximately 15%; reflecting a gradual continuum without sharp prognostic thresholds between grades","B":"Typical carcinoid — approximately 99%; atypical carcinoid — approximately 85%; large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma — approximately 60%; small cell carcinoma — approximately 25%; based on modern multiagent chemotherapy and immunotherapy regimens","C":"Typical carcinoid — approximately 80%; atypical carcinoid — approximately 60%; large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma — approximately 20%; small cell carcinoma — approximately 10%; with all four subtypes demonstrating a linear decline proportional to mitotic count","D":"Typical carcinoid — approximately 95%; atypical carcinoid — approximately 70%; large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma — approximately 30%; small cell carcinoma — approximately 5%; with a steep drop between the low-grade carcinoids and the high-grade carcinomas","E":"Typical carcinoid — approximately 95%; atypical carcinoid — approximately 70%; large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma — approximately 30%; small cell carcinoma — approximately 5%; but only if complete surgical resection is achieved — all subtypes have equivalent 2% 5-year survival when unresectable"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The 5-year survival values are specific Robbins figures: typical carcinoid ~95%, atypical carcinoid ~70%, LCNEC ~30%, SCLC ~5%. The patient in this vignette has 8 mitoses/10HPF + necrosis = atypical carcinoid → 70% 5-year survival. This contrast emphasizes the enormous prognostic difference between low-grade (carcinoid) and high-grade (SCLC) neuroendocrine tumors despite sharing neuroendocrine markers. All four represent a biologic spectrum of the same cell lineage but with dramatically different clinical behaviors. SCLC's 5% 5-year survival reflects that virtually all SCLC is metastatic at diagnosis.","boardPrep":"Memorize the four survival figures: typical carcinoid 95%, atypical 70%, LCNEC 30%, SCLC 5%. Corresponding mitotic thresholds: <2/10HPF (typical) → 2–10/10HPF (atypical) → >10/10HPF (LCNEC and SCLC). SCLC distinguished from LCNEC by cell size: SCLC = small cells (size of 3 lymphocytes); LCNEC = large cells. Both are high-grade neuroendocrine carcinomas.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 3 — Compare carcinoid (surgically resectable, favorable prognosis) with malignant mesothelioma (invariably fatal, median survival 12–18 months) as two contrasting thoracic tumors at opposite ends of the malignancy spectrum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q18","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Carcinoid Tumors — Pathologic and Morphologic Features, Location, and Affected Populations","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old nonsmoking woman has recurrent pneumonia of the left lower lobe. Bronchoscopy shows a smooth, rounded mass protruding into the lumen of the left mainstem bronchus, covered by intact mucosa. The resected specimen shows that the tumor also penetrates the bronchial wall and fans out into the peribronchial tissue. Histology shows uniform cells in organoid nests with a delicate fibrovascular stroma and fewer than 2 mitoses per 10 HPF without necrosis. What term describes this gross configuration?","choices":{"A":"Hourglass dumbbell tumor of a neurogenic origin","B":"Collar-button lesion of a bronchial carcinoid tumor","C":"Popcorn calcification of a pulmonary hamartoma","D":"Napkin-ring stricture of an infiltrating carcinoma","E":"Pancoast extension of an apical squamous carcinoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Robbins describes central carcinoid tumors as finger-like or spherical polypoid masses that commonly project into the bronchial lumen, are usually covered by intact mucosa, rarely exceed 3 to 4 cm, and are mostly confined to the mainstem bronchi; others penetrate the bronchial wall to fan out in the peribronchial tissue, producing the so-called collar-button lesion. Fewer than 2 mitoses per 10 HPF without necrosis makes it a typical carcinoid; intraluminal growth explains the recurrent infection, and about 10% of bronchial carcinoids cause carcinoid syndrome. A dumbbell shape describes neurogenic tumors growing through a neural foramen. Popcorn calcification describes the cartilage of a hamartoma, a peripheral coin lesion. A napkin-ring stricture is a description used for annular colon carcinoma. Pancoast tumors are apical carcinomas invading the chest wall and brachial plexus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bronchial carcinoid — 1%–5% of lung tumors, patients usually under 60, equal sex ratio, 20%–40% nonsmokers; central polypoid mass with intact mucosa or collar-button lesion; typical <2 mitoses/10 HPF, no necrosis; atypical 2–10 and/or necrosis; dense-core granules; 5-year survival 95% typical vs 70% atypical. COMLEX: Recurrent pneumonia in one lobe of a young nonsmoker should raise concern for an endobronchial lesion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 6 — SCLC: neuroendocrine markers (chromogranin, synaptophysin, CD56) shared with carcinoid tumors; contrast SCLC (virtually never resectable) with carcinoid (mostly resectable with 95% 5-yr survival for typical subtype)","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q19","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Miscellaneous Lung Tumors — Hamartoma, LAM, Inflammatory Myofibroblastic Tumor, and Lymphomas","batch":2,"stem":"A pathologist receives two resected pulmonary coin lesions for intraoperative frozen section. Lesion A shows islands of hyaline cartilage, adipose tissue, and smooth muscle separated by clefts lined by ciliated pseudostratified epithelium with scattered goblet cells. Lesion B shows a well-circumscribed nodule composed entirely of lobules of mature hyaline cartilage without any epithelial elements. Which of the following single histologic feature most definitively differentiates Lesion A from Lesion B and determines their correct diagnoses?","choices":{"A":"The presence of adipose tissue in Lesion A versus its absence in Lesion B — fat is present exclusively in hamartomas and its identification by CT (Hounsfield units below -40) is both sensitive and specific for chondroma on imaging","B":"The presence of entrapped ciliated respiratory epithelial clefts in Lesion A — confirming pulmonary hamartoma, whose clonal mesenchymal elements trap the epithelium — versus the absence of any epithelial component in Lesion B, confirming pulmonary chondroma","C":"The lobular architecture of hyaline cartilage in Lesion B versus the disorganized arrangement in Lesion A — lobular cartilaginous growth indicates a hamartoma, while disorganized cartilage indicates chondroma based on WHO 2021 classification criteria","D":"The presence of goblet cell metaplasia within the epithelial clefts of Lesion A — goblet cells are absent in chondroma because goblet cell differentiation requires the co-expression of MUC5AC by the adjacent mesenchymal cells, which is unique to hamartoma stroma","E":"The presence of smooth muscle bundles in Lesion A versus their absence in Lesion B — smooth muscle differentiation reflects the mesenchymal origin of hamartoma and is the chromosomally aberrant clonal component that distinguishes it from all purely cartilaginous lesions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The single most important histologic distinction: hamartoma contains ENTRAPPED RESPIRATORY EPITHELIUM (ciliated pseudostratified or bronchiolar epithelium) forming cleft-like spaces within the lesion — this is the feature that definitively distinguishes it from chondroma. Chondroma is a pure cartilaginous neoplasm with no epithelial component. Both may contain cartilage as the dominant mesenchymal element. Adipose tissue, smooth muscle, and fibrous tissue are additional mesenchymal components of hamartoma but are not universally present. Popcorn calcification on CT suggests hamartoma, but fat attenuation (-40 to -120 HU) is the most specific CT feature — fat is NOT a feature of chondroma. The epithelial clefts in hamartoma represent entrapped non-neoplastic bronchiolar epithelium; the mesenchymal component is the clonal neoplastic element.","boardPrep":"Hamartoma vs. chondroma: BOTH are benign lung nodules with cartilage. KEY DIFFERENCE: hamartoma has ENTRAPPED EPITHELIUM (respiratory clefts) — chondroma does NOT. CT: fat density (HU < -40) in hamartoma is the most specific CT finding. Popcorn calcification = classic but less specific. Hamartoma is clonal (6p21 or 12q14-q15 in mesenchymal cells). Most common benign lung tumor.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 3 — Solitary fibrous tumor of the pleura is another benign-appearing mass that may be confused with hamartoma on imaging; both are incidental findings but differ in location (pleural vs. parenchymal) and histologic composition","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Popcorn calcification on CT su","E":"Adipose tissue, smooth muscle,"}},{"id":"sdl41_q20","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Miscellaneous Lung Tumors — Hamartoma, LAM, Inflammatory Myofibroblastic Tumor, and Lymphomas","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman presents with progressive dyspnea over 3 years and two prior spontaneous pneumothoraces. She has no smoking history. Chest CT demonstrates diffuse bilateral thin-walled cystic changes. Serum VEGF-D is markedly elevated. Pulmonary function testing shows an obstructive pattern with reduced DLCO. Genetic testing reveals a TSC2 heterozygous frameshift mutation. Which of the following best describes the current treatment approach and its limitations?","choices":{"A":"EGFR tyrosine kinase inhibitors reduce cyst burden in TSC2-mutant LAM because TSC2 normally suppresses EGFR recycling; TSC2 loss leads to EGFR surface overexpression on perivascular epithelioid cells, creating a targetable oncogenic dependency","B":"ALK inhibitors (crizotinib or alectinib) are first-line therapy because TSC2 mutations activate ALK signaling through a shared PI3K-mTOR-ALK axis; remission is typically durable and therapy can be discontinued after 18 months of cyst resolution on CT","C":"Bilateral pleurodesis is the curative intervention — by sealing both pleural spaces, progressive cyst expansion is halted and the underlying perivascular epithelioid cell proliferation spontaneously regresses due to increased pleural pressure","D":"Estrogen receptor blockade with tamoxifen is first-line therapy based on the X-linked inheritance of TSC2 mutations in females; tamoxifen prevents mTOR pathway activation by sequestering estrogen ligand away from perivascular epithelioid cell nuclei","E":"mTOR inhibitors (sirolimus or everolimus) slow the rate of lung function decline and reduce angiomyolipoma burden — but must be continued indefinitely because cysts and dysfunction do not fully reverse on drug; lung transplantation is the only curative option"},"correct":"E","explanation":"LAM treatment is centered on mTOR inhibition. TSC2 encodes tuberin, a GTPase-activating protein (GAP) for Rheb — without tuberin, Rheb-GTP accumulates and constitutively activates mTOR complex 1 (mTORC1), driving perivascular epithelioid cell (PEComa) proliferation. Sirolimus (rapamycin) directly inhibits mTORC1, producing stabilization of FEV1 decline and reduction in angiomyolipoma size. However, the response is cytostatic (not cytotoxic) — cells survive but do not proliferate — so lung function does not recover and disease resumes if drug is stopped. Indefinite therapy is required. Lung transplantation remains the only curative treatment. ALK has no role in LAM (that's inflammatory myofibroblastic tumor). Estrogen contributes but tamoxifen is not standard treatment.","boardPrep":"LAM treatment: sirolimus (rapamycin) — mTOR inhibitor — slows FEV1 decline, reduces angiomyolipoma size. Mechanism: TSC2 loss → unconstrained mTORC1 → sirolimus blocks mTORC1 directly. Limitation: cytostatic only; disease returns if stopped; must be lifelong. Lung transplant = only cure. Avoid exogenous estrogen (accelerates disease). Serum VEGF-D elevated in LAM → diagnostic biomarker.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 1 — Lung transplantation as the only cure for end-stage pulmonary diseases; LAM is one of the indications for lung transplantation listed alongside COPD, IPF, and cystic fibrosis in the transplant indications spectrum","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"ALK has no role in LAM (that's","D":"Estrogen contributes but tamox"}},{"id":"sdl41_q21","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Miscellaneous Lung Tumors — Hamartoma, LAM, Inflammatory Myofibroblastic Tumor, and Lymphomas","batch":2,"stem":"A lung biopsy from a woman of childbearing age shows cystic, emphysema-like dilation of terminal airspaces, interstitial thickening, and obstructed lymphatics, all caused by a proliferation of perivascular epithelioid cells. Which immunophenotype does Robbins describe for these lesional cells?","choices":{"A":"Cytokeratin and TTF-1, as in type II pneumocytes","B":"Chromogranin and synaptophysin, as in carcinoids","C":"CD1a and langerin, as in Langerhans cell histiocytosis","D":"Markers of both melanocytes and smooth muscle cells","E":"Calretinin and WT1, as in mesothelial cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins describes lymphangioleiomyomatosis as a disorder primarily of young women of childbearing age, characterized by proliferation of perivascular epithelioid cells that express markers of both melanocytes and smooth muscle cells; the proliferation causes cystic, emphysema-like dilation of terminal airspaces, interstitial thickening, and lymphatic obstruction. The cells frequently harbor TSC2 loss-of-function mutations (increased mTOR activity) and often express estrogen receptors, consistent with the female predominance. Cytokeratin and TTF-1 mark pneumocytes and lung adenocarcinoma. Chromogranin and synaptophysin mark neuroendocrine tumors such as carcinoids. CD1a and langerin mark Langerhans cells. Calretinin and WT1 mark mesothelial cells and mesothelioma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LAM — young women; perivascular epithelioid cells with melanocytic + smooth muscle markers, often estrogen-receptor positive; TSC2 loss → ↑mTOR; cystic lungs, pneumothorax, chylous effusions from lymphatic obstruction. COMLEX: mTOR inhibitors slow decline but must be continued indefinitely; only lung transplantation is curative.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 6 — pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis, the other cystic lung disease, marked by CD1a-positive Langerhans cells","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl41_q22","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Metastatic Disease — Why the Lung is the Most Common Site and Which Tumors Spread There","batch":2,"stem":"Most metastases reach the lung through the blood or lymphatics, producing patterns such as cannonball nodules or lymphangitic spread. Robbins also names tumors that commonly involve the lung by direct continuity from an adjacent structure. Which of the following is one of them?","choices":{"A":"Clear-cell renal cell carcinoma","B":"Osteosarcoma of the distal femur","C":"Cutaneous malignant melanoma","D":"Infiltrating ductal breast carcinoma","E":"Carcinoma of the mid-esophagus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Robbins states that carcinomas and sarcomas arising anywhere in the body may spread to the lungs via the blood or lymphatics or by direct continuity, and that esophageal carcinoma and mediastinal lymphoma may also invade the lung by direct extension. The usual pattern is multiple discrete peripheral nodules (cannonball lesions), but spread may also take the form of a solitary nodule, endobronchial or pleural involvement, pneumonic consolidation, or foci of lepidic growth resembling adenocarcinoma in situ. Renal cell carcinoma, osteosarcoma, and melanoma typically reach the lung hematogenously. Breast carcinoma commonly reaches the lung by hematogenous or lymphatic routes, such as lymphangitic carcinomatosis, not by direct extension from the breast.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The lung is the most common site of metastases (receives the entire venous return). Routes: hematogenous (cannonball nodules, e.g., RCC), lymphatic (lymphangitic carcinomatosis, e.g., breast), and direct extension (esophageal carcinoma, mediastinal lymphoma). COMLEX: Multiple peripheral nodules of varying size in a patient with a known primary favor metastases over primary lung cancer.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 1 — Pleural effusion from metastatic disease: exudative effusion by Light's criteria; pleural involvement may coexist with cannonball parenchymal metastases; contrast hematogenous (peripheral nodules) with lymphangitic (interstitial thickening) patterns","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl41_q23","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Metastatic Disease — Why the Lung is the Most Common Site and Which Tumors Spread There","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with metastatic breast adenocarcinoma presents with progressive dyspnea and dry cough. Chest CT shows bilateral interstitial thickening with a beaded appearance of the bronchovascular bundles and thickening of the interlobular septa. Discrete parenchymal nodules are absent. Pulmonary function testing shows a restrictive pattern with markedly reduced DLCO. Pleural fluid analysis shows an exudate. Which of the following best describes the pathologic mechanism and pattern of tumor spread responsible for this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Hematogenous cannonball metastases — breast carcinoma cells implant in peripheral pulmonary arterioles and grow as multiple discrete peripheral nodules; the absence of discrete nodules on CT in this case reflects early-stage involvement before nodules become radiographically visible","B":"Military hematogenous spread from bone marrow breast carcinoma — circulating tumor cells preferentially colonize pulmonary interstitial macrophages through CXCR4-CXCL12 signaling, producing diffuse interstitial inflammation without discrete nodule formation","C":"Endobronchial metastasis — breast carcinoma cells shed into airways and implant in the central bronchial epithelium, causing progressive airway narrowing and post-obstructive consolidation; interstitial thickening is a secondary reactive change from obstructive pneumonia","D":"Pleural carcinomatosis with secondary parenchymal invasion — tumor cells implant on the parietal pleura and invade centripetally through the visceral pleura, producing periseptal thickening as a reaction to tumor invasion from the pleural surface inward","E":"Lymphangitic carcinomatosis — tumor cells invade pulmonary lymphatics via hilar lymph node retrograde drainage, producing diffuse interstitial and periseptal thickening without discrete nodule formation; lymphatic obstruction drives the exudative effusion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lymphangitic carcinomatosis is a distinct metastatic pattern where tumor cells invade pulmonary lymphatic channels — typically via retrograde spread from hilar/mediastinal lymph nodes overloaded with metastatic deposits. The result is diffuse lymphatic obstruction producing: interstitial thickening, beaded bronchovascular bundles (lymphatic permeation around arteries), thickened interlobular septa (Kerley B lines on plain film), and exudative pleural effusion. Discrete parenchymal nodules are characteristically ABSENT — distinguishing lymphangitic carcinomatosis from cannonball hematogenous metastases. Reduced DLCO reflects impaired gas exchange from interstitial thickening. Breast, gastric, pancreatic, and prostate carcinomas most commonly produce this pattern (mucin-secreting adenocarcinomas have high lymphatic tropism).","boardPrep":"Lymphangitic carcinomatosis: breast/gastric/pancreatic adenocarcinoma → hilar node retrograde → pulmonary lymphatics → beaded bronchovascular bundle thickening + interlobular septal thickening (Kerley B) + NO discrete nodules + exudative effusion. CT: peribronchovascular and septal thickening. Restrictive PFTs + low DLCO. Distinguish from: cannonball (discrete peripheral nodules, hematogenous); miliary (uniform tiny nodules, thyroid/lymphoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 42 Obj 1 — Pleural effusions: lymphangitic carcinomatosis causes exudative effusion from lymphatic obstruction; contrast with hydrothorax (transudative) from systemic venous hypertension; Light's criteria differentiate exudate from transudate","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Discrete parenchymal nodules a"}},{"id":"sdl41_q24","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Metastatic Disease — Why the Lung is the Most Common Site and Which Tumors Spread There","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old male with no smoking history presents with bilateral pulmonary nodules identified incidentally on chest CT performed after a motor vehicle accident. He reports mild right knee pain that worsened over 6 months and was attributed to sports injury. CT of the chest shows 12 bilateral peripheral nodules ranging from 0.5 to 3.5 cm. CT of the right femur reveals a primary bone lesion with periosteal elevation and 'sunburst' pattern. Biopsy of the femoral lesion shows malignant osteoid production by pleomorphic cells. Which of the following best explains the pattern of metastatic spread in this patient and the prognosis it implies?","choices":{"A":"Osteosarcoma has an exceptionally high propensity for early hematogenous pulmonary metastases — even after limb-salvage resection; pulmonary metastasectomy can be curative in selected patients if all lesions are resectable, and is part of standard multimodal osteosarcoma management","B":"Osteosarcoma metastasizes exclusively via lymphangitic spread through the regional lymphatic chains of the popliteal and inguinal nodes before reaching the thoracic duct — the bilateral symmetric distribution in this case confirms lymphangitic rather than hematogenous dissemination","C":"The bilateral pulmonary nodules represent primary multifocal osteosarcoma arising synchronously in the lungs — a recognized phenomenon in Li-Fraumeni syndrome (TP53 germline mutation) that is treated with systemic chemotherapy alone without surgical resection","D":"Pulmonary metastases from osteosarcoma are uniformly fatal within 3 months of diagnosis regardless of treatment — the 'sunburst' periosteal reaction confirms cortical breakthrough, which universally indicates unresectable systemic disease at the time of primary diagnosis","E":"The bilateral pulmonary nodules represent reactive pulmonary ossification triggered by elevated serum alkaline phosphatase from the osteogenic primary tumor — a paraneoplastic response independent of true metastatic spread and without prognostic significance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Osteosarcoma has one of the highest rates of pulmonary metastasis among all sarcomas. The lung is the most common site because sarcomas (arising from systemic veins) enter the systemic venous circulation → right heart → pulmonary arterial circulation → peripheral capillary implantation. The classic teaching: even with successful limb-salvage surgery, osteosarcomas are generally assumed to have occult metastases at diagnosis and spread hematogenously, most often to the lungs (Robbins Ch 26). Critically, pulmonary metastasectomy (surgical resection of lung nodules) IS part of standard curative-intent management for osteosarcoma if all lesions are resectable — this is a Step 1 teaching point distinguishing osteosarcoma from most other cancers (where lung metastases = palliative intent). The bilateral distribution reflects hematogenous (not lymphangitic) spread.","boardPrep":"Osteosarcoma → lung is the #1 metastatic site; bilateral peripheral cannonball nodules in a young patient with bone lesion → high suspicion. Unique feature: pulmonary metastasectomy is part of CURATIVE management for osteosarcoma with lung mets (unlike most cancers). This distinguishes osteosarcoma boards questions. Prognosis: 5-year survival reaches about 70% in patients without overt metastases at diagnosis (Robbins Ch 26). Sunburst periosteal pattern + osteoid-producing cells = osteosarcoma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 2 — Primary lung cancer risk factors (tobacco, asbestos, radon) that generate primary lung tumors which must be distinguished from pulmonary metastases in the clinical workup of bilateral pulmonary nodules; molecular profiling by NGS is the gold standard to distinguish primary vs. metastatic adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The bilateral distribution ref"}}]};
